	Since each non-empty level subset $\mu_t$ is a subgroup of $G$, by Theorem \ref{lev_gp}, $\mu$ is an $L$-subgroup of $G$. We show that $\mu$ is a nilpotent $L$-group. Since $G' = A_4$, it is easy to verify that the commutator $(\mu,\mu)$ is given by
	\[ (\mu,\mu)(x) = \begin{cases}
