\section{Related works}
\subsection{Multi-task Robot Learning}
Extensive research has been conducted on agents capable of learning unified strategies for diverse tasks based on varying instructions. Multi-task learning in robotics has been explored through several methodologies, including network sharing with branching architectures \cite{cross, movements}, goal-conditioned reinforcement learning \cite{lcil}, hierarchical reinforcement learning \cite{hierarchical, discovery} and distilling separate task policies into a shared multi-task policy \cite{actormimic, distral}. Recent works have made significant strides in robots’ complex multi-task operations and generalization to new scenarios by distilling policies from separate task data. These methods commonly involve training large models via imitation learning on extensive multi-task datasets and conditioned on languages or goal images \cite{rt1,rdt}. Nevertheless, previous studies have predominantly focused on the manipulation domain, resulting in limited applications in legged robotics. There still lacks a robust multi-task policy that can handle free-form natural language instructions for task completion, similar to those available in the manipulation domain. Our work builds upon the imitation learning approach using multi-task datasets and focuses specifically on applications in the legged robotics domain.

\subsection{Diffusion Models In Robotics}
Recent advances have led to the growing applications of diffusion models in robotics, incorporating techniques such as imitation learning \cite{diffuseloco,diffusionpolicy,DP3,umi,disco,render,consistency,3d}, reward learning \cite{diffusionreward,extracting}, data generation, online and offline reinforcement learning \cite{dppo}, and motion generation \cite{disney}. Among these approaches, representing policies as conditional diffusion models has achieved significant success in real-world tasks. However, previous works have primarily focused on high-level planning for manipulation tasks, which are characterized by low-dimensional action spaces, low replanning frequencies, and inherently more stable dynamics. While \cite{diffuseloco} has explored in locomotion tasks and increased the control frequency to 30Hz, it still remains below the typical control frequencies required for quadruped robots and continues to exhibit lower robustness compared to expert policies. In this work, we employ a two-stage training approach to enhance robustness and leverage DDIM to achieve a higher inference frequency of 50Hz.

