\section{Experiments}
In this section, we evaluate the performance of DMLoco and conduct ablation experiments to analyze its key components. Building on the framework introduced in previous sections, we aim to address three main questions: a) Can DMLoco learn a language-conditioned multi-task policy and how does it perform compared to non-language-based control strategies?
b) To what extent can finetuning improve the robustness of DMLoco?
c) Which design choices play a critical role in the performance of DMLoco?

Our experiments are conducted in both simulation and real-world environments. Simulations are performed in Isaac Gym on a 4080 laptop, while physical experiments are carried out on a Unitree Aliengo robot equipped with an NVIDIA Jetson Orin NX computing platform. The setup also includes a microphone for speech recognition, utilizing OpenAI's Whisper model for speech-to-text conversion. All the language-conditioned experiments are conducted on the test dataset genereated in \ref{language}.

\subsection{Multi-Task Performance}
We conduct a series of comparative experiments to evaluate the effectiveness of DMLoco, with a particular focus on its language-conditioned control capability. The experiments involve velocity-tracking tasks across four distinct gaits (trotting, bounding, pacing, and pronking) at varying velocity commands. The target velocities are set within the following ranges: \(v_x \in [-1.0, 1.0] \, \text{m/s}\), \(v_y \in [-0.6, 0.6] \, \text{m/s}\) , and \(\omega_z \in [-0.5, 0.5] \, \text{rad/s}\). To provide a comprehensive comparison, we select three state-of-the-art baselines: IBC \cite{ibc}, BeT \cite{bet}, and DiffuseLoco \cite{diffuseloco}. These baselines represent prominent approaches in multi-task learning but lack language conditioning, making them ideal for evaluating performance of DMLoco and assessing the added value of DMLoco's language control mechanism.
Details of these baseline algorithms are described below. 
\begin{itemize}
    \item IBC (Implicit Behavioral Cloning): The IBC algorithm uses implicit energy-based models to approximate complex, multi-modal functions directly from demonstrations.
    \item BeT (Behavior Transformer): BeT leverages transformer architectures to model multi-modal demonstration data by combining action discretization with a multi-task action correction inspired by offset prediction. 
    \item DiffuseLoco: A diffusion-based multi-task policy for locomotion. In our experiment, we use Unet1d architectures to keep consistency with DMLoco, and it shares the same parameter count of 5.2M.
\end{itemize}
