\subsection{Robust Transition between Tasks}
To further demonstrate the improvement in robustness brought by finetuning, we evaluate the success rate of gait transitions at three different speeds: low (0 m/s), medium (0.5 m/s), and high (1 m/s). In the simulation, each experiment is conducted 100 times, with the target gait randomly selected to differ from the current gait during each transition. For real-world experiments, we test four types of gait transitions: trotting to bounding, bounding to pacing, pacing to pronking, and pronking to trotting. Each transition type is repeated 5 times, resulting in 20 trials in total. The stability, calculated as the number of stable trajectories without falling divided by the total number of trials, is presented in Table \ref{gait}.

Experimental results show that the finetuned policy significantly improves stability across all speed ranges. Compared to the pretrained policy, the finetuned policy improves success rates in simulation by 15\% (low speed), 23\% (medium speed), and 28\% (high speed), and by 45\% (low speed), 70\% (medium speed), and 65\% (high speed) in real-world experiments. These results highlight that finetuning substantially enhances DMLoco's performance during gait transitions, with notable improvements observed during high-speed motion. Moreover, the finetuned DMLoco demonstrates a significantly smaller sim-to-real gap, likely due to the interaction with the environment during finetuning, which exposes the model to a broader range of out-of-distribution (OOD) states and improves its generalization capabilities.

