\subsection{Ablation Studies}
To further validate the effectiveness of various design choices in the DMLoco framework, we conduct four ablation experiments. These experiments aimed to explore the impact of different components on model performance and provide guidance for future improvements.

We first evaluate the impact of different pretrained language models on the performance of DMLoco. Success rate in Table \ref{language} is defined as the robot's ability to maintain stability and correctly execute language instructions. The experimental results show that using pretrained language models yield similar performance, indicating that DMLoco is robust to the choice of pretrained models. Among these models, BERT achieves the best performance with a loss of 0.079 and a success rate of 0.97. However, all-MiniLM-L6-v2, with only 23M parameters, achieves comparable performance, making it a more efficient choice for deployment in resource-constrained scenarios. In contrast, training a model from scratch leads to a significant performance drop. The from-scratch model uses randomly initialized word embeddings and mean pooling for language embedding, but its lack of pretrained semantic knowledge results in poor generalization. 
This highlights the importance of pretrained models in understanding language instructions, especially in multi-task learning.

