\section{Numerical simulation of MBL initialisation for solving optimization tasks}\label{appx:numerical-simulation}

To demonstrate the capability of the proposed MBL initialisation scheme, we apply it to solve two optimization tasks: the Variational Quantum Eigensolver (VQE) and the Max-Cut problem by optimizing the on-site longitudinal and transversed field, and the evolution time for each quench.


\subsection{Variational Quantum Eigensolver (VQE)}
The objective of VQEs is to estimate the ground state energy of a given Hamiltonian, which can be associated with the most stable molecule energy or the solution to a problem of interest. Here, we employ 100 instances of our ans\"{a}tze--a parameterized quench dynamics initialised in the MBL phase--to solve VQE problems in two examples. We select the number of quenches in ans\"{a}tze to be either 2 or 6, as these different numbers of quenches lead to noticeable differences in expressivity and the variance of the loss function.


\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Example 1:} We use an ans\"{a}tze governed by the Hamiltonian in Eq.~\eqref{eq:IsingModel} to find the ground state of a 7-qubit long-range Ising model Hamiltonian of the form in Eq.~\eqref{eq:expising} within the thermalized phase. After 100 optimization epochs: 
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{2-quench ans\"{a}tze:} Average relative error is $0.68\%$ with a standard deviation of $1.1\%$.
    \item \textbf{6-quench ans\"{a}tze:} Average relative error is $0.48\%$ with a standard deviation of $0.57\%$.
\end{itemize}

\noindent\textbf{Example 2:} We use the ans\"{a}tze from the Hamiltonian in Eq.~\eqref{eq:expising} to solve a 7-qubit nearest-neighbour Ising Hamiltonian of the form in Eq.~\eqref{eq:IsingModel} within the thermalized phase. After 100 optimization epochs:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{2-quench ans\"{a}tze:} Average relative error is $3.1\%$ with a standard deviation of $2.6\%$.
    \item \textbf{6-quench ans\"{a}tze:} Average relative error is $0.89\%$ with a standard deviation of $0.51\%$.
\end{itemize}

The optimization processes of both examples are shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:VQE}. In both cases, increasing the number of quenches from 2 to 6 improves the accuracy of the estimated ground state energy. This suggests that increasing the number of quenches can enhance optimization performance to some extent. 

