\begin{abstract}
This paper presents a two-timescale hierarchical decentralized architecture for control of Cyber-Physical Systems. The architecture consists of $N$ independent sub-processes, a global controller, and $N$ local controllers, each formulated as a Markov Decision Process (MDP). The global controller, operating at a slower timescale optimizes the infinite-horizon discounted cumulative reward under budget constraints. For the local controllers, operating at a faster timescale, we propose two different optimization frameworks, namely the COpt and FOpt. In the COpt framework, the local controller also optimizes an infinite-horizon MDP, while in the FOpt framework, the local controller optimizes a finite-horizon MDP. The FOpt framework mimics a federal structure, where the local controllers have more autonomy in their decision making. First, the existence of stationary deterministic optimal policies for both these frameworks is established. Then, various relationships between the two frameworks are studied, including a bound on the difference between the two optimal value functions. Additionally, sufficiency conditions are provided such that the two frameworks lead to the same optimal values.  
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Cyber-physical systems (CPSs)  are large scale engineering systems consisting various computing, communication, and control units. They aim to achieve system performance objectives through the control of complex subsystems, and have applications in energy, healthcare, industrial automation, disaster management, defense, etc. \cite{Kim2012:CPSperspective,Rajkumar2010:CPSrevolution}.


Complex cyber-physical systems may involve layers of decision making comprising of ``global'' and ``local'' controllers. These layers represent different aspects of the overall objective. For example, the local controllers manage individual sub-processes, while the global controller oversees the entire system, making control decisions based on the collective state of all sub-processes. Further, controllers at these two layers operate on different timescales. For example, the global controller might not track the intermediate local state evolution, but only the total output or the state of a sub-process at the beginning of a decision interval (for resource allocation). Whereas, a local controller tracks the intermediate state evolution as it has to directly provide the control/actuation signal to the sub-process. 
%For example, the global controller's objective is ``macro-optimization'' and local controllers are tasked with ``micro-optimization''. 
We consider such a layered multi-timescale CPS with an overall objective to control a complex process consisting of independent sub-processes. Here, the global controller which is the main decision-maker can simultaneously
monitor or engage only a subset of the local controllers due to resource constraints, such as limited communication channels or occasional unavailability of local controllers. The layered CPSs can also be called nested or hierarchical control systems where both local and global controllers must coordinate effectively to achieve an overall objective.

In this paper, we consider a two-timescale (two-layered) CPS with stochastic controllers at both layers, and the state evolution is Markovian. The local controllers are weakly coupled through the resource allocation/scheduling decisions of the global controller. The  objective is to find the optimal sequence of decisions at both layers such that the system is steered along a path of maximum long-term reward.  In the following we present literature on sequential decision problems with focus on MDP models which are relevant to our work.

