\begin{abstract}
 Multi-agent systems empowered by large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated remarkable capabilities in a wide range of downstream applications, including machine translation. However, the potential of LLMs in translating Hong Kong legal judgments remains uncertain due to challenges such as intricate legal terminology, culturally embedded nuances, and strict linguistic structures. In this work, we introduce TransLaw, a novel multi-agent framework implemented for real-world Hong Kong case law translation. It employs three specialized agents, namely, Translator, Annotator, and Proofreader, to collaboratively produce translations for high accuracy in legal meaning, appropriateness in style, and adequate coherence and cohesion in structure. This framework supports customizable LLM configurations and achieves tremendous cost reduction compared to professional human translation services. We evaluated its performance using 13 open-source and commercial LLMs as agents and obtained interesting findings, 
including that it surpasses GPT-4o in legal semantic accuracy, structural coherence, and stylistic fidelity, yet trails human experts in contextualizing complex terminology and stylistic naturalness. Our platform website is available at CityUHK,\footnote{\url{https://hmit.LT.cityu.edu.hk} (On Campus)} and our bilingual judgment corpus used for the evaluation is available at Hugging Face. \footnote{\url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/xxuan-nlp/CFA_Judgement_Corpus_97-22}}
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\begin{figure}[t]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.49\textwidth]{Fig/11.png}
    \caption{Compared to conventional machine translation (MT) systems that utilize a single MT engine,
\textsc{TransLaw} leverages the collaboration among multiple language agents, each powered by large language
models (LLMs), for translation.}
    \label{fig:1}
\end{figure}


This year marks the 28th anniversary of Hong Kong's return to China after 155 years of British rule. On 1 July 1997, when the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region (HKSAR) was established, it took pride in having all its written statutes translated into Chinese, a mammoth task once considered impossible by many. This achievement was mandated by of the Basic Law, the former stipulating retention of the English common law system while the latter establishing legal bilingualism with Chinese and English as official languages, of equal status. \footnote{\url{https://www.doj.gov.hk/en/about/orgchart_ldd_drafting_chi_eng.html}}

Against this constitutional backdrop, the translation of Hong Kong judicial judgments as case laws constitutes a pivotal component in sustaining the territory's bilingual legal framework \cite{Cheng2016}. Since the handover, Hong Kong has confronted persistent challenges in reconciling linguistic transformation with inherited legal infrastructure \cite{Chen2002}. The foundations trace to the 1987 Bilingual Laws Project which systematized statute translation \cite{Jones1987} and institutionalized parallel legislative drafting \cite{Mushkat1997}. Nevertheless, judicial practice reveals that English remained the predominant courtroom language in higher courts throughout the post-colonial transitional period \cite{daniels2011legacy}, yet through progressive legal localization \cite{tam2012legal}, judgment translation has evolved into an essential mechanism ensuring jurisprudential precision \cite{PrietoRamos2014} and facilitating cross-jurisdictional legal communication \cite{Lin2023}. Confronted with the voluminous corpus of common law documentation within HK's judicial system \cite{Hau2019}, the establishment of efficient, accurate, and large-scale translation processes for laws assumes critical significance in Hong Kong \cite{xuan2024efficient, Sin2025}.



% Consequently, to address the aforementioned challenges and inspired by multi-agent systems \cite{durante2024agent, tao2025magis, yu2025fincon, he2025llm} and real-world case law translation workflows. First, we propose \textsc{TransLaw}(as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:1}), the first LLM-powered multi-agent system simulating professional legal translation studios, 
% we propose \textsc{TransLaw} (as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:1}). Similar to human translation studios, \textsc{TransLaw} functions as a virtual multi-agent translation system. It mitigates challenges in generating high-quality translations through process decomposition and collaborative specialization. Specifically, each agent in \textsc{TransLaw} manages discrete translation phases, aimed at producing translations comparable to human translators in accuracy and naturalness. Each of our agents plays a specialized role, including Translator, Annotator, and Proofreader. Together, these agents replicate the traditional human translation judgment process, delivering translations that are accurate in Legal Meaning, Appropriateness in Style, and of Coherence and Cohesion in Structure. 

% 进一步，We establish the first Bilingual Judgment Corpus (BJC) Benchmark for legal translation, enabling comprehensive evaluation of LLMs 使用自动评估以及人工评估三个维度
%  across three critical dimensions: Legal Meaning Accuracy, Stylistic Appropriateness, and Structural Coherence & Cohesion;

To address the aforementioned challenge of judgement translation, we follow the inspiration from multi-agent systems~\cite{durante2024agent,tao2025magis,yu2025fincon,he2025llm} and the real-world case law translation workflow to formulate and implement \textbf{the first LLM-powered multi-agent system called \textit{TransLaw}} as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:1} for simulating a professional legal translation studio. It mitigates challenges in generating high-quality translations through process decomposition and collaborative specialization using task-oriented agents. Specifically, each agent handles a unique task for a translation phase, aimed at producing translation comparable to human translators in terms of translation quality criteria like accuracy and naturalness. There are three agents in our system to play specialized roles, namely, Translator, Annotator and Proofreader. They work together to replicate the human translation process, in hopes of delivering judgment translations that can achieve high accuracy of legal meaning, coherence and cohesion in structure, and adequate appropriateness in style. 

For its evaluation, \textbf{we have established the Bilingual Judgment Corpus (BJC) as benchmark dataset}, the first in its kind for use to enable legal translation evaluation and comprehensive LLM assessment with the id of suitable automated metrics and necessary human evaluation. 

Finally, \textbf{we deploy a production-ready MT platform for legal professionals}, is featured by the following characteristics (1) \textbf{Flexible:} It allows legal practitioners to configure their translation aids according to their specific needs, such as the number of employees for each role, source/target languages, and the selection of LLMs for agents. (2) \textbf{Universal:} Human evaluation results demonstrate that our system consistently achieves superior performance over commercial LLMs such as GPT-4o, across all three key dimensions of legal translation quality, namely, legal meaning accuracy, stylistic appropriateness, and structural coherence \& cohesion.
(3) \textbf{User-Friendly:} We design an intuitive interface to enhance user experience. This streamlined interface is intended to facilitate navigation while allowing users to focus on the human proofreading process and access system functionalities effortlessly.
(4) \textbf{Cost-Effective:} The cost of translating HK legal judgments using \textsc{TransLaw} is verified nearly 4,000 times cheaper than professional translation services, as quantified in Section~\ref{sec:cost_analysis}.


