\section{Evaluation}


In this section, we report both automated and human evaluation of our \textsc{TransLaw}.


\subsection{Automated evaluation}

Automated evaluation of an MT system is conducted by applying available authentic automated metrics to compute quality scores for its translation output by contrasting the output with the gold standard answers in a given bilingual text dataset.

\paragraph{Metrics}
    The metrics that we adopted for our evaluation are the following two that have been the most popular in recent years for automated MT evaluation: (1) xCOMET-XL, a version of xCOMET, which is a state-of-the-art learned metric for various levels of evaluation \cite{guerreiro2024xcomet}; 
    (2) Unified MT quality evaluation model wmt22-unite-da, a unified MT quality evaluation model \cite{guttmann2024comet}.

% \newcounter{partnumber} % 定义计数器
% \setcounter{partnumber}{1} % 将计数器设置为 1

\paragraph{Test Set}

The CFA Judgement Corpus 97-22 was constructed by collecting 344 bilingual Chinese-English judicial decisions from the HK Court of Final Appeal (1997–2022), publicly released on Hugging Face\footnote{\url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/xxuan-nlp/CFA_Judgement_Corpus_97-22}}. The corpus totals 28,819.61 KB, comprising 21,766,836 characters. 

\paragraph{Evaluated LLM Models}
We evaluate 13 widely adopted large language models (LLMs), classified into two categories: General LLMs and Legal-specific LLMs. For General LLMs, the testbed encompasses GPT-4o \cite{openai_gpt4o}, GPT-4 \cite{openai_gpt4_turbo}, ChatGPT \cite{openai_gpt35_turbo}, the DeepSeek series (DeepSeek-R1 \cite{guo2025deepseekr1}, DeepSeek-V3 \cite{liu2024deepseekv3}) , the ChatGLM series (ChatGLM2-6B, ChatGLM3-6B) \cite{zeng2023glm}, the Baichuan series (Baichuan-13B-base, Baichuan-13B-Chat) \cite{yang2023baichuan}, and the Qwen series (Qwen-7B-chat, Qwen14B-Chat) \cite{bai2023qwen}; for Legal-specific LLMs, we include ChatLaw-13B and ChatLaw-33B \cite{cui2023chatlaw}. Detailed model specifications are provided in Appendix C.





\paragraph{Evaluation Results and Analysis}


   We report zero-shot performance scores for all models in Table~1 (columns 2--4). Our analysis reveals:

\begin{itemize}
   \item The open-source LLMs perform slightly worse compared to the closed-source (commercial) LLMs GPT-4o and GPT-4, which achieve  the best performance in the benchmark. However, due to insufficient knowledge of Hong Kong's legal system, both GPT-4o and GPT-4 exhibit substantial limitations in legal judgment translation tasks. This indicates significant potential for improvement in legal-domain LLM capabilities.

   \item Increased LLM model scale consistently enhances performance within the legal domain. For instance, \texttt{Qwen-14B} outperforms \texttt{Qwen-7B}. Furthermore, chat-optimized LLMs (\textit{e.g.}, \texttt{Baichuan-13B-Chat}) surpass their base models (\textit{e.g.}, \texttt{Baichuan-13B-base}), suggesting that instruction-following capabilities gained through supervised fine-tuning and alignment optimization substantially unlock LLMs' potential.

   \item Surprisingly, Legal-specific LLMs do not always perform better than General LLMs. We speculate
that there are two possible reasons. First, the capability of these Legal-specific LLMs could be
limited by their base models, which are usually not as strong as other LLMs such as GPT-4o and GPT-4; Moreover, the continuous pre-training on the legal corpus may affect the abilities of the original
base models. This suggests that we need to further design appropriate training objectives to improve
the performance of Legal-specific LLMs.


\end{itemize}

We report few-shot performance scores for all models in Table 1 (columns 5–7). Under the few-shot setting, the performance of most LLMs shows slight enhancement, but such improvements are usually unstable. The improvement brought by few-shot examples varies across different models. Some models (e.g. GPT-4o and GPT-4) experience performance improvements, while others (e.g. Qwen-14B-Chat) may suffer degradation. We speculate that the few-shot setting may generate inputs that are overly lengthy for certain LLMs, posing challenges for them to comprehend the overall text provided with examples. Also, it indicates that in-context learning may not be an ideal way to inject legal knowledge into certain LLMs.

Overall, at present, LLMs can to some extent assist in effectively solving legal translation tasks under the Hong Kong bilingual legal system, but still require human review and proofreading. Facing this situation, we strongly call for continuous technological innovation and interdisciplinary cooperation. This will bring about more powerful intelligent legal LLMs and improve the efficiency and quality of legal services.


