\section{Limitations and Future work}


\paragraph{LLM's multi-turn dialogues exhibit hallucination}
When setting multiple rounds (3
rounds, 5 rounds) of dialogue between the Annotator LLM and the Proofreader LLM for repeated
revisions, we found that the meaning of the translation often deviates from the original text after
multiple revisions (hallucination phenomenon). The preliminary solution we propose, referencing
\cite{wu2024beyond}, is to add an extra hallucination arbitrator LLM. This part of
the work will be addressed in a subsequent paper.

\section{Conclusion}
In this paper, we have introduced TransLaw, a novel collaborative interactive multi-agent translation framework designed for Hong Kong judgment translation, which comprises three agents employed to perform the three typical subtasks, namely, translation, annotation of translation errors, and proofreading for error correction, in the traditional translation process. For its evaluation, we have constructed the large-scale bilingual corpus BJC Judgments as a benchmark dataset to evaluate 13 open-source and commercial LLMs used as agents in this framework. Experimental results have been obtained to verify the collaborative strategies. Interesting validity of this collaborative multi-agent approach is demonstrated, in addition to providing an informative performance comparison of the LLMs. Our error typology analysis has also identified more critical translation challenges that are yet to be resolved. Our future work will focus on addressing these challenges, in addition to developing more comprehensive and reliable benchmarks for cost-effective evaluation, as well as further optimizing the agent architecture to enhance performance.



