\subsection*{Appendix B: More Cases}

 In this section, we present additional case studies on how professional translators annotate and proofread judgment document paragraphs using Proofread Codes in
Table 4. We also include commentary from a translator\footnote{The second author.} with 30 years of experience in Hong Kong judgment translation on the output translations of \textit{TransLaw-ChatGPT (Zero-shot)}, \textit{TransLaw-ChatGPT (Few-shot)} and \textit{Reference}.


Based on the Marking Codes provided and the comparison of the three translations, here is a detailed evaluation of each version in terms of \emph{diction}, \emph{sentence structure}, \emph{style}, and \emph{fidelity} to the original, using the codes to identify translation issues. 

\paragraph{Overall Comments}


The main flaw of this translation is that it violates the information structure of the first sentence. In the sentence “the increasingly notable national security risks in the HKSAR (subject) have become a prominent problem (predicate),” the new information (message) is carried by the predicate. However, in the Chinese translation, “a prominent problem” (subject) is rendered as given information while “is the increasingly noticeable national security risks in HKSAR” becomes the new information. Neither TLC-Z nor TLC-F makes such a mistake.
\begin{CJK*}{UTF8}{bsmi}
The second sentence “2019年香港發生“修例風波”以來” is ill-structured. “onset” is omitted in the translation. The very first adverbial “當前” is seldom used as a stand-alone adverbial. “目前” in TLC-Z and TLC-F is a better choice. “主張” as a translation of “notions” is not accurate. It should be expressed either as “觀念” or “思想”. “還要看到” as a translation of “Moreover” is awkward and inaccurate. “一些” (non-specific) is an inaccurate translation of “certain” (specific but not specified). “某些” is a better choice. “進行插手” is inappropriate collocation. “發展利益” should follow a connective (連詞) as in TLC-Z and TLC-F. First, it avoids the use of the personal plural pronoun “他們” to refer to the “forces”. While “they” in English is perfectly fine to refer to “forces”, “他們” for “勢力” may raise a frown. Second, “沆瀣一氣” is a perfect translation of “forces of the same ilk”, which both TLC-Z and TLC-F omitted to translate. Third, the flow of language is fluent.



\paragraph{Comments on Reference Text}

This version is the most idiomatic, stylistically consistent, and politically aligned with standard Chinese official discourse. It reads like a professionally edited government document, demonstrating strengths across multiple dimensions. \textit{Diction (ID, CL, SL)} makes effective use of idiomatic and formulaic expressions such as \emph{「反中亂港勢力」}, \emph{「撐腰打氣」}, and \emph{「插手和搗亂」}, all of which are well-established in Chinese state discourse and contribute to the text’s natural tone. In terms of \textit{Sentence structure (ST, TS)}, the writing is varied, rhythmic, and coherent, with long sentences logically segmented using semicolons and commas for clarity and flow. The \textit{Tone and style (SL, CN)} appropriately matches the authoritative register expected in high-level official statements, maintaining a balanced use of formal political rhetoric throughout. Regarding \textit{Translation accuracy (MT, CW, NA)}, the version shows strong fidelity to the source material, with key political concepts rendered clearly and effectively. Additionally, \textit{Idiomaticity (ID)} is consistently high, with phrases like \emph{「蓄意破壞」}, \emph{「暴力對抗」}, and \emph{「撐腰打氣」} sounding particularly natural in context. There are, however, a few minor issues: some translations, such as \emph{「沆瀣一氣」}, carry a stronger pejorative tone than their source equivalents (e.g., “of the same ilk”), though this amplification aligns with the rhetorical expectations of the target discourse and should not be considered a mistranslation.



\paragraph{Comments on TLC-F}

TLC-F benefits from 5-shot in-context learning and shows clear improvement over TLC-Z in terms of fluency, cohesion, and alignment with formal writing norms. The text demonstrates several strengths: \textit{Fluency and cohesion (TS)} are notably improved, with more natural sentence flow and effective restructuring in places such as “使政府治理和立法機構運作陷於癱瘓”; \textit{Style and vocabulary (SL, CL)} are mostly appropriate, with lexical choices like “顯著問題”, “透過非政府組織的各種方式”, and “從事危害國家安全的活動” sounding well-formed and contextually suitable; \textit{Information structure (IF)} is largely preserved, maintaining the logical progression of ideas found in the original. However, there are also a number of limitations: \textit{CW} reveals that certain expressions, such as “反華勢力”, are less idiomatic than alternatives like “反中勢力”, with “反華” being more typical of public opinion discourse rather than formal governmental language; \textit{ID} occasionally results in slightly awkward phrasing, such as “破壞者”, which, while grammatically correct, lacks the rhetorical sharpness of more conventional terms like “破壞分子” or “亂港分子”; \textit{SL} exhibits constructions like “國家安全風險已成為一個顯著問題” that, though syntactically sound, fall short of the more idiomatic and rhetorically polished “日益凸顯的國家安全風險已成為一個突出問題”; finally, in terms of \textit{TS}, a few segments appear mechanically structured and lack the rhythmic layering and deliberate rhetorical design characteristic of the Reference Text.



\paragraph{Comments on TLC-Z}


This version reflects 0-shot output without contextual learning. It is grammatically sound but lacks stylistic maturity, and several expressions feel awkward, mechanical, or unidiomatic. \emph{Accuracy (MT, NA)} is relatively acceptable, as the translation retains all major content points from the source, and \emph{Grammar} is mostly correct when considered in isolation. However, a number of issues affect its overall quality. \emph{ID/SL} includes several awkward phrasings, such as “煽動香港人反對中國和中國共產黨”, which misses the more idiomatic and formulaic “反中反共”; similarly, “在港機構” sounds unnatural and could be improved to “中央駐港機構”. From a \emph{Collocation (CL)} perspective, expressions like “進行干擾和創造混亂” are problematic — “創造混亂” is an awkward collocation, whereas “製造混亂” would be far more idiomatic. In terms of \emph{Textual structure / Sentence structure (TS / ST)}, the flow between clauses appears robotic and lacks the deliberate rhetorical rhythm seen in more polished versions. Stylistically, \emph{Conventionalization (CN)} also reveals weaknesses: while “反中勢力” is acceptable, the phrase “反中香港破壞分子” feels cumbersome compared to the tighter and more standard “反中亂港分子”. Finally, there is some \emph{Redundancy (RN)} in the text — for instance, “政府治理和立法機構的運作” is over-specified and less fluid than the more concise and natural “政府管治和立法會運作”.
