\section{Conclusion}
In this paper, we present \emph{\technique}, a novel interaction technique that encourages programmers to directly influence LLM decision-making during code generation.
Our implementation, \sys, highlights critical decision points and provides contextual explanations for alternative implementations at each point.
Through a security-focused user study with 18 programmers, we found that participants had more \emph{intentional} interactions with \sys compared to a baseline, ultimately adopting significantly safer code practices.
Additionally, \sys guided participants to achieve their programming goals---regardless of their initial intent---as they discovered diverse alternatives, interpreted how each aligned with their goals, and selected the best option to effectively steer code generation.
Our findings offer valuable insights for designing LLM-driven programming interfaces that encourage more intentional, interactive code generation with end-users.

