Case (d): When $|x_{n}|=|x_{n-1}|=4$, so $\phi{(4)}+\phi{(4)}=n+3\implies n=1$, thus $G\cong \mathbb{Z}_2$, which is not a solution. And finally when $|x_{n-2}|=4$ and $|x_{n-1}|=8$, then $\langle x_{n-2}\rangle \leq \langle x_{n-1}\rangle$ thus $G\cong \mathbb{Z}_{10}$, which doesn't have cyclic subgroup of order $4$ and $8$. 
