\begin{abstract}% prevent space after \enspace
A matrix is said to be blocky if, modulo permutation of rows and columns, it can be obtained from  an identity matrix by successively either duplicating a row or column or adding a zero row or column. Blocky matrices are precisely the boolean matrices that are contractive when considered as Schur multipliers. It is conjectured that any boolean matrix with Schur multiplier norm at most $\gamma$ is expressible as
\begin{equation*}A = \sum_{i=1}^L \sigma_i B_i\end{equation*}
for some signs $\sigma_i$ and blocky matrices $B_i$, where $L$ depends only on $\gamma$. This conjecture is an analogue of Green and Sanders's quantitative version of Cohen's idempotent theorem. 
In this paper, we prove bounds on $L$ that are polylogarithmic in the dimension of $A$. Concretely, if $A$ is an $n\times n$ matrix, we show that one may take $L = 2^{O(\gamma^7)} \log(n)^2$. 
\vskip5pt
\noindent\textbf{Keywords.}\enspace Block complexity, Schur multipliers, factorization norm.
\vskip5pt
\noindent\textbf{MSC2020 Classification.}\enspace 15B36, 47L80, 94D10.
\end{abstract}

