\section{Introduction} 

A celebrated classical result in harmonic analysis is the 1960 theorem of P.~J.~Cohen~\cite{cohen1960}, which states that a measure $\mu$ on a locally compact abelian group $G$ is idempotent if and only if $\hat\mu$ is a member of the coset ring of $\hat G$. This was conjectured by W.\ Rudin \cite{rudin1959}, who settled the case of discrete groups and finite-dimensional torus groups. An explicit description of idempotent measures on the circle group was given earlier by H.\ Helson \cite{helson1954}. Cohen's idempotent theorem is sometimes referred to as the Cohen--Host idempotent theorem, as in 1986, B.~Host extended Cohen's result to arbitrary locally compact groups~\cite{host1986}.

For finite groups, Cohen's theorem holds without offering any content, since in this case, the coset ring consists of all functions on $\hat G$. However, a 2008 theorem of B.~Green and T.~Sanders~\cite{greensandersannals} gives a quantitative version of the idempotent theorem that is useful even for finite groups.
Subsequent refinements by Sanders~\cite{sanders2011, sanders2020} give better bounds and show that the group need not even be abelian for this result to hold, be for the sake of simplicity, in the brief exposition below, we shall take $G$ to be abelian and finite.

Let $\norm{f}_A$ denote the Fourier algebra norm $\sum_{a\in \hat G} \bigl|\hat f(a)\bigr|$. A classical result of Kawada and It\^o~\cite{kawadaito} states that a nonzero boolean function $f:G \to \{0,1\}$ satisfies $\norm{f}_A \le 1$ if and only if $f=\one_{s+H}$ for a coset $s+H$ of $G$.
The quantitative idempotent theorem states that every boolean function $f:G \to \{0,1\}$ with $\norm{f}_A \le \gamma$ can be expressed as a signed sum $f = \sum_{i=1}^L \pm \one_{s_i + H_i}$ for some $L \le \exp(\gamma^{3+o(1)})$ and some cosets $s_1 + H_1,\ldots, s_L + H_L$ of $G$. The term ``idempotent'' refers to the fact that boolean functions are precisely those that satisfy $f^2=f$, and therefore, Green and Sanders' theorem characterizes the idempotents that have small Fourier algebra norm.

\paragraph{The factorization norm.}\hskip-0.3em% Eyeballing this measurement because I don't know how to modify \paragraph. I want the space after the dot to be the same as in the theorem environment (an \enspace I believe).
The \emph{$\gamma_2$ factorization norm} (or the \emph{$\gamma_2$ norm} for short) is defined to be
\begin{equation}\normgamma A = \min_{UV = A} \normrow U \normcol V,\end{equation}
where the minimum is taken over all factorizations $UV$ of $A$,
$\normrow U$ is the largest $\ell_2$-norm of a row in $U$ and
$\normcol V$ is the largest $\ell_2$-norm of a column in $V$.
Note that the $\gamma_2$ norm cannot decrease by restricting to a submatrix, since removing rows of $U$ or columns of $V$ cannot increase $\normrow U$ or $\normcol V$. In particular, $\normgamma A$ is at least $\normmax A = \max_{(x,y)\in X\times Y} \bigl|A(x,y)\bigr|$.

The $\gamma_2$ norm is related to the Green--Sanders theorem, because if one defines $A :  G\times G \to \{0,1\}$ by $A(x,y) = f(x-y)$ and regards it as a boolean matrix, then it can be shown (see, e.g., \cite[Corollary~3.13]{hhh2023}) that
\begin{equation}\normgamma A = \norm{f}_A.\label{eqgammaspectral}\end{equation}
Hence, the matrix $A$ can be written as a signed sum of matrices corresponding to indicators of cosets of $G$.
Let $g+H$ be such a coset and define $B(x,y) = \one_{g+H}(x-y)$. Since $x-y\in g+H$ if and only if $x-y = g$ in $G/H$, one sees that the support of $B$ is exactly $\bigcup_{s\in G/H} (s+H)\times (s-g+H)$.

\paragraph{Blocky matrices and Schur multipliers.}\hskip-0.3em%
The following definition captures the block structure immanent in the support of the matrix $B$ constructed above. We call an
$m\times n$ matrix $B$ \emph{blocky} if there exist disjoint sets $S_1,\ldots, S_k\subseteq [m]$
and disjoint sets $T_1,\ldots,T_k\subseteq [n]$ such that the support of $B$ is exactly $\bigcup_{i=1}^k S_i\times T_i$.
The simplest examples of blocky matrices are zero matrices, all-$1$s matrices, and identity matrices.

Let $A \circ B$ denote the entry-wise product of two matrices $A$ and $B$ of the same dimensions. This is often called the \emph{Schur product} or \emph{Hadamard product}.
Let $X$ and $Y$ be countable sets and let $B(Y,X)$ denote the space of bounded linear operators from $\ell_2(Y)$ to $\ell_2(X)$, with the operator norm
\begin{equation}\norm{A} = \sup_{y\in \ell_2(Y)} \frac{\norm{Ay}_2}{\norm{y}_2}.\end{equation}
A matrix $M : X\times Y\to \RR$ is called a \emph{Schur multiplier} if $\norm{M\circ A} < \infty$ for every matrix $A : X\times Y\to \RR$ with $\norm A < \infty$. In other words, a matrix $M$ is a Schur multiplier if its \emph{Schur multiplier norm}, defined by
\begin{equation} \norm{M}_m = \sup_{A\in B(Y,X)} \frac{\norm{M\circ A}}{\norm A},
\end{equation}
is finite. Since
\begin{equation}\norm{A\circ B}_m \le \norm{A}_m \cdot \norm{B}_m,\label{eqgammaalgebra}
\end{equation}
the set of Schur multipliers is closed under addition and Schur product,
and thus forms a Banach algebra. The algebra of Schur multipliers is germane to our study of the factorization norm, because it turns out that $\norm{M}_m = \normgamma M$ for every matrix $M$; this result is due to A.~Grothendieck~\cite{grothendieck} (see also~\cite{pis12} for a readable proof in a more modern context).

An element $a$ of an algebra is said to be \emph{idempotent} if $a^2 = a$. It is natural to wonder what the idempotent elements in the algebra of Schur multipliers are. Any matrix satisfying $M\circ M = M$ must be boolean, but not all infinite boolean matrices have finite $\gamma_2$ norm. For example, one can place a sequence of finite matrices with increasing $\gamma_2$ norms along the diagonal.

Recall that an operator is said to be \emph{contractive} if its operator norm is at most $1$. As a starting point, we observe that blocky matrices are exactly the contractive idempotents in the algebra of Schur multipliers.

\begin{proposition}[{\rm\cite{liv95}}] \label{propcontractive}
If $A$ satisfies $\normgamma A\le 1$, then $A$ is a
blocky matrix. In particular, either $A = 0$ and $\normgamma A = 0$,
or $A$ is a nonzero blocky matrix and $\normgamma A = 1$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
One way to see that blocky matrices have $\gamma_2$ norm equal to $1$ is to note that this norm remains the same under duplication or permutation of rows or columns, as well as addition of zero rows or columns. Any blocky matrix is obtained from an identity matrix by some sequence of these operations.
To prove the converse, we must show that a boolean matrix with $\normgamma A \le 1$ does not contain a $2\times 2$ submatrix with exactly three $1$-entries. A direct calculation, due to L.~Livshits~\cite{liv95},
shows that $\normgamma{\matr1011} = 2/\sqrt 3 > 1$,
and the $\gamma_2$ norm does not decrease upon restriction to a submatrix.
\end{proof}

Consider the algebra of functions $f: G\to \CC$ under addition and pointwise multiplication. This is a Banach algebra under the norm $\norm{f}_A$, since $\norm{fg}_A \le \norm{f}_A\cdot \norm{g}_A$. The idempotent elements of this algebra are precisely the boolean functions $f: G\to \{0,1\}$, and
\Cref{propcontractive} is the matrix analogue of Kawada and It\^o's theorem that a function is idempotent and contractive if and only if it is the indicator of a coset.

In the same way that Cohen's idempotent theorem shows that any idempotent element of the Fourier algebra can be written as a finite sum of contractive idempotents, it is an open problem, first stated (to our knowledge) in~\cite[Section 2]{kp05}, whether any idempotent Schur multiplier can be written as a finite sum of contractive idempotents in the algebra of Schur multipliers (see also~\cite[Question 3.13]{elt16}). A compactness argument, given as \cite[Theorem~3.10]{hhh2023}, shows that a positive answer to this open problem, while only being meaningful for infinite matrices, is equivalent to the following conjecture for finite matrices.

