%   In the first inductive step we consider $e=e_1+e_2$, in which case the result follows immediately from the induction hypothesis.

%   Consider $e=e_1\cdot e_2$. We have $(\langle h, s\rangle, \langle h'',s''\rangle )\in\Sem{e_1\cdot e_2}$ if and only if $(\langle h, s\rangle, \langle h',s'\rangle )\in\Sem{e_1}$ and $(\langle h', s'\rangle, \langle h'',s''\rangle )\in\Sem{e_2}$. Using the induction hypothesis we obtain that $(\langle h, s\rangle, \langle h',s'\rangle )\in [L(e_1)]$ and $(\langle h', s'\rangle, \langle h'',s''\rangle )\in L(e_2)$, which holds if and only if $(\langle h, s\rangle, \langle h',s'\rangle )\in \Sem{x}$ and $(\langle h', s'\rangle, \langle h'',s''\rangle )\in \Sem{y}$ such that $x\in L(e_1)$ and $y\in L(e_2)$. Hence, $xy\in L(e_1\cdot e_2)$, and it follows immediately that $(\langle h, s\rangle, \langle h'',s''\rangle )\in\Sem{xy}$.

