Considering the $\nu_e$-electron scattering cross section (approximately $9.4\times10^{-45}~\mr{cm}^2$ for $E_{\nu_e} = 10~\mr{MeV}$), we can estimate the expected event count rate for detecting $\nu_e$ signals from $\nu_H$ decay, as shown in figure~\ref{fig:nueSignalCounts}. In most of the phase space where $|U_{eH}|^2 > 10^{-2}$, we expect to observe at least a few $\nu_H$ events by detecting $\nu_e$ from its decay, using about one year of data from a 500-ton solar neutrino detector. Unlike figure~\ref{fig:eeSignalRateAndSpectrum}, much of this phase space cannot be explored by searching for $e^+e^-$ signals due to the short lifetime of $\nu_H$.

