\begin{abstract}
    Distinct knots $K, K'$ can sometimes share a common $p/q$-framed Dehn surgery. A folk conjecture held that for a fixed pair of knots, this can occur for at most one value of $p/q$. We disprove this conjecture by constructing pairs of distinct knots $K,K'$ that have common Dehn surgeries for four distinct slopes. 
    We also construct non-isotopic Legendrian knots $K,K'$ that have contactomorphic contact $(+1)$- and $(-1)$-surgeries, disproving an analogous conjecture in contact geometry.
\end{abstract}

\keywords{Dehn surgery, characterizing slopes, knot traces, knot invariants, Legendrian knots, contact surgery} 

