\subsection{Validation against ground truth data}\label{sec:valdation}

To assess how well the procedure described above can capture individuals' home and work locations, we measure i) the accuracy of the algorithm as the fraction of home/work locations that are correctly identified, and ii) the fraction of not-detected users, measured as the number of users where a home/work location was not detected against the number of users for which a home/work location was reported in the ground truth data (see Methods for more details). 
We run the validation using the ground-truth datasets D1 and D2, described in Methods.
In dataset D2, annotators reviewed complete stop sequences; therefore, the temporal dimension (changes of home and work locations) can not be considered.
For this reason, for D2, we consider a single window covering the entire data period (see Sec.SI-7 for parameter details).


\subsubsection{Algorithm performance across parameter choices}\label{sec:expconfig}

We find that sliding window length $\Delta_T$ and minimum home-time fraction $hf_{H}$ have the greatest impact on the accuracy of HoWDe’s home detection, while other parameters have a smaller effect (see Sec.SI-8).

Both the accuracy of the home location detection and the fraction of non-detected homes increase as the minimum home-time fraction threshold $hf_{H}$ increases (Figure~\ref{fig:home_work_config}a,b). 
In dataset D1, the fraction of non-detected homes increases with the window size,
but accuracy shows no substantial improvement beyond $\Delta_T = 28$ days ($t \pm 2$ weeks).
This suggests that a window of $4$ weeks offers a good balance between high accuracy and user retention for home detection.

