\section{Discussion}

In this work, we contributed to the development of accurate and robust methods for home and work detection.
Using two datasets (D1, D2) covering $\sim5,100$ individuals from over $80$ countries, we characterized home/work visit patterns. 
We found that individuals are most likely at home between midnight and 6 AM ($\sim75\%$ probability) and at work between 9 AM and 4 PM on workdays ($\sim50\%$). 
However, visit patterns and missing data vary widely across users and over time, and even within individual users’ routines.

Building on these insights, we provided an open-source implementation of \textit{HoWDe}, a home/work detection algorithm that integrates existing approaches while improving robustness against missing data. 
We validated \textit{HoWDe} against ground truth datasets, achieving up to $\sim97\%$ accuracy in D1 and $\sim88\%$ in D2 for home detection, and up to $\sim88\%$ accuracy in D1 and $\sim82\%$ accuracy in D2 for work detection.

Our analysis highlights the trade-off between accuracy and retaining users with low-quality data. 
We identified two key parameters affecting performance: the window length considered and the minimum visit proportion required for classification.
A window of at least $28$ days ensures a reliable home estimation, while work windows longer than $42$ days reduce user retention without significant accuracy gains.

We also revealed how the algorithm's accuracy varies across population groups, with the largest gaps appearing between urban and suburban areas (for home detection), and between high- and middle-income countries (for work detection).
Although these accuracy gaps persist across configurations, two case studies show that prioritizing accuracy over user retention is preferable for individual-level tasks, whereas maximizing retention may be more valuable for population-level analyses.

The insights gained from this study, along with a formalized algorithm, can support research on commuting~\cite{lucchini2023socioeconomic,centellegher2024long}, social contact networks~\cite{aleta2022quantifying}, and access to amenities~\cite{lucchini2021living}, with implications for areas such as computational social science, urban planning, and public health~\cite{oliver2020mobile,barreras2024exciting}.
Improving location data processing will enhance our understanding of both universal mobility laws and micro-level social dynamics~\cite{schlapfer2021universal}. 


Despite its strengths, \textit{HoWDe} has key limitations. 
While it allows customization of detection windows and thresholds, more advanced methods may be required to fully capture mobility patterns that are collected by diverse GPS-equipped devices \cite{rinzivillo2014purpose}. 
Its performance may also vary with lower temporal resolution or geographically limited datasets, particularly in underrepresented low-income regions with sparser mobile data. 
Future work should assess its applicability in rural and less densely populated areas~\cite{gvirtz2024limits}.

Detecting home and work locations raises important ethical concerns, particularly regarding privacy~\cite{de2013unique,zang2011anonymization}, as the geographical coordinates of these locations are highly identifying and can enable re-identification of individuals in mobility datasets. 
Currently, many data holders apply in-house heuristics to detect home and work, but these methods are often opaque, introducing methodological uncertainty. 
By providing a validated and open-source detection framework, our approach enables data holders to perform this sensitive step internally using a standardized and transparent pipeline. 
We hope this will facilitate the sharing of privacy-preserving datasets—for example, through the release of obfuscated or aggregated representations—while ensuring methodological consistency and scientific utility for downstream research.

In summary, by advancing methods for home and work detection, this study contributes to a more robust and scalable foundation for mobility research. 
By providing an open-source, validated solution, we hope to facilitate more reliable and ethical research, contributing to a deeper understanding of human mobility and its implications for society.
