\section{Material and Methods}\label{sec:mat_met}
\subsection{Dataset 1: DTU stop data and self-reported annotations}\label{subsec:D1}
The first dataset re-uses data collected during 2017-19 already used in previous publications \cite{alessandretti2020scales}, where stop locations were detected using the scalable stop-location detection algorithm InfoStop~\cite{aslak2020infostop}. The stop location data captures the mobility patterns of 96,065 users between 25 and 64 years old for 81 countries across continents. 

The dataset also contains self-recorded home and work labels for $2,309$ labelled home and $2,479$ work locations, collected across $4,599$ mobile phone and smart band users. 
Note that the labelled dataset is biased towards urban areas and higher- to middle-income countries. 
The spatial coordinates of locations are no longer available to preserve individuals' privacy. 

All data analysis was carried out in accordance with the European Union's General Data Protection Regulation 2016/679 (GDPR).

\subsection{Dataset 2: Veraset Movements data and annotation}\label{subsec:D2}
Anonymous raw GPS points are provided at a global level by the \textit{Veraset} data company in the framework of the \textit{World Bank} ``Monitoring COVID-19 policy response through human mobility data'' project. 
Veraset Movements data consists of raw GPS point information from devices all around the world. In contrast to the majority of GPS data providers, Verasets Movements are sourced from thousands of Software Development Kits (SDK) to reduce sample biases. It features coverage of about $5\%$ of the global population~\cite{VerasetMovementVeraset}.

In our work, unique-device GPS points are processed and analyzed as a proxy of individuals' mobility and patterns of visits. The data at our disposal spans over a period of \textit{$12$} months, starting from Jan 01, 2020, until \textit{Dec 31, 2020}. In this work, multiple countries were analyzed to assess the impact of the pandemic and the policy responses across a vast spectrum of potentially different behaviors.

Dataset D2, in particular, consists of the GPS trajectories of $100$ distinct devices for each of the $5$ selected countries: Brazil, Colombia, Indonesia, Mexico, and the Philippines. For each country independently, we require $33\%$ of the users to be living in high-wealth neighbourhoods, $33\%$ of the users to be living in medium-wealth neighbourhoods, and the remaining part in low-wealth neighbourhoods. Wealth classification and users' place of residence are drawn leveraging previous work on GPS mobility in middle-income countries~\cite{lucchini2023socioeconomic}. In particular, each user is also required to have a minimum level of recorded activity, consisting of at least a stop record in $20\%$ in the days before the COVID-19 pandemic declaration and $20\%$ of days with at least a stop record over the entire data period.

This second dataset involves, for each user's stop location sequences, the expert annotation through detailed inspection of location visits, satellite imagery, and local amenities, focusing on five middle-income countries (Brazil, Colombia, Indonesia, Mexico, and the Philippines) for 500 individuals, from which 287 have at least one work location annotated (see Sec. SI-1 detailed description of the annotation procedure).
Given that annotators only labelled the complete longitudinal stop sequences, an explicit consideration of changes in home and work for each individual is not possible.

\subsection{Home and Work locations detection algorithms: a primer}\label{sec:background}
Smartphones use an in-built GPS sensor to estimate the device's latitudes and longitude coordinates over time, at a frequency that varies depending on device usage.  
The device position is then collected by various location-based apps, such as navigation or social media apps.
These data may then be used by location intelligence companies or researchers to study human mobility and its regularities, both at the individual and at the population level.
Raw location data utilized for mobility analysis consists of timestamped sequences of \textit{(latitude, longitude)} records associated with a unique user ID. 

The first pre-processing step involved in the analysis is the so-called ``stop-location detection"~\cite{aslak2020infostop,hariharan2004project}. 
Stop location detection consists of (i) identifying records that are captured when individuals stay within a small area for a sufficient amount of time (e.g. when they are at home, at the office, or in a restaurant); (ii) applying spatial clustering to all these ``static records", to identify a set of unique locations, i.e. those places where people spend time.
After detecting the individuals' stops-locations, individual trajectories can be summarized as sequences of stays.
Each stay is associated with a start time (when the stay started), a duration (how long the stay lasted), and a location ID (where the stay happened). 

A second critical processing step often involved in mobility studies is to identify key locations, such as individuals' home and work locations, starting from the sequences of stays. 
To achieve this, researchers and practitioners often define heuristics based on the assumption that visits to home and work locations follow specific daily and weekly patterns. 
While a standard practice does not exist, in this work, we focus on two distinct and widely used state-of-the-art methods that exploit travel circadian rhythms and pattern regularities. These methods were designed and tested over the past decade with different approaches, in a collective scientific effort, separately leveraging diverse sets of mobile-phone-based data (e.g. Call Detail Records (CDRs), eXtended Detail Records, (XDRs), or GPS records)~\cite{frias2010towards,jo2012spatiotemporal,jiang2017activity,pappalardo2021evaluation} as well as activity-based travel surveys~\cite{jiang2012clustering}.
The main rationale is to rank locations based on the day of the week (weekdays vs. weekends) and the time of day (daytime vs. nighttime) when visits occur.


\subsubsection{The \textit{Atlas} approach}
The method used in~\cite{moro2021mobility,aleta2022quantifying,moro2023diversity,TheAtlasofInequality} (hereinafter ``Atlas'' method) defines a home location as the most probable location visited on weekdays between 10 p.m. and 6 a.m., i.e. the stop location where a device was found spending most of the weekday nighttime. While the Atlas methods mostly focuses on home locations, its definition can be easily generalized to the case of work locations. Following a similar rationale, we define an Atlas work location as the most probable stop location visited during weekdays daytime. % (xx).

\subsubsection{The \textit{Time-Geo} approach}
In contrast with the definition introduced by the Atlas method, more complex heuristics are adopted in Jiang et al.~\cite{jiang2016timegeo} (hereinafter referred to as the ``Time-geo'' method), in particular for the case of identifying work locations. 
Time-geo ranks stop locations based on the frequency of visits during weekday nights (between 7 p.m. and 8 a.m.) and weekends and picks the most visited location as an individual's home. 
The same logic is applied when detecting the work location, but now focusing on weekday work time boundaries (between 8 a.m. and 7 p.m.) and including additional requirements:
i) the candidate's work location is visited at least three times across the study period, and ii) the distance between the work candidate and an individual's home location is greater than $500$ meters. 

Note that both methods rely on the definition of stop location. Stop location definition might vary depending on the type of data analyzed and the specific characteristics of the data points, e.g. whether you use Call Detail Records (CDR), eXtended Detail Records (XDR), or GPS points. However, the principled choices these algorithms are founded on can naturally be extended to any of these different cases, making them easily generalizable.

In this work, stop locations are defined as spatiotemporally close sets of GPS points. All comparisons between home-work detection methods are always drawn from a set of stop locations constructed following identical procedures. For more information about stop location processing, we refer to the Supplementary Information (SI) Sec.SI-2 and the existing literature~\cite{aslak2020infostop,lucchini2021living}.


\subsection{Home and work visits behavioral clusters}\label{subsec:HW_visits_clustering}
Section~\ref{sec:temppatt} focused on the temporal attributes of users' daily mobility patterns. To better highlight groups of users with similar behavioral features, we reported the results in Figure ~\ref{fig:home_work_patterns}.c-f, sorting daily patterns based on the visit hour of the first home visit. behavioral groups were generated using the K-Modes clustering algorithm, a categorical adaptation of the traditional K-Means algorithm~\cite{cao2009new}. Here, we use daily sequences of 24 hourly bins with three possible stop categories. For example, in the case of home patterns, each sequence consists of either a home (category 1), a visit to ``a different location'', e.g., work or recreational locations (category 2), or an hour with missing data (category 3). A similar approach is used for work patterns, where visits to work location are considered as ``category 1'', while visits to home location are considered as ``a different location'' (category 2). The K-Modes algorithm requires as a parameter, the number of desired clusters to compute. In Figure~\ref{fig:home_work_patterns}.c,e we reported 6 clusters. In Figure~\ref{fig:home_work_patterns}.d,f we reported 3 and 4 clusters, respectively. Note that, while the number of clusters is the result of the selection of cluster number following the elbow method for each of the four plots, our purpose is to simply highlight the main different visit patterns. Following this idea, in Sec.SI-3, where we present results for the smaller D2 dataset, we manually selected three as the number of clusters used to report the hourly day-visit patterns.

\subsection{Behavioral profile entropy}\label{sec:behprof_entropy}

We quantify the degree to which users transition between behavioral profiles measuring the normalized Shannon entropy \cite{cover1991entropy}, treating each behavioral cluster as a distinct state, following:
\begin{equation}
H_{\text{norm}} = - \frac{1}{\log{k}}\sum_{i=1}^{k} p_i \log{p_i},
\end{equation}
where $k$ is the total number of possible clusters, and $p_i$ is the probability of occurrence of each state, calculated as: $p_i = \frac{n_i}{N}$ where $n_i$ are the total days classified in cluster $i$ and $N$ the total number of days for a given user. 
Normalizing by the maximum entropy $\frac{1}{\log{k}}$, ensures that entropy remains comparable across cases (weekend vs. weekday and home vs. work), staying within the same range from 0 (when there is only one profile) to 1 (when there are multiple profiles, and all are equally important).

\subsection{HoWDe parameters details}\label{sec:howde_params}

$\mathbf{dhn}$:
First, to account for a minimum level of information within a day, we focus only on days with hour sequences consisting of at least three stops, i.e. stops visited in at least three hours of the day (i.e. days with at least three hours of data). The HoWDe algorithm, however, can be tuned to handle different thresholds based on the specific needs or constraints in place.% "bnd_nan"

\noindent$\mathbf{dn_{H,W}}$: Second, to account for the potentially varying data quality across different datasets, we introduce two independently configurable pairs of parameters for home and work locations. 
The first pair of parameters works as a maximum \textit{fraction of days without data per sliding window}, marking a day with an ``undetected home/work location'' if the fraction of days without data within the sliding window exceeds the threshold. % "bnb_none_home/work"

\noindent$\mathbf{hf_{H,W}}$: A third pair of parameters threshold the minimum \textit{fraction of hours per night or day period} for a location to be home or work respectively.
If the average fraction of visits across all days in the sliding window is below the threshold, the location cannot be considered a potential home/work. % "bnd_freq_home/work"

\noindent$\mathbf{df_{H,W}}$: Fourth, while home locations are expected to be visited almost every day (provided that enough data are available), work locations are likely to follow a different pattern, as those locations will be subject to local work habits, e.g. four days working week.
To account for regularities across days, we introduce an additional requirement for work locations: a work location should be visited for a minimum \textit{fraction of days with data}.

