\begin{abstract}
Linearly distributive categories (LDC), introduced by Cockett and Seely to model multiplicative linear logic, are categories equipped with two monoidal structures that interact via linear distributivities. A seminal result in monoidal category theory is the Fox theorem, which characterizes cartesian categories as symmetric monoidal categories whose objects are equipped with canonical comonoid structures. The aim of this work is to extend the Fox theorem to LDCs and characterize the subclass of cartesian LDCs. To do so, we introduce the concepts of medial linearly distributive categories, medial linear functors, and medial linear transformations. The former are LDCs which respect the logical medial rule, appearing frequently in deep inference, or alternatively are the appropriate structure at the intersection of LDCs and duoidal categories. 
\end{abstract}

% NOTE: it is good practice to \label all headings (and proclamations) immediately

\section{Introduction}\label{sec:Introduction}

Within monoidal category theory, \emph{cartesian categories} refer to categories whose monoidal product is given by the categorical product and whose monoidal unit is the terminal object. Perhaps the most well-known result concerning cartesian categories is Fox's theorem. In his seminal 1976 paper ``Coalgebras and cartesian categories'', Fox demonstrated that there is a right adjoint to the inclusion functor from cartesian categories into the category of symmetric monoidal categories \cite{Fox_1976}. This right adjoint functor consists of taking the cocommutative comonoids of a symmetric monoidal category $(\cX, \os, I)$, and their maps, in other words considering objects $A$ equipped with coherent diagonal and counit maps:
\[\Delta_A\c A\rarr A\os A \qquad\qquad  e_A\c A\rarr I\]
As a corollary of this adjunction, we see that cartesian categories are precisely the symmetric monoidal categories whose objects have a canonical comonoid structure and whose maps preserve these structures. \\

Cartesian categories have played a key role in the field of categorical logic. Indeed, one of the greatest successes of categorical logic was Lambek’s equivalence between intuitionistic propositional calculus and cartesian closed categories \cite{Lambek_1972}. If we extend this paradigm and consider cartesian closed categories with a notion of involutive negation to capture the semantics of classical logic, these categories collapse to Boolean algebras due to Joyal’s paradox. Thus, there has not yet been a definitive framework for the categorical semantics of classical logic. \\

The field of categorical logic continued to thrive with the development of linear logic by Girard \cite{Girard_1987}. The categorical semantics for linear logic were subsequently explored by Seely, who identified that the appropriate definition was given by $*$-autonomous categories \cite{Seely_1989}, previously introduced by Barr \cite{Barr_1979}. \emph{Linearly distributive categories} (LDC)s were later introduced by Cockett and Seely as alternative categorical semantics for linear logic, which take the notions of multiplicative conjunction and disjunction, and their interaction via linear distributivity, as primitive \cite{Cockett_Seely_1997_LDC}. In particular, LDCs are categories $\bX$ with two monoidal structures $(\bX, \ot, \top)$ and $(\bX, \op, \bot)$, the former known as the tensor structure and the latter as the par structure, equipped with linear distributivity natural transformations:
\[\delta^L_{A,B,C}\c A\ot (B\op C)\rarr (A\ot B)\op C \quad\qquad \delta^R_{A,B,C}\c (A\op B)\ot C\rarr A\op (B\ot C).
\]

\vspace{0.5\baselineskip}
The theory of categorical linear logic proved useful in developing categorical semantics for classical logic. LDCs provide the minimal framework necessary to discuss two connectives with left and right introduction rules, and cut as the only structural rule. Since any reasonable model of classical logic should at least include such structure, categorical models for classical logic can be built upon the foundations of LDCs (or $*$-autonomous categories, if negation is instead taken as primitive). \\

As the rules of contraction and weakening are central structural rules of classical logic, which are traditionally modeled by cartesian monoidal structures, a recent paradigm of categorical classical logic has been to add ``cartesian'' structure to LDCs (or $*$-autonomous categories). In this vein, F\"{u}hrmann  and Pym developed the notions of classical categories and Dummett categories, poset-enriched $*$-autonomous categories and LDCs respectively whose objects have coherent notions of $\ot$-diagonals and counits, and $\op$-codiagonals and units \cite{Fuhrmann_Pym_2007}. While, Stra{\ss}burger and Lamarche both seperately considered $*$-autonomous categories whose objects also have coherent $\ot$-diagonals and counits, and $\op$-codiagonals and units, but whose maps do not necessarily preserve these structures \cite{Strassburger_2007_1, Lamarche_2007}. \\

More recently, Naeimabadi developed the theory of cartesian linear bicategories \cite{Naeimabadi_2024} and recent work has investigated De Morgan and Peirce's calculus of relations, providing an axiomatisation based on the concepts of cartesian and linear bicategories \cite{Bonchi_DiGiorgio_Haydon_Sobocinski_2024}. In fact, the central examples of linear bicategories, the bicategorical analogues of LDCs introduced by Cockett, Koslowski, and Seely \cite{Cockett_Koslowski_Seely_2000}, are also cartesian bicategories. Fox’s theorem is, in fact, central to the theory of cartesian bicategories, as introduced by Carboni and Walters \cite{Carboni_Walters_1987}. \\

It was within this context that the idea arose to investigate \emph{cartesian linearly distributive categories} (CLDC) and to develop a linearly distributive version of Fox’s theorem. CLDCs refer to LDCs whose tensor structure is cartesian and par structure is cocartesian. They were first introduced alongside LDCs by Cockett and Seely, though the notion received little attention beyond the first paper \cite{Cockett_Seely_1997_LDC}. \\

In developing a linearly distributive analogue of Fox’s theorem, it quickly became apparent that no such construction could be adapted to all LDCs. For the same reason that Fox’s theorem is an adjunction between cartesian categories and symmetric monoidal categories, we needed to restrict to LDCs equipped with additional structure maps:
\[\Delta_{\bot}\c \bot\rarr \bot\ot\bot \qquad \nabla_{\top}\c \top\op\top\rarr \top \qquad m\c \bot\rarr \top\]
\[\mu_{A,B,C,D}\c (A\ot B)\op (C\ot D)\rarr (A\op C)\ot (B\op D) \]
Viewed as a logical entailment, the latter map is known as the \emph{medial rule}, first introduced by Br\"unnler and Tiu for a system of classical logic within the deep inference framework \cite{Brunnler_Tiu_2001}. Moreover, medial maps were central to both Stra{\ss}burger’s and Lamarche’s categorical models for classical logic. \\

Within monoidal category theory, these medial maps are better known as instances of the interchange maps of \emph{duoidal categories}. Duoidal categories are categories $\cX$ with two monoidal structures $(\cX, \diamond, I)$ and $(\cX, \star, J)$, equipped with precisely the structure maps introduced above \cite{Aguiar_Mahajan_2010}. The theory of duoidal categories first appeared in the work of Joyal and Street while studying braided monoidal categories \cite{Joyal_Street_1993}. \\

With this perspective in mind, we introduce the notion of \emph{medial linearly distributive categories} (MLDC), inspired both by the work of Stra{\ss}burger and Lamarche in categorical classical logic and by the theory of duoidal categories. MLDCs can be regarded both as the categorical semantics for multiplicative linear logic with the medial rule, and as a appropriate intersection point between LDCs and duoidal categories. \\

Leveraging the well-developed theory of LDCs and duoidal categories, we define the appropriate functors and transformations between MLDCs. Within this framework, we consider an analogue to comonoids: \emph{medial bimonoids}, objects $A$ equipped with coherent $\ot$-diagonal, $\ot$-counit, $\op$-multiplication, and $\op$-unit maps,
\[\Delta_A\c A\rarr A\ot A \qquad e_A\c A\rarr \top \qquad \nabla_A\c A\op A\rarr A \qquad u_A\c \bot\rarr A \]
Considering the bicommutative medial bimonoids and their maps in a symmetric MLDC gives the construction needed to define a right adjoint 2-functor to the inclusion 2-functor from CLDCs to the 2-category of symmetric MLDCs. This yields the desired linearly distributive Fox theorem and, as a corollary, a characterization of CLDCs as symmetric MLDCs whose objects have coherent medial bimonoid structure. \\

In the process of developing this paper, it became evident how poorly understood CLDCs are. This prompted the author to collaborate with Dr. Pacaud Lemay to explore the nature of CLDCs beyond their characterization via the linearly distributive Fox theorem. This resulted in a separate paper, ``Cartesian Linearly Distributive Categories: Revisited'' \cite{Kudzman-Blais_Lemay_2025}. While both papers are written to be independently comprehensible, we recommend reading them in tandem to gain a complete perspective on cartesian and cocartesian structure in the context of LDCs. \\

\noindent {\bf Outline.} Given that the results developed in this paper are based on the theories of Fox's theorem, LDCs, and duoidal categories, the paper begins with three substantial preliminary sections. Section~\ref{sec:Fox_thm} presents the basics of monoidal categories, cartesian categories, and Fox's theorem. Section~\ref{sec:LDC} reviews the literature on LDCs, including linear functors and linear transformations. Section~\ref{sec:duoidal_categories} introduces the necessary background on duoidal categories. Then, Section~\ref{sec:MLDC} begins by motivating the introduction of the medial rule and subsequently defines MLDCs. The theory of MLDCs is developed: symmetric MLDCs are defined, adding negation is discussed, examples are introduced, and properties are presented. Section~\ref{sec:medial_linear_functor} defines the appropriate linear functors and linear transformations between symmetric MLDCs. Section~\ref{sec:medial_bimonoids} introduces medial bimonoids and shows that considering such structures and their maps does, in fact, yield a CLDC. Finally, Section~\ref{sec:LD_Fox} proves the main result of this paper: the linearly distributive Fox theorem.\\

\noindent {\bf Conventions.} There are a few notational choices made in this paper and we take a moment to detail the most important.
\begin{itemize}
    \item Composition of morphisms/arrows in a category is denoted by the symbol $;$ and is written in diagrammatic order. Objects in a category are denoted by capital letters, while the morphisms are denoted by lowercase letters. 
    \item Monoidal products and monoidal units will be denoted with various different symbols to help distinguish the particular type of category in question. In particular:
    \begin{itemize}
    \item $\os$ and $I$ are used for standard monoidal categories \cX. 
    \item $\ot$ and $\top$ are used for the tensor monoidal structure, and $\op$ and $\bot$ for the par monoidal structure in a linearly distributive category \bX, in agreement with the notation introduced by Cockett and Seely in \cite{Cockett_Seely_1997_LDC}, as opposed to Girard's notation \cite{Girard_1987}. 
    \item $\diamond$ and $I$ are used for the first monoidal structure and $\star$ and $J$ for the second in a duoidal category \cX, in agreement with the notation introduced by Aguiar and Mahajan \cite{Aguiar_Mahajan_2010}. 
    \item $\times$ and $\bone$ denote the binary categorical product and the terminal object respectively, while $+$ and $\bzero$ denote the binary categorical coproduct and the initial object in a category.
    \end{itemize}
\end{itemize}

\section{Fox Theorem}\label{sec:Fox_thm}

We begin by giving a fairly complete description of the Fox theorem for cartesian categories, without proof, as the logic behind the traditional version of the result will be a guiding thread to generalizing it to the context of linearly distributive categories. We invite any reader who is well versed in the Fox theorem to skip this subsection.

\subsection{Monoidal Categories}  \hfill\

\vspace{0.5\baselineskip}
We briefly outline the foundational definitions of monoidal category theory, primarily to establish the notation used throughout this paper and to provide a reference for the axioms, which will frequently appear in the proofs that follow.

\begin{definition}{\cite[Sec 7.1]{MacLane_1998}}
A {\bf monoidal category} $(\cX, \os, I, \alpha, \rho, \lambda)$ consists of:
\begin{itemize}
\item a category $(\cX, ;, 1_X)$,
\item the {\bf product} and the {\bf unit} functors
\begin{align*}
& \os\c\cX\times\cX\rarr\cX &I\c{\bf1}\rarr\cX
\end{align*}
\item the {\bf associator}, the {\bf right unitor} and the {\bf left unitor} natural isomorphisms 
\begin{align*}
& \alpha\c(\os\times1_{\ccX});\os\Rarr (1_{\ccX}\times\os);\os & \alpha_{X,Y,Z}\c (X\os Y)\os Z\rarr X\os (Y\os Z) \\
& \rho\c 1_{\ccX}\Rarr (1_{\ccX}\times I);\os & \rho_{X}\c X\rarr X\os I \\
& \lambda\c 1_{\ccX}\Rarr (I\times 1_{\ccX});\os &\lambda_{X}\c X\rarr I\os X
\end{align*}
\end{itemize}
such that the associativity pentagon \eqref{cc:monoidal_cat_asso_pentagon} and unit triangle identities \eqref{cc:monoidal_cat_unit_triangle} hold.
\begin{equation}\begin{gathered}\label{cc:monoidal_cat_asso_pentagon}
\xymatrixrowsep{1.75pc}\xymatrixcolsep{1.75pc}\xymatrix@L=0.5pc{ 
& (W\os X)\os (Y\os Z)\ar[dr]^{\alpha_{W,X,Y\os Z}}  \\
((W\os X)\os Y)\os Z \ar[ru]^{\alpha_{W\os X, Y, Z}} \ar[d]_{\alpha_{W,X,Y}\os 1_{Z}} && W\os (X\os (Y\os Z)) \\
(W\os (X\os Y))\os Z\ar[rr]_{\alpha_{W, X\os Y, Z}} && W\os ((X\os Y)\os Z \ar[u]_{1_{W}\os \alpha_{X,Y,Z}}
}
\end{gathered}\end{equation}
\begin{equation}\begin{gathered}\label{cc:monoidal_cat_unit_triangle}
\xymatrixrowsep{1.75pc}\xymatrixcolsep{1.75pc}\xymatrix@L=0.5pc{  
& (X\os I) \os Y\ar[dd]^{\alpha_{X,I,Y}}  \\
X\os Y\ar[ru]^{\rho_{X}\os 1_{Y}} \ar[dr]_{1_{X}\os \lambda_{Y}} \\
& X\os (I\os Y)
}
\end{gathered}\end{equation}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}{\cite[Sec 9.1]{MacLane_1998}}
A monoidal category is {\bf braided} if it has a {\em braiding} natural isomorphism 
\[ \sigma\c \os\Rarr {switch}_{\ccX,\ccX};\os \qquad\qquad \sigma_{X, Y}\c X\os Y\rarr Y\os X \]
which behaves coherently with associators  \eqref{cc:braided_monoidal_cat_asso_hexagon} and with unitors \eqref{cc:braided_monoidal_cat_unit_triangle}.
\begin{equation}\begin{gathered}\label{cc:braided_monoidal_cat_asso_hexagon}
\xymatrixrowsep{1.75pc}\xymatrixcolsep{2.75pc}\xymatrix@L=0.5pc{ 
(X\os Y)\os Z \ar[r]^{\alpha_{X,Y,Z}} \ar[d]_{\sigma_{X,Y}\os 1_{Z}} & X\os (Y\os Z)\ar[r]^{\sigma_{X, Y\os Z}} & (Y\os Z)\os X \ar[d]^{\alpha_{ Y, Z, X}} \\
%
(Y\os X)\os Z \ar[r]_{\alpha_{Y, X, Z}} & Y\os (X\os Z) \ar[r]_{1_{Y}\os \sigma_{X,Z}} & Y\os (Z\os X) \\
X\os (Y\os Z) \ar[r]^{\alpha^{-1}_{X,Y,Z}} \ar[d]_{1_X \os\sigma_{Y,Z}} & (X\os Y)\os Z\ar[r]^{\sigma_{X\os Y, Z}} & Z\os (X\os Y) \ar[d]^{\alpha^{-1}_{Z, X, Y}} \\
%
X\os (Z\os Y) \ar[r]_{\alpha^{-1}_{X, Z, Y}} & (X\os Z)\os Y \ar[r]_{\sigma_{X,Z}\os 1_Y} & (Z\os X)\os Y \\
}
\end{gathered}\end{equation}
\begin{equation}\begin{gathered}\label{cc:braided_monoidal_cat_unit_triangle}
\xymatrixrowsep{1.75pc}\xymatrixcolsep{2.75pc}\xymatrix@L=0.5pc{ 
X \ar[r]^{\rho_X}\ar[dr]_{\lambda_X} & X\os I\ar[d]^{\sigma_{X, I}} \\ 
& I\os X
}
\end{gathered}\end{equation}

\vspace{\baselineskip}
\noindent A monoidal category is {\bf symmetric} if it is braided and the braiding is self-inverse \eqref{cc:braiding_self_inverse}.

\begin{equation}\begin{gathered}\label{cc:braiding_self_inverse}
\xymatrixrowsep{1.75pc}\xymatrixcolsep{2.75pc}\xymatrix@L=0.5pc{ 
        X\os Y \ar[r]^{\sigma_{X,Y}}\ar@{=}[dr] & Y\os X \ar[d]^{\sigma_{Y,X}} \\
        & X\os Y
}
\end{gathered}\end{equation}
\end{definition}

Given a braided monoidal category, we can define a composite of associators and braidings, sometimes known as the ``canonical flip''. This natural isomorphism is key to the Fox theorem, therefore, to improve readability, we define it here. 

\begin{definition}
Let \cX\ be a braided monoidal category. Define the natural isomorphism 
\[ \tau_{W,X,Y,Z}\c (W\os X)\os (Y\os Z) \Rarr (W\os Y)\os (X\os Z) \]
to be the following equivalent composites
\begin{equation*}
\xymatrixrowsep{1.75pc}\xymatrixcolsep{2.75pc}\xymatrix@L=0.5pc{ 
(W\os X)\os (Y\os Z)\ar[d]_{\alpha_{W,X,Y\os Z}} \\
W\os (X\os (Y\os Z))\ar[r]^{1_{W}\os\alpha_{X,Y,Z}^{-1}} \ar[d]_{1_{W}\os\sigma_{X, Y\os Z}} & W\os ((X\os Y)\os Z) \ar[r]^{1_{W}\os(\sigma_{X, Y}\os 1_{Z})} & W \os ((Y\os X)\os Z)\ar[d]^{1_{W}\os\alpha_{ Y,X,Z}} \\
W\os ((Y\os Z)\os X) \ar[r]_{1_W \os \alpha_{Y,Z,X}} & W\os (Y\os (Z\os X))\ar[r]_{1_W\os (1_Y \os \sigma_{X,Z})}& W\os (Y\os (X\os Z))\ar[d]^{\alpha^{-1}_{W, Y, X\os Z}}\\
&& (W\os Y)\os (X\os Z)
}
\end{equation*}
\end{definition}

\subsection{Monoidal Functors and Transformations}  \hfill\

\vspace{0.5\baselineskip}
The Fox theorem is an adjunction between the 2-categories of symmetric monoidal categories and cartesian categories. As such, we need to the appropriate notion of functors and natural transformations between monoidal categories.

\begin{definition}{\cite[Sec 9.2]{MacLane_1998}}
Consider monoidal categories $(\cX, \os, I)$ and $(\cY, \os, I)$.
A {\bf monoidal functor} $(F, m_{I},m_\os)\c(\cX,\os,I)\rarr(\cY,\os, I)$ is a functor $F\c\cX\rarr\cY$ equipped with structure maps:
\begin{itemize}
	\item a morphism $m_{I}\c I\rarr F(I)$, and
	\item a natural transformation 
	\begin{align*}
	&m_\os\c (F\times F);\os \Rarr \os;F &{m_\os}_{A, B}\c F(A)\os F(B)\rarr F(A\os B)
	\end{align*}
\end{itemize}
satisfying associativity \eqref{cc:mon_functor_associativity} and unitality \eqref{cc:mon_functor_unit} coherence conditions. 
%
\begin{equation}\begin{gathered}\label{cc:mon_functor_associativity}
\xymatrixrowsep{1.75pc}\xymatrixcolsep{2.5pc}\xymatrix@L=0.5pc{  
(F(X) \os F(Y))\os F(Z)\ar[r]^{\alpha_{F(X), F(Y), F(Z)}} \ar[d]_{{m_\os}_{X, Y}\os 1_{F(Z)}}& F(X)\os (F(Y)\os F(Z))\ar[r]^{1_{F(X)}\os {m_\os}_{Y, Z}} & F(X) \os F(Y\os Z)\ar[d]^{{m_\os}_{X, Y\os Z}} \\
F(X\os Y)\os F(Z)\ar[r]_{{m_\os}_{X\os Y, Z}} & F((X\os Y)\os Z)\ar[r]_{F(\alpha_{X,Y,Z}) } & F(X\os (Y\os Z))
}
\end{gathered}\end{equation}
%
\begin{equation}\begin{gathered}\label{cc:mon_functor_unit}
\xymatrixrowsep{1.75pc}\xymatrixcolsep{2.5pc}\xymatrix@L=0.5pc{
F(X)\ar[r]^-{\rho_{F(X)}}\ar[d]_-{F(\rho_X)} & F(X) \os I\ar[d]^{1_{F(X)}\os m_I} & F(X)\ar[r]^-{\lambda_{F(X)}}\ar[d]_{F(\lambda_X)} & I \os F(X)\ar[d]^{m_I\os 1_{F(X)}} \\
F(X\os I) & F(X)\os F(I) \ar[l]^{{m_\os}_{X, I}} & F(I\os X) & F(I) \os F(X)\ar[l]^-{{m_\os}_{I, X}}
}
\end{gathered}\end{equation}

\vspace{\baselineskip}
\noindent A {\bf comonoidal functor} $F = (F, n_{I}, n_\os)\c(\cX, \os, I)\rarr(\cY,\os,I)$ is a monoidal functor from $\cX^{op}$ to $\cY^{op}$.

\vspace{\baselineskip}
\noindent If \cX\ and \cY\ are symmetric, then $(F, m_{I},m_\os)\c(\cX,\os,I)\rarr(\cY,\os, I)$ is a {\bf symmetric monoidal functor} if it additionally interacts coherently with the braidings \eqref{cc:mon_functor_braiding}.
%
\begin{equation}\begin{gathered}\label{cc:mon_functor_braiding}
\xymatrixrowsep{1.75pc}\xymatrixcolsep{2.5pc}\xymatrix@L=0.5pc{
F(X)\os F(Y)\ar[r]^{\sigma_{F(X), F(Y)}}\ar[d]_{{m_\os}_{X,Y}} & F(Y)\os F(X)\ar[d]^{{m_\os}_{Y,X}} \\
F(X\os Y)\ar[r]_{F(\sigma_{X,Y})} & F(Y\os X)
}
\end{gathered}\end{equation}

\vspace{\baselineskip}
\noindent A monoidal (or comonoidal) functor is called {\bf strong} when the structures maps $m_{I}$ and $m_{\os}$ (or $n_I$ and $n_\os$) are isomorphisms.
\end{definition}

\begin{remark} Given a symmetric monoidal functor $(F, m_{I},m_\os)\c(\cX,\os,I)\rarr(\cY,\os, I)$, we note that the following commuting diagram involving the canonical flip for use in a future proof:
\begin{equation}\begin{gathered}\label{cc:strong_symmetric_monoidal_flip}
\resizebox{0.9\linewidth}{!}{\xymatrixrowsep{2.5pc}\xymatrixcolsep{1.75pc}\xymatrix@L=0.5pc{ 
(F(W)\os F(X)) \os (F(Y)\os F(Z))\ar[r]^-{{m_\os}_{W,X}\os {m_\os}_{Y, Z}} \ar[d]_-{\tau_{F(W), F(X), F(Y), F(Z)}}& F(W\os X) \os F(Y\os Z)\ar[r]^-{{m_\os}_{W\os X, Y\os Z}} & F((W\os X)\os (Y\os Z))\ar[d]^-{F(\tau_{W, X, Y, Z})} \\
(F(W)\os F(Y)) \os (F(X)\os F(Z))\ar[r]_-{{m_\os}_{W,Y}\os {m_\os}_{X,Z}} & F(W\os Y) \os F(X\os Z) \ar[r]_-{{m_\os}_{W\os Y, X\os Z}}& F((W\os Y)\os (X\os Z))
}}
\end{gathered}\end{equation}
\end{remark}

\begin{definition}{\cite[Sec 9.2]{MacLane_1998}}\label{def:monoidal_transformation}
Let $(F, m^{F}_{I},m^{F}_\os), (G, m^{G}_{I},m^{G}_\os)\c\cX\rarr\cY$ be monoidal functors. A {\bf monoidal transformation} $\alpha\c(F, m^{F}_{I},m^{F}_\os)\Rarr (G, m^{G}_{I},m^{G}_\os)$ is a natural transformation $\alpha\c F\Rarr G$ whose component morphisms behave coherently with the monoidal products and the units \eqref{cc:mon_trans}.

\begin{equation}\begin{gathered}\label{cc:mon_trans}
\xymatrixrowsep{1.75pc}\xymatrixcolsep{2.75pc}\xymatrix@L=0.5pc{
F(X) \os F(Y) \ar[r]^{\alpha_X \os \alpha_Y} \ar[d]_{{m_\os^F}_{X,Y}}& G(X) \os G(Y) \ar[d]^{{m_\os^G}_{X, Y}} && I\ar[r]^{m^F_I} \ar[dr]_{m^G_I} & F(I)\ar[d]^{\alpha_I} \\
F(X\os Y) \ar[r]_{\alpha_{X\os Y}} & G(X\os Y) && & G(I)
}
\end{gathered}\end{equation}

\vspace{\baselineskip}
\noindent Let $(F, n^{F}_{I},n^{F}_\os), (G, n^{G}_{I},n^{G}_\os)\c\cX\rarr\cY$ be comonoidal functors. A {\bf comonoidal transformation} $\alpha\c(F, n^{F}_{I},n^{F}_\os)\Rarr (G, n^{G}_{I},n^{G}_\os)$ is a monoidal transformation between the monoidal functors $(F, n^{F}_{I},n^{F}_\os), (G, n^{G}_{I},n^{G}_\os)\c\cX^{op}\rarr\cY^{op}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition} There is a 2-category $\mathbf{SMON}$ of symmetric monoidal categories, strong symmetric monoidal functors and monoidal transformations.
\end{proposition}

\subsection{Cartesian Categories}  \hfill\

\vspace{0.5\baselineskip}
The Fox theorem is concerned with characterizing the symmetric monoidal categories which are cartesian, therefore we quickly introduce the appropriate sub-2-category $\mathbf{CART}$. 

\begin{definition}\label{def:cartesiancat} 
A {\bf cartesian category} is a category with finite categorical products and a {\bf cocartesian category} is a category with finite categorical coproducts, where binary products, terminal objects, binary coproducts and initial objects, with the corresponding unique maps, are denoted by
\begin{equation*}
\xymatrix{
& C\ar[ld]_{f}\ar[dr]^{g}\ar@{-->}[d]_(.6){\langle f,g\rangle} & && A\ar@{-->}[d]_{t_A} && A\ar[r]^-{\iota^0_{A,B}}\ar[dr]_{h} & A+ B\ar@{-->}[d]_(0.4){[h,k]}& B\ar[l]_-{\iota^1_{A,B}}\ar[dl]^{k} && \bzero\ar@{-->}[d]_{b_A}\\
A& A\times B\ar[l]^{\pi^0_{A,B}}\ar[r]_{\pi^1_{A,B}} & B && \bone && & C &&& A
}
\end{equation*}
\end{definition}

Cartesian categories have a canonical monoidal structure given by the binary categorical product and the terminal object, and similarly for the cocartesian categories:

\begin{proposition}{\cite[Sec 3.5]{MacLane_1998}}\label{prop:monoidal_cart_cocart}
Consider a cartesian category \cX, then it is a symmetric monoidal category $(\cX, \times, \bone)$ whose monoidal product is the categorical product of \cX and monoidal unit is the terminal object of \cX:
\begin{align*}
&(X,Y)\mapsto X\times Y \\
&(f\c X\rarr X', g\c Y\rarr Y')\mapsto f\times g = \langle \pi^0_{X, Y};f, \pi^1_{X,Y}; g\rangle\c X\times Y\rarr X'\times Y'
\end{align*}
with isomorphisms 
\begin{align*}
& \alpha_{X,Y,Z} = \langle \pi^0_{X\times Y, Z}; \pi^0_{X,Y}, \pi^1_{X,Y}\times 1_{Z}\rangle \c (X\times Y)\times Z\rarr X\times (Y\times Z)\quad \\
& \rho_{X} = \langle 1_{X}, t_X\rangle \c X\rarr X\times\bone \\
& \lambda_{X} = \langle t_X, 1_X\rangle \c X\rarr \bone\times X\\
& \sigma_{X, Y} = \langle \pi^1_{X,Y}, \pi^0_{X,Y}\rangle \c X\times Y\rarr Y\times X
\end{align*}
%
Consider a cocartesian category \cX, then it is a symmetric monoidal category $(\cX, +, \bzero)$ whose monoidal product is the categorical coproduct of \cX and monoidal unit is the initial object:
\begin{align*}
&(X,Y)\mapsto X+Y \\
&(f\c X\rarr X', g\c Y\rarr Y')\mapsto f+g = [ f; \iota^0_{X', Y'}, g; \iota^1_{X',Y'}]\c X+Y\rarr X'+ Y'
\end{align*}
with isomorphisms 
\begin{align*}
& \alpha_{X,Y,Z} = [\iota^0_{X,Y};\iota^0_{X+Y, Z}, \iota^1_{X,Y}+1_{Z}] \c X+(Y+Z)\rarr (X+Y)+Z \\
& \rho_{X} = [1_{X}, b_{X}] \c X+\bzero\rarr X \\
& \lambda_{X} = [b_{X}, 1_{X}]\c \bzero+ X\rarr X\\
& \sigma_{X, Y} = [\iota^1_{Y,X}, \iota^0_{Y,X}] \c X+ Y\rarr Y+ X
\end{align*}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition} 
There is a 2-category \CART of cartesian categories, strong symmetric monoidal functors and monoidal transformations, known as {\bf cartesian functors} and {\bf cartesian transformations} in this context. It is a full sub-2-category of $\mathbf{SMC}$. 
\end{proposition}

\subsection{Comonoids}  \hfill\

\vspace{0.5\baselineskip}
With the 2-categories now appropriately defined, we can introduce the construction which provides the adjoint to the inclusion.

\begin{definition}{\cite{Fox_1976}} Let \cX\ denote a monoidal category.
\begin{itemize}
\item A {\bf comonoid} in \cX\ is a triple $\langle X, \Delta_X, e_X\rangle$ of an object $A$ in \cX\ equipped with two morphisms, the {\bf diagonal} and the {\bf counit}
\[ \Delta_X\c X\rarr X\os X \qquad\qquad e_X\c X\rarr I \]
satisfying 
%
\begin{equation}\begin{gathered}\label{cc:comonoid_assoc}
\xymatrixrowsep{1.75pc}\xymatrixcolsep{2.75pc}\xymatrix@L=0.5pc{ 
X \ar[r]^-{\Delta_X} \ar[d]_-{\Delta_X}& X\os X \ar[r]^-{1_X \os \Delta_X}  & X\os(X\os X)\\
X\os X\ar[rr]_-{\Delta_X\os 1_X} && (X\os X)\os X\ar[u]_-{\alpha_{X,X,X}} 
}
\end{gathered}\end{equation}
%
\begin{equation}\begin{gathered}\label{cc:comonoid_units}
\xymatrixrowsep{1.75pc}\xymatrixcolsep{1.75pc}\xymatrix@L=0.5pc{ 
X\ar@{=}[rrd]\ar[r]^-{\Delta_X}\ar[d]_-{\Delta_X} & X\os X \ar[r]^-{1_X\os e_X} & X\os I\ar[d]^-{\rho_X^{-1}}\\
X\os X\ar[r]_-{e_X\os 1_X} & I\os X\ar[r]_-{\lambda_X^{-1}} & X 
}
\end{gathered}\end{equation}

\item A {\bf comonoid morphism} $f\c \langle X, \Delta_{X}, e_X\rangle\rarr\langle Y, \Delta_{Y}, e_Y\rangle$ is a morphism $f\c X\rarr Y$ in \cX\ such that 
\begin{equation}\begin{gathered}\label{cc:comonoid_morphism}
\xymatrixrowsep{1.75pc}\xymatrixcolsep{2.75pc}\xymatrix{ 
X\ar[r]^-{f}\ar[d]_-{\Delta_X} & Y\ar[d]^-{\Delta_Y} & X\ar[r]^-{f}\ar[dr]_-{e_X} & Y\ar[d]^-{e_Y}\\
X\os X\ar[r]_-{f\os f} & Y\os Y & & I
}
\end{gathered}\end{equation}

\item If \cX\ is a symmetric monoidal category, a comonoid $\langle X, \Delta_X, e_X\rangle$ is {\bf cocommutative} if 
\begin{equation}\begin{gathered}\label{cc:cocommutative_comonoid}
\xymatrixrowsep{1.75pc}\xymatrixcolsep{2.75pc}\xymatrix{ 
X\ar[r]^-{\Delta_X}\ar[dr]_-{\Delta_X} & X\os X \ar[d]^-{\sigma_{X,X}}\\
& X\os X
}
\end{gathered}\end{equation}

\end{itemize}
We denote the category of cocommutative comonoids and comonoid morphisms in \cX\ by $\mathrm{C}[\cX]$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:unique_comonoid_cartesian_cat}
Given a cartesian category $(\cX, \times, \bone)$, every object $A$ in \cX\ has a canonical unique cocommutative \mbox{$\times$-comonoid} structure 
\[ \langle A, \langle 1_A, 1_A\rangle \c A\rarr A\times A, t_A\c A\rarr\bone\rangle \]
and every arrow $f\c A\rarr B$ in \cX\ is a comonoid morphism with respect to these structures.
\end{lemma}

The category of cocommutative comonoids in \cX\ inherits a symmetric monoidal structure from \cX, which is in fact cartesian.

\begin{lemma}{\cite{Fox_1976}}\label{lem:constructing_comonoids}
Given a pair of cocommutative comonoids $\langle X, \Delta_X, e_X\rangle$ and $\langle X', \Delta_{X'}, e_{ X'}\rangle$ in \cX, then the triple $\langle X\os X', \Delta_{X\os X'}, e_{X\os X'}\rangle$ defined by
\[ \Delta_{X\os X'} = X\os X' \xrightarrow{\Delta_{X} \os \Delta_{X'}} (X\os X)\os (X'\os X') \xrightarrow{s_{X,X,X',X'}} (X\os X')\os (X\os X')\]
\[ e_{X\os X'} = X\os X' \xrightarrow{e_X\os e_{X'}} I \os I \xrightarrow{\rho^{-1}_{I}} I \]
is a cocommutative comonoid. Consequently, $\mathrm{C}[\cX]$ is a symmetric monoidal category and the monoidal structure is given by the categorical product. Thus, $\mathrm{C}[\cX]$ is a cartesian category.
\end{lemma}

Further, this extends canonically to strong symmetric monoidal functors and monoidal transformations. While the original Fox theorem is only an adjunction between categories, it is well-known that it easily extends to include transformations.

\begin{lemma}{\cite{Fox_1976}}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Given a strong symmetric monoidal functor $F\c\cX\rarr\cY$ and a cocommutative comonoid $\langle X, \Delta_{X}, e_{X}\rangle$ in $\cX$, the triple $\langle F(X), \Delta_{F(X)}, e_{F(X)}\rangle$ defined by
\[ \Delta_{F(X)} = F(X) \xrightarrow{F(\Delta_{X})} F(X\os X) \xrightarrow{{m^{-1}_\os}_{X, X}} F(X) \os F(X)\] \[e_{F(X)} = F(X) \xrightarrow{F(t_{X})} F(I) \xrightarrow{m^{-1}_{I}} I \]
is a cocommutative comonoid. As such, $F$ canonically extends to a cartesian functor between the categories of cocommutative comonoids $\mathrm{C}[F]\c\mathrm{C}[\cX]\rarr\mathrm{C}[\cY]$.

\item Given a monoidal transformation between strong symmetric monoidal functors \\ $\alpha\c F\Rarr G\c \cX\rarr\cY$ and a cocommutative comonoid $\langle X, \Delta_{X}, e_{X}\rangle$ in $\cX$, the morphism \mbox{$\alpha_X\c F(X)\rarr G(X)$} is a comonoid morphism. As such, $\alpha$ canonically extends to cartesian transformation $\mathrm{C}[\alpha]\c \mathrm{C}[F]\Rarr \mathrm{C}[G]$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{lemma}

We are finally ready state Fox's theorem:

\begin{theorem}{\cite{Fox_1976}}\label{thm:Foxthm}
The functor $\mathrm{C}[-]\c\SMC\rarr\CART$, mapping a symmetric monoidal category to its category of cocommutative comonoids and comonoid morphisms, is right adjoint to the inclusion functor $\inc\c\mathbf{CART}\rarr\mathbf{SMC}$. 
\end{theorem}

An incredibly useful corollary is the following characterization of cartesian categories:
\begin{corollary}{\cite{Fox_1976}}\label{cor:SMC_cartesian_iff}
A symmetric monoidal category \cX\ is cartesian if and only if it is isomorphic to its category of cocommutative comonoids $\mathrm{C}[\cX]$. 
\end{corollary}

This previous result can be unwrapped as a statement about the existence of certain natural transformations, as was stated in \cite{Heunen_Vicary_2019}.

\begin{corollary}{\cite[Thm 4.28]{Heunen_Vicary_2019}}\label{cor:char_cartesian_cat}
A symmetric monoidal category \cX\ is cartesian if and only if there are natural transformations
\begin{align*}
&e\c 1_{\ccX}\Rarr I\c\cX\rarr\cX  &e_{A}\c A\rarr I \\
&\Delta\c 1_{\ccX}\Rarr copy_{\ccX};\os\c\cX\rarr\cX & \Delta_{A}\c A\rarr A\os A
\end{align*}
such that, $\forall A\in\cX, (A, \Delta_{A}, e_{A})$ is a cocommutative comonoid and, $\forall A,B\in\cX$,
\begin{align*}
&e_{A\os B} = (e_{A}\os e_{B}); \rho_{I}^{-1} &e_{I} = 1_{I} \\
&\Delta_{A\os B} = (\Delta_{A}\os\Delta_{B}); s_{A, A, B, B} & \Delta_{I} = \rho_{I}
\end{align*}
\end{corollary}

Of course, there is a dual statement for cocartesian categories, by taking the category of commutative monoids and monoid morphisms in a symmetric monoidal category. We quickly outline these definitions as they will be used throughout the work.

\begin{definition}
Let \cX\ denote a monoidal category.
\begin{itemize}
\item A {\bf monoid} in \cX\ is a comonoid in $\cX^{op}$, i.e. a triple $\langle X, \nabla_X, u_X\rangle$ of an object $A$ in \cX\ equipped with two morphisms, the {\bf multiplication} and the {\bf unit}
\[ \nabla_X\c X\os X\rarr X \qquad\qquad u_X\c I\rarr X \]
satisfying 
\begin{equation}\begin{gathered}\label{cc:monoid_assoc}
\xymatrixrowsep{1.75pc}\xymatrixcolsep{2.75pc}\xymatrix@L=0.5pc{ 
(X\os X)\os X \ar[r]^-{\alpha_{X,X,X}} \ar[d]_-{\nabla_X \os 1_X}& X\os (X\os X) \ar[r]^-{1_X\os\nabla_X}  & X\os X\ar[d]^-{\nabla_X}\\ 
X\os X\ar[rr]_-{\nabla_X} && X
}
\end{gathered}\end{equation}
%
\begin{equation}\begin{gathered}\label{cc:monoid_units}
\xymatrixrowsep{1.75pc}\xymatrixcolsep{1.75pc}\xymatrix@L=0.5pc{ 
X\ar@{=}[rrd]\ar[r]^-{\rho_X}\ar[d]_-{\lambda_X} & X\os I \ar[r]^-{1_X\os u_X} & X\os X\ar[d]^-{\nabla_X} \\
I\os X\ar[r]_-{u_X\os 1_X} & X\os X\ar[r]_-{\nabla_X} & X
}
\end{gathered}\end{equation}

\item A {\bf monoid morphism} $f\c \langle X, \nabla_{X}, e_X\rangle\rarr\langle Y, \nabla_{Y}, u_Y\rangle$ is a morphism $f\c X\rarr Y$ in \cX\ such that 
\begin{equation}\begin{gathered}\label{cc:monoid_morphism}
\xymatrixrowsep{1.75pc}\xymatrixcolsep{2.75pc}\xymatrix{ 
        X\os X\ar[r]^-{f\os f}\ar[d]_-{\nabla_X} & Y\os Y\ar[d]^-{\nabla_Y} & & I\\
        X\ar[r]_-{f} & Y & X\ar[r]_-{f}\ar[ru]^-{u_X} & Y\ar[u]_-{u_Y}
}
\end{gathered}\end{equation}
\item If \cX\ is a symmetric monoidal category, a monoid $\langle X, \Delta_X, e_X\rangle$ is {\bf commutative} if 
\begin{equation}\begin{gathered}\label{cc:commutative_monoid}
\xymatrixrowsep{1.75pc}\xymatrixcolsep{2.75pc}\xymatrix{ 
X\os X\ar[r]^-{\nabla_X}\ar[d]_-{\sigma_{X,X}} & X\\
X\os X\ar[ru]_-{\nabla_X}
}
\end{gathered}\end{equation}

\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\section{Linearly Distributive Categories}\label{sec:LDC}

Girard introduced linear logic in 1987 to capture the constructive nature of intuitionistic logic and the inherent dualities of classical logic \cite{Girard_1987}. The categorical semantics for linear logic were subsequently explored by Seely, who identified that the appropriate framework were $*$-autonomous categories \cite{Seely_1989}, previously introduced by Barr \cite{Barr_1979}. In this context, the monoidal product is the multiplicative conjunction, while the involutive functor $*$ is negation. \\

Linearly distributive categories (LDC) were introduced by Cockett and Seely as an alternative framework for the semantics of linear logic to $*$-autonomous categories. \cite{Cockett_Seely_1997_LDC}. Unlike $*$-autonomous categories, LDCs take multiplicative conjunction and disjunction as primitive. Therefore, they consist of two monoidal structures, which interact via linear distributivities.  Monoidal products connected through negation and de Morgan duality inherently possess such a linear distribution. Thus, $*$-autonomous categories are LDCs. However, there are significant examples of LDCs without negation, making them more general. \\

The current work generalizes the Fox theorem to the context of LDCs in order to characterize the notable subclass of cartesian LDCs (CLDC). In this section, we introduce all the necessary background on LDCs needed, as developed by co-authors Cockett and Seely in the series of papers \cite{Cockett_Seely_1997_LDC}, \cite{Cockett_Seely_1997} and \cite{Cockett_Seely_1999}. 

\subsection{Definition, Negation, Mix and Examples}  \hfill\

\begin{definition}\cite[Sec 2.1]{Cockett_Seely_1997_LDC}\label{def:LDC}
A {\bf linearly distributive category} $(\bX,\ot,\top,\op,\bot)$ consists of:
\begin{itemize}
\item a category $(\bX, ;, 1_A)$,

\item a {\bf tensor} monoidal structure $(\bX, \ot, \top)$
	\begin{itemize}
		\item the {\bf tensor} product and {\bf top} unit functor 
		\begin{align*}
		& \ot\c\bX\times\bX\rarr\bX & \top\c{\bf 1}\rarr\bX
		\end{align*} 
            \vspace{-1.5\baselineskip}
		\item the $\ot$-associator, right $\ot$-unitor, and left $\ot$-unitor natural isomorphisms
		\begin{align*}
		& \alpha_\ot\c (\ot\times1_{\bbX});\ot\Rarr (1_{\bbX}\times\ot);\ot &{\alpha_\ot}_{A,B,C}\c (A\ot B)\ot C\rarr A\ot (B\ot C) \\
		& u_\ot^R\c 1_{\bbX}\Rarr 1_{\bbX}\ot \top & {u_\ot^R}_{A}\c A\rarr A\ot \top \\
		& u_\ot^L\c 1_{\bbX}\Rarr \top\ot 1_{\bbX} & {u_\ot^L}_{A}\c A\rarr \top\ot A
		\end{align*}
		such that $(\bX, \ot, \top, \alpha_\ot, u_\ot^R, u_\ot^L)$ is a monoidal category,
	\end{itemize}

\item a {\bf par} monoidal structure $(\bX, \op, \bot)$
	\begin{itemize}
		\item the {\bf par} product and {\bf bottom} unit functor 
		\begin{align*}
		& \op\c\bX\times\bX\rarr\bX & \bot\c{\bf 1}\rarr\bX
		\end{align*} 
             \vspace{-1.5\baselineskip}
		\item the $\op$-associator, right $\op$-unitor, and left $\op$-unitor natural isomorphisms
		\begin{align*}
		& \alpha_\op\c (\ot\times1_{\bbX});\ot\Rarr (1_{\bbX}\times\ot);\ot &  {\alpha_\op}_{A,B,C}\c A\op(B\op C)\rarr (A\op B)\op C \\
		& u_\op^R\c 1_{\bbX}\op \bot\Rarr 1_{\bbX}\ & {u_\op^R}_{A}\c A\op\bot\rarr A \\
		& u_\op^L\c \bot\op 1_{\bbX}\Rarr 1_{\bbX} & {u_\op^L}_{A}\c\bot\op A\rarr A
		\end{align*}
		such that $(\bX, \op, \bot, {\alpha_\op}^{-1}, {u_\op^R}^{-1}, {u_\op^L}^{-1})$ is a monoidal category,
	\end{itemize}	

