If the LDC is symmetric, the above lemma was then utilized to determine that, if $\bot$ has a $\ot$-comonoid structure or if $\top$ has a $\op$-monoid structure, the LDC is mix.

\begin{theorem}\cite[Thm 3.11]{Fuhrmann_Pym_2007}\label{thm:prove_mix}
Every symmetric linearly distributive category with a $\ot$-comonoid 
\[ \Delta_\bot\c \bot\rarr\bot\ot\bot \qquad e_\bot\c \bot\rarr \top\]
is mix with $m=e_\bot$. Dually, every symmetric linearly distributive category with a $\op$-monoid 
\[ \nabla_\top\c \top\op\top\rarr\top \qquad u_\top\c \bot\rarr\top \]
is mix with $m=u_\top$. 
\end{theorem}

There is a stronger version of the nullary MIX rule, which often holds in categorical models: $\bot \dashv\vdash \top$. In this case, $\bot\cong\top$ and we call such LDCs isomix.

\begin{definition}{\cite[Def 6.5]{Cockett_Seely_1997}}
An linearly distributive category is {\bf isomix} if it is mix and $m\c\bot\rarr\top$ is an isomorphism.
\end{definition}

In fact, we do not need to check \eqref{cc:mix_LDC} if the two monoidal units are isomorphic:

\begin{lemma}{\cite[Lem 6.6]{Cockett_Seely_1997}}
A linearly distributive category where $\top\cong\bot$ is isomix.
\end{lemma}

We can also consider LDCs where the tensor monoidal product and the parr monoidal product are isomorphic to one another.

\begin{definition}\cite[Sec 2.3]{Cockett_Comfort_Srinivasan_2021}\label{def:compact_LDC}
A linearly distributive category is {\bf compact} if it is isomix and the mix maps are isomorphisms. 
\end{definition}\

While LDCs make multiplicative conjunction and disjunction primitive, we also want to be able to consider negation in this categorical framework and have it recover $*$-autonomous categories. We shall present the negation in terms of complemented objects.

\begin{definition}\cite[Def A.5]{Cockett_Seely_1999}
Consider a linearly distributive category \bX. The objects $A, A^c$ in \bX\ form a complementation pair if there are morphisms 
\[ \gamma\c A\ot A^c \rarr \bot \qquad\qquad \tau\c \top\rarr A^c\op A\]
such that 
\begin{equation}\label{cc:complement_LDC}
\begin{gathered}\xymatrixrowsep{1.75pc}\xymatrixcolsep{2.75pc}\xymatrix{
A\ar[d]_-{{u^R_\ot}_A}\ar[r]^-{1_A} & A & A^c\ar[d]_-{{u^L_\ot}_A^c}\ar[r]^-{1_{A^c}} & A^c\\
A\ot \top\ar[d]_-{1_A\ot \tau} & \bot\ot A\ar[u]_-{{u^L_\op}_A} & \top\ot A^c\ar[d]_-{\tau\ot 1_{A^c}}  & A^c\op \bot\ar[u]_-{{u^R_\op}_{A^c}} \\
A\ot (A^c\op A)\ar[r]_-{\delta^L_{A,A^c,A}} & (A\ot A^c)\op A\ar[u]_-{\gamma\op 1_A} & (A^c\op A)\ot A^c\ar[r]_-{\delta^R_{A^c,A,A^c}} & A^c\op (A\ot A^c)\ar[u]_-{1_{A^c}\op \gamma}
}
\end{gathered}\end{equation}
The pair is denoted by $(A,A^c, \gamma, \tau)$. $A$ is said to be the left complement and $A^c$ is the right complement.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}\cite[Def 4.1, 4.3]{Cockett_Seely_1997_LDC}\label{def:SLDC_negation}
A linearly distributive category has {\bf negation} if there are object function $(-)^\perp$ and ${^\perp(-)}$, together with the following parametrized family of maps
\begin{align*}
    &\gamma_A^R\c A\ot A^\perp \rarr \bot & \tau^R_A\c \top\rarr A\op {^\perp A}\\
    &\gamma_A^L\c {^\perp A}\ot A \rarr \bot & \tau^L_A\c \top\rarr A^\perp \op A
\end{align*}
such $(A, A^\perp, \gamma^R_A, \tau^L_A)$ and $({^\perp A}, A, \gamma^L_A, \tau^R_A)$ form a complementation pairs. \\

A symmetric linear distributive category has {\bf negation} if additionally $(-)^\perp = {^\perp(-)}$ and
\[ \gamma_A^L = A^\perp \ot A \xrightarrow{{\sigma_\ot}_{A^\perp, A}} A\ot A^\perp \xrightarrow{\gamma^R_A} \bot \qquad\qquad \tau^L_A = \top \xrightarrow{\tau^R_A} A\op A^\perp \xrightarrow{{\sigma_\op}_{A,A^\perp}} A^\perp\op A\]
\end{definition}

We shall only consider the symmetric case. Notice that the above definition does not require $(-)^\perp$ to act on morphisms, nor does it ask for the many natural isomorphisms which connect the multiplicative connectives and negation together. These are in fact a consequence of the above structure, and excluding them from the definition allows it to be much simpler to verify in examples. We outline in the following lemma how $(-)^\perp$ defines a functor and some of the isomorphisms we will need in Section \ref{sec:adding_negation}. 

\begin{lemma}\cite[Lem 4.4]{Cockett_Seely_1997_LDC}
In a symmetric linearly distributive category with negation $(\bX, \ot, \top, \op, \bot)$, the object function $(-)^\perp$ is a contravariant functor, which acts on morphisms as follows. Given $f\c A\rarr B$ in \bX, $f^\perp\c B^\perp\rarr A^\perp$ is defined by
\begin{equation*}
\begin{gathered}\xymatrixrowsep{1.75pc}\xymatrixcolsep{3.75pc}\xymatrix{
B^\perp\ar[r]^-{{u^R_\ot}_{B^\perp}}\ar[dd]_-{f^\perp} & B^\perp\ot \top \ar[r]^-{1_{B^\perp}\ot \tau^R_A} & B^\perp \ot (A\op A^\perp)\ar[d]^-{1_{B^\perp}\ot (f\op 1_{A^\perp})} \\
&& B^\perp \ot (B\op A^\perp)\ar[d]^-{\delta^L_{B^\perp, B, A^\perp}}\\
A^\perp & \bot\op A^\perp \ar[l]^{{u^L_\op}_{A^\perp}}& (B^\perp\ot B)\op A^\perp\ar[l]^-{{\gamma^L_B}\op 1_{A^\perp}} 
}
\end{gathered}\end{equation*}
and we have the following adjunctions
\begin{align*}
    & A\ot - \dashv A^\perp \op - & A^\perp \ot - \dashv A\op - \\
    & -\ot B \dashv -\op B^\perp & -\ot B^\perp \dashv -\op B
\end{align*}
corresponding to the following bijections
\begin{align*}
    & \frac{A\ot B\rarr B}{B\rarr A^\perp \op C} & \frac{A^\perp\ot B\rarr C}{B\rarr A\op C} \\
& \frac{A\ot B\rarr C}{A\rarr C\op B^\perp} & \frac{A\ot B^\perp\rarr C}{A\rarr C\op B}
\end{align*}
Moreover, there are  following invertible morphisms and natural isomorphisms
\begin{align*}
    &\beta_\top\c \bot^\perp\rarr \top &  \epsilon_{A,B}\c (A\op B)^\perp\rarr B^\perp\ot A^\perp\\ \\
    &\chi_{\bot}\c \bot \rarr \top^\perp & \phi_{A,B}\c A^\perp \op B^\perp \rarr (B\ot A)^\perp 
\end{align*}
\end{lemma}

\begin{definition}
A {\em $*$-autonomous category} is a symmetric monoidal category $(\bX, \ot, \top)$ equipped with a full and faithful functor 
\[ (-)^\perp\c \bX^{op}\rarr \bX\]
such that there is a natural isomorphism $\bX(A,\ot B, C^\perp)\cong \bX(A, (B\ot C)^\perp)$.
\end{definition}

