The proof that $A\op B$ is a bicommutative medial bimonoid follows similarly. 
\end{proof}

Let us for a moment consider our most important class of symmetric medial LDCs, the cartesian LDCs, and discuss the medial bimonoids present in these cases.

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:canonical_medial_bimonoids_in_CLDC}
Every object $A$ in a cartesian linearly distributive category \bX\ has a canonical unique bicommutative medial bimonoid structure 
\[ \langle A, \langle 1_A, 1_A\rangle\c A\rarr A\times A, t_A\c A\rarr \bone, [1_A, 1_A]\c A+A\rarr A, b_A\c \bzero\rarr A\rangle \]
and every arrow $f\c A\rarr B$ in \bX\ is a medial bimonoid morphism with respect to these canonical structures.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma \ref{lem:unique_comonoid_cartesian_cat}, it is clear $\langle A, \langle 1_A, 1_A\rangle, t_A\rangle$ is the unique cocommutative $\times$-comonoid structure on $A$ and, dually, $\langle A, [1_A, 1_A], b_A\rangle$ is the unique commutative $+$-monoid structure on $A$. The proof the structure maps equally satisfy the additional medial bimonoid coherence conditions is a simple exercises using the canonical medial structure of a cartesian LDC and universal properties. Once more, by Lemma \ref{lem:unique_comonoid_cartesian_cat} and its dual, every arrow is a medial bimonoid morphism with respect to these structures. 
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}
Let \bX\ be a symmetric medial linearly distributive category. Define $\B[\bX]$ to be the category of bicommutative medial bimonoids and medial bimonoid morphisms in \bX. 
\end{definition}

The fact that this is in fact a category is an easy exercise, as the category of commutative $\op$-monoids and of cocommutative $\ot$-comonoids in \bX\ would themselves be categories. The above propositions will be the key to showing that $\B[\bX]$ is more than a simple category, but is in fact a symmetric LDC.

\begin{lemma}
Let \bX\ be a symmetric medial linearly distributive category, then $\B[\bX]$ is a symmetric linearly distributive category, with $\ot$ and $\op$ monoidal structures as defined in Propositions \ref{prop:top_bottom_medial_bimonoids} and \ref{prop:constructing_medial_bimonoids}.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
It is sufficient to show that given medial bimonoid morphisms $f\c A\rarr B$ and $g\c A'\rarr B'$, $f\ot g\c A\ot B\rarr A'\ot B'$ and  $f\op g\c A\op B\rarr A'\op B'$ are medial bimonoid morphisms, and that all the LDC structure maps are bimonoid morphisms as well to prove that $\B[\bX]$ is a LDC, as composition is inherited directly from \bX. \\

Firstly, it follows from Lemma \ref{lem:constructing_comonoids} that $f\ot g$ is $\ot$-comonoid morphism, thus we need only show it is also a $\op$-monoid morphism, i.e. satisfies \eqref{cc:monoid_morphism}, which is proved by the following commuting diagrams:
\begin{equation*}
\xymatrixrowsep{2.75pc}\xymatrixcolsep{3.75pc}\xymatrix@L=0.5pc{
(A\ot B)\op (A\ot B)\ar[r]^-{(f\ot g)\op (f \ot g)}\ar[d]_-{\mu_{A,B,A,B}}\ar@{}[rd]|{(\nat)} & (A'\ot B')\op (A'\ot B')\ar[d]^-{\mu_{A',B',A',B'}} && \bot\\
(A\op A)\ot (B\op B)\ar[r]^-{(f\op f)\ot (g\op g)}\ar[d]_-{\nabla_A\ot \nabla_B}\ar@{}[rd]|{\eqref{cc:monoid_morphism}} & (A'\op A')\ot (B' \op B')\ar[d]^-{\nabla_{A'}\ot \nabla_{B'}} & \bot\ot\bot \ar@{=}[r]\ar[ru]^-{\Delta_\bot}\ar@{}[rd]|{\eqref{cc:monoid_morphism}} & \bot\ot\bot\ar[u]_-{\Delta_\bot}\\
A\ot B\ar[r]_-{f\ot g} & A'\ot B' & A\ot B\ar[r]_-{f\ot g}\ar[u]^-{u_A\ot u_B} & A'\ot B'\ar[u]_-{u_{A'}\ot u_{B'}}
}
\end{equation*}

\noindent The proof that $f\op g$ is a medial bimonoid morphism follows similarly. \\

To show that the left linear distributivity is a bimonoid morphism, we need to show it is a $\ot$-comonoid morphism and a $\op$-monoid morphism, which follows respectively from the commuting diagrams below:

\begin{equation*}
\xymatrixrowsep{2.75pc}\xymatrixcolsep{3.75pc}\xymatrix@L=0.5pc{
A\ot (B\op C)\ar[r]^-{\delta^L_{A,B,C}}\ar[d]_-{\Delta_A\ot (\Delta_B\op \Delta_C)}\ar@{}[rd]|{(\nat)} & (A\ot B)\op C\ar[d]^-{(\Delta_A\ot \Delta_B)\op \Delta_C} \\
(A\ot A) \ot ((B\ot B)\op (C\ot C))\ar[d]_-{1_{A\ot A} \ot \mu_{B,C,B,C}}\ar[r]_-{\delta^L_{A\ot A, B\ot B, C\ot C}} \ar@{}[rdd]|{\eqref{cc:interaction_canonical_flip_medial_maps_linear_dist}} & ((A\ot A)\ot (B\ot B)) \op (C\ot C)\ar[d]^-{\tau^\ot_{A,A,B,B} \op 1_{C\ot C}} \\
(A\ot A) \ot ((B\op C)\ot (B\op C))\ar[d]_-{\tau^\ot_{A,A,B\ot C, B\ot C}} & ((A\ot B)\ot (A\ot B)) \op (C\ot C)\ar[d]^-{\mu_{A\ot B, A\ot B, C, C}}\\
(A\ot (B\op C)) \ot (A\ot (B\op C))\ar[r]_-{\delta^L_{A,B,C}\ot \delta^L_{A,B,C}} & ((A\ot B)\op C) \ot ((A\ot B)\op C) 
}
\end{equation*}
%
\begin{equation*}
\xymatrixrowsep{2.75pc}\xymatrixcolsep{3.75pc}\xymatrix@L=0.5pc{
A\ot (B\op C)\ar[r]^-{e_A\ot (e_B\op e_C)}\ar[d]_-{\delta^L_{A,B,C}}\ar@{}[rd]|{(\nat)} & \top\ot(\top\op\top)\ar[r]^-{1_\top \ot \nabla_\top}\ar[d]_-{\delta^L_{\top,\top,\top}}^-{\,\,\eqref{cc:unit_lineardist}}\ar[rd]^-{{u^L_\ot}^{-1}_{\top\op\top}} & \top\ot\top \ar[r]^-{{u_\ot}^{-1}_\top}\ar@{}[d]|{(\nat)}& \top \\
(A\ot B)\op C\ar[r]_-{(e_A\ot e_B)\op e_C} & (\top\ot\top)\op\top\ar[r]_-{{u_\ot}^{-1}_\top \op 1_\top} & \top\op\top \ar[ru]_-{\nabla_\top}
}
\end{equation*}
%
\begin{equation*}
\xymatrixrowsep{2.75pc}\xymatrixcolsep{3.75pc}\xymatrix@L=0.5pc{
(A\ot (B\op C))\op (A\ot (B\op C))\ar[r]^-{\delta^L_{A,B,C}\op \delta^L_{A,B,C}}\ar[d]_-{\mu_{A, B\op C, A, B\op C}}\ar@{}[rdd]|{\eqref{cc:interaction_canonical_flip_medial_maps_linear_dist}}  & ((A\ot B)\op C)\op ((A\ot B)\op C)\ar[d]^-{\tau^\op_{A\ot B, C, A\ot B, C}} \\
(A\op A)\ot ((B\op C)\op (B\op C))\ar[d]_-{1_{A\op A}\ot \tau^\op_{B,C,B,C}} & ((A\ot B)\op (A\ot B)) \op (C\op C)\ar[d]^-{\mu_{A,B,A,B}\op 1_{C\op C}} \\
(A\op A) \ot ((B\op B)\ot (B\op C))\ar[d]_-{\nabla_A\ot (\nabla_B\op \nabla_C)}\ar[r]^-{\delta^L_{A\op A, B\op B, C\op C}}\ar@{}[rd]|{(\nat)} & ((A\op A)\ot (B\op B)) \op (C\op C)\ar[d]^-{(\nabla_A\ot \nabla_B)\op \nabla_C} \\
A\ot (B\op C) \ar[r]_-{\delta^L_{A,B,C}}& (A\ot B)\op C
}
\end{equation*}
%
\begin{equation*}
\xymatrixrowsep{2.75pc}\xymatrixcolsep{3.75pc}\xymatrix@L=0.5pc{
\bot\ar[r]^-{\Delta_\bot}\ar[rd]_-{{u_\op}^{-1}_\bot} & \bot\ot\bot\ar[r]^-{1_\bot\ot {u_\op}_\bot^{-1}}\ar[rd]^-{{u^R_\op}_{\bot\ot\bot}^{-1}}\ar@{}[d]|{(\nat)} & \bot\ot(\bot\op\bot)\ar[r]^-{u_A\ot (u_B\op u_C)}\ar[d]^-{\delta^L_{\bot,\bot,\bot}}_-{\eqref{cc:unit_lineardist}}\ar@{}[rd]|{(\nat)} & A\ot (B\op C)\ar[d]^-{\delta^L_{A,B,C}} \\
& \bot\op\bot\ar[r]_-{\Delta_\bot\op 1_\bot} & (\bot\ot\bot)\op \bot \ar[r]_-{(u_A\ot u_B)\op u_C}& (A\ot B)\op C
}
\end{equation*}

\vspace{\baselineskip}
The proofs that the other structure maps, i.e. the associators, unitors and braidings for the tensor and par monoidal products, follow similarly, involving only coherence conditions from the duoidal structure of \bX. They are left up to the reader. 
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:BX_CLDC}
Let \bX\ be a symmetric medial linearly distributive category, then $\B[\bX]$ is a cartesian linearly distributive category.    
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
We will prove this by showing $\B[\bX]$ satisfies the characterization in Proposition \ref{prop:char_CLDC}. \\

Given a bicommutative medial bimonoid $\langle A, \Delta_A, e_A, \nabla_A, u_A\rangle$, then the coherence conditions in \eqref{cc:medial_bimonoid} ensure that the four bimonoid maps $\Delta_A$, $e_A$, $\nabla_A$ and $u_A$ are themselves medial bimonoid morphisms. These give exactly the component maps of the four necessary natural transformations. These in fact determine natural transformations as the naturality squares correspond exactly to the conditions for every map to be a bimonoid morphism. \\

The conditions that the component maps form comonoid and monoid structures is immediate by the definition of a bicommutative medial bimonoid and the remaining coherence conditions are satisfied as the correspond to exactly to the construction of the tensor and par monoidal structures in $\B[\bX]$ as outlined in Propositions \ref{prop:top_bottom_medial_bimonoids} and \ref{prop:constructing_medial_bimonoids}.
\end{proof}

And with this theorem, the first part of the linearly distributive Fox theorem has been proven. It remains only to show that the $\B[-]$ construction extends canonically to the strong symmetric medial linear functors and the medial linear transformation, giving a right adjoint to the inclusion functor outlined in Proposition \ref{prop:inclusion_CLDC_SMLDC}. \\

It is also important to note that negation is preserved by the $\B[-]$ construction. 

\begin{proposition}
Let \bX\ be a symmetric medial linearly distributive category with negation, then $\B[\bX]$ is a cartesian linearly distributive category with negation, and therefore a bounded distributive lattice.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
As \bX\ is a symmetric medial LDC with negation, there is a full and faithful functor $(-)^\perp\c \bX^{op}\rarr \bX$ such that $\bX(A\ot B, C^\perp)\cong \bX(A, (B\op C)^\perp)$ defined in , in other words \bX\ is a $*$-autonomous category. We shall show that $(-)^\perp$ extends to a functor on $\B[\bX]$, meaning $\B[\bX]$ is a $*$-autonomous category whose monoidal product is cartesian. Then, by Joyal's paradox, it is a bounded distributive lattice. \\

Consider a bicommutative medial bimonoid $\langle A, \Delta_A, e_A, \nabla_A, u_A\rangle$, then define \\$\langle A^\perp, \Delta_{A^\perp}, e_{A^\perp}, \nabla_{A^\perp}, u_{A^\perp}\rangle$ by 
\begin{align*}
    &\Delta_{A^\perp} = A^\perp \xrightarrow{(\nabla_A)^\perp} (A\op A)^\perp \xrightarrow{\epsilon_{A,A}} A^\perp \ot A^\perp & e_{A^\perp} = A^\perp \xrightarrow{(u_A)^\perp} \bot^\perp \xrightarrow{\beta_\top} \top \\
    & \nabla_{A^\perp} = A^\perp\op A^\perp \xrightarrow{\phi_{A,A}} (A\ot A)^\perp \xrightarrow{(\Delta_A)^\perp} A^\perp & u_{A^\perp} = \bot \xrightarrow{\chi_{\bot}} \top^\perp \xrightarrow{(e_A)^\perp} A^\perp 
\end{align*}

This is a bicommutative medial bimonoid as follows. \\

$\langle A^\perp, \Delta_{A^\perp}, e_{A^\perp}\rangle$ is a cocommutative $\ot$-comonoid as:
\begin{align*}
   \Delta_{A^\perp}; (1_{A^\perp}\ot e_{A^\perp}) &=  (\nabla_A)^\perp; \epsilon_{A,A}; (1_{A^\perp}\ot (u_A)^\perp); (1_{A^\perp}\ot \beta_\top) &\text{by definition} \\
   &= (\nabla_A)^\perp; (u_A\op 1_A)^\perp; \epsilon_{\bot,A}; (1_{A^\perp}\ot \beta_\top) &\text{by naturality of $\epsilon$}\\
   &= ((u_A\op 1_A);\nabla_A)^\perp; \epsilon_{\bot,A}; (1_{A^\perp}\ot \beta_\top) &\text{by functoriality of $(-)^\perp$}\\
   &= ({u^A_\ot}_\bot)^\perp; \epsilon_{\bot,A}; (1_{A^\perp}\ot \beta_\top) &\text{as $A$ is a medial bimonoid} \\
   &= {u^R_\ot}_{A^\perp} &\text{by $(*)$}
\end{align*}
\begin{align*}
   &\Delta_{A^\perp}; (\Delta_{A^\perp}\ot 1_{A^\perp}); {\alpha_\ot}_{A^\perp, A^\perp, A^\perp} \\
   &= (\nabla_A)^\perp; \epsilon_{A,A}; ((\nabla_A)^\perp \ot 1_{A^\perp}); (\epsilon_{A,A}\ot 1_{A^\perp}); {\alpha_\ot}_{A^\perp, A^\perp, A^\perp}  &\text{by definition}\\
   &= (\nabla_A)^\perp; (1_A\op \nabla_A)^\perp; \epsilon_{A, A\op A};  (\epsilon_{A,A}\ot 1_{A^\perp}); {\alpha_\ot}_{A^\perp, A^\perp, A^\perp} &\text{by naturality of $\epsilon$}\\
   &= ((1_A\op \nabla_A); \nabla_A)^\perp; \epsilon_{A, A\op A};  (\epsilon_{A,A}\ot 1_{A^\perp}); {\alpha_\ot}_{A^\perp, A^\perp, A^\perp} &\text{by functoriality of $(-)^\perp$} \\
   &= ({\alpha_\op}_{A,A,A};(\nabla_A\op 1_A);\nabla_A)^\perp;  \epsilon_{A, A\op A}; \\
   &\qquad (\epsilon_{A,A}\ot 1_{A^\perp}); {\alpha_\ot}_{A^\perp, A^\perp, A^\perp} &\text{as $A$ is a medial bimonoid}\\
   &= (\nabla_A)^\perp; (\nabla_A\op 1_A)^\perp; ({\alpha_\op}_{A,A,A})^\perp; \\
   &\qquad \epsilon_{A, A\op A}; (\epsilon_{A,A}\ot 1_{A^\perp}); {\alpha_\ot}_{A^\perp, A^\perp, A^\perp} &\text{by functoriality of $(-)^\perp$}\\
   &= (\nabla_A)^\perp; (\nabla_A\op 1_A)^\perp; \epsilon_{A\op A, A}; (1_{A^\perp}\ot \epsilon_{A,A}) &\text{by $(*)$}\\
   &= (\nabla_A)^\perp; \epsilon_{A,A}; (1_{A^\perp}\ot (\nabla_A)^\perp); (1_{A^\perp}\ot \epsilon_{A,A}) &\text{by naturality of $\epsilon$}\\
   &= \Delta_{A^\perp}; (1_{A^\perp}\ot \Delta_{A^\perp}) &\text{by definition}
\end{align*}
\begin{align*}
    \Delta_{A^\perp}; {\sigma_\ot}_{A^\perp, A^\perp} &= (\nabla_A)^\perp; \epsilon_{A,A}; {\sigma_\ot}_{A^\perp,A^\perp} &\text{by definition}\\
    &= (\nabla_A)^\perp; ({\sigma_\op}_{A,A})^\perp; \epsilon_{A,A} &\text{by $(*)$}\\
    &= ({\sigma_\op}_{A,A}; \nabla_A)^\perp; \epsilon_{A,A} &\text{by functoriality of $(-)^\perp$}\\
    &= (\nabla_A)^\perp; \epsilon_{A,A} &\text{as $A$ is a medial bimonoid}\\
    &= \Delta_{A^\perp} &\text{by definition}
\end{align*}

The equalities marked with $(*)$ are incredibly long to show directly, but fairly straightforward if one uses the circuit diagram (or proof net) calculus introduced by Blute, Cockett, Seely and Trimble \cite{Blute_Cockett_Seely_Trimble_1996}.\\

Similarly, $\langle A, \nabla_{A^\perp}, u_{A^\perp}\rangle$ is a commutative $\op$-monoid. \\

The first coherence condition of \eqref{cc:medial_bimonoid} holds as:
\begin{align*}
    \nabla_{A^\perp}; \Delta_{A^\perp}&= \phi_{A,A}; (\Delta_A)^\perp; (\nabla_A)^\perp; \epsilon_{A,A}  &\text{by definition}\\
    & = \phi_{A,A}; (\nabla_A; \Delta_A)^\perp; \epsilon_{A,A}  &\text{by functoriality of $(-)^\perp$} \\
    &= \phi_{A,A}; ((\Delta_A\op \Delta_A); \mu_{A,A,A,A}; (\nabla_A\op \nabla_A))^\perp; \epsilon_{A,A}  &\text{as $A$ is a medial bimonoid} \\
    &= \phi_{A,A};(\nabla_A\op \nabla_A)^\perp (\mu_{A,A,A,A})^\perp; (\Delta_A\op \Delta_A)^\perp; \epsilon_{A,A}  &\text{by functoriality of $(-)^\perp$} \\
    &= ((\nabla_A)^\perp\ot (\nabla_A)^\perp); \phi_{A\op A, A\op A};\\
    &\qquad(\mu_{A,A,A,A})^\perp;\epsilon_{A\ot A,A\ot A};  ((\Delta_A)^\perp\ot (\Delta_A)^\perp)  &\text{by naturality of $\phi$ and $\epsilon$} \\
    &= ((\nabla_A)^\perp\ot (\nabla_A)^\perp); (\epsilon_{A,A}\ot \epsilon_{A,A}); \\
    & \qquad\mu_{A^\perp, A^\perp, A^\perp, A^\perp}; (\phi_{A,A}\op \phi_{A,A}); ((\Delta_A)^\perp\ot (\Delta_A)^\perp)  &\text{by \eqref{cc:medial_selfdual}} \\
    &=(\Delta_{A^\perp}\op \Delta_{A^\perp}); \mu_{A^\perp, A^\perp, A^\perp, A^\perp}; (\nabla_{A^\perp}\ot \nabla_{A^\perp})  &\text{by definition}\\
\end{align*}
The second coherence condition of \eqref{cc:medial_bimonoid} holds as:
\begin{align*}
    u_{A^\perp}; e_{A^\perp} &= \chi_{\bot}; (e_A)^\perp; (u_A)^\perp; \beta_\top &\text{by definition}\\
    & = \chi_\bot; (u_A; e_A)^\perp; \beta_\top &\text{by functoriality of $(-)^\perp$} \\
    &= \chi_\bot;(m)^\perp; \beta_\top &\text{as $A$ is a medial bimonoid} \\
    &= m &\text{by \eqref{cc:m_dual}}
\end{align*}
The third coherence condition of \eqref{cc:medial_bimonoid} holds as:
\begin{align*}
    \nabla_{A^\perp}; e_{A^\perp} &= \phi_{A,A}; (\Delta_A)^\perp; (u_A)^\perp; \beta_\top &\text{by definition}\\
    &= \phi_{A,A}; (u_A; \Delta_A)^\perp; \beta_\top &\text{by functoriality of $(-)^\perp$}\\
    &= \phi_{A,A}; (\Delta_\bot; (u_A\ot u_A))^\perp; \beta_\top &\text{as $A$ is a medial bimonoid}\\
    &= \phi_{A,A}; (u_A\ot u_A)^\perp; (\Delta_\bot)^\perp;  \beta_\top &\text{by functoriality of $(-)^\perp$}\\   
    &= (u_A)^\perp\op(u_A)^\perp; \phi_{\bot,\bot};(\Delta_\bot)^\perp; \beta_\top &\text{by naturality of $\phi$}\\   
    &= (u_A)^\perp\op(u_A)^\perp; (\beta_\top\op \beta_\top); \nabla_\top &\text{by \eqref{cc:delta_nabla_dual}}\\  
    &= (e_{A^\perp}\op e_{A^\perp}); \nabla_\top &\text{by definition}\\  
\end{align*}
The final coherence condition of \eqref{cc:medial_bimonoid} holds as:
\begin{align*}
    u_{A^\perp}; \Delta_{A^\perp} &= \chi_\bot; (e_A)^\perp; (\nabla_A)^\perp; \epsilon_{A,A} &\text{by definition}\\
    &= \chi_\bot; (\nabla_A; e_A)^\perp; \epsilon_{A,A} &\text{by functoriality of $(-)^\perp$}\\
    &= \chi_\bot; ((e_A\op e_A); \nabla_\top)^\perp; \epsilon_{A,A} &\text{as $A$ is a medial bimonoid}\\
    &= \chi_\bot; (\nabla_\top)^\perp; (e_A\op e_A)^\perp; \epsilon_{A,A} &\text{by functoriality of $(-)^\perp$}\\   
    &= \chi_\bot; (\nabla_\top)^\perp; \epsilon_{\top,\top}; ((e_A)^\perp\ot (e_A)^\perp) &\text{by naturality of $\epsilon$}\\   
    &=  \Delta_\bot; (\chi_\bot\ot \chi_\bot); ((e_A)^\perp\ot (e_A)^\perp) &\text{by \eqref{cc:delta_nabla_dual}}\\  
    &= \Delta_\bot; (u_{A^\perp}\op u_{A^\perp}) &\text{by definition}\\  
\end{align*}

Now, let $f\c \langle A, \Delta_A, e_A, \nabla_A, u_A\rangle \rarr \langle B, \Delta_B, e_B, \nabla_B, u_B\rangle$ be a medial bimonoid morphism, then $f^\perp\c B^\perp\rarr A^\perp$ is a $\ot$-comonoid morphism as follows.
\begin{align*}
\Delta_{B^\perp}; (f^\perp \ot f^\perp) &= (\nabla_B)^\perp; \epsilon_{B,B};(f^\perp \ot f^\perp) & \text{by definition} \\
&= (\nabla_B)^\perp; (f\op f)^\perp; \epsilon_{A,A} & \text{by naturality of $\epsilon$} \\
&= ((f\op f);\nabla_B)^\perp; \epsilon_{A,A} &\text{by functoriality of $(-)^\perp$}\\
&= (\nabla_A; f)^\perp; \epsilon_{A,A} &\text{as $f$ is a medial bimonoid morphism}\\
&= f^\perp; (\nabla_A)^\perp; \epsilon_{A,A} &\text{by functoriality of $(-)^\perp$}\\ 
&= f^\perp; \Delta_{A^\perp}
\end{align*}
\begin{align*}
f^\perp; e_{A^\perp} & = f^\perp; (u_A)^\perp; \beta_\top &\text{by definition} \\
&= (u_A;f)^\perp; \beta_\top & \text{by functoriality of $(-)^\perp$}\\
&= (u_B)^\perp;  \beta_\top &\text{as $f$ is a medial bimonoid morphism}\\
&= u_{B^\perp} &\text{by definition}
\end{align*}
Similarly, $f^\perp\c B^\perp\rarr A^\perp$ is a $\op$-monoid morphism and therefore a medial bimonoid morphism. 

\end{proof}

\subsection{The Right Adjoint 2-Functor \B[-]}\label{sec:right_adjoint}  \hfill\

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:medial_linear_functor_cartesian_linear_functor}
Consider a strong symmetric medial linear functor $F=(F_\ot, F_\op)\c \bX\rarr\bY$ between symmetric medial linearly distributive categories, then it canonically extends to a cartesian linear functor $\B[F]=(\B[F]_\ot, \B[F]_\op)\c \B[\bX]\rarr\B[\bY]$. 
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Consider a strong symmetric medial linear functor $F=(F_\ot, F_\op)\c\bX\rarr\bY$. Recall that 
\[(F_\ot,  m_{ \bot},m_\op, m_{ \top}^{-1}, m_\ot^{-1}),\,\, (F_\op, n_{\bot}^{-1}, n_\op^{-1}, n_{\top}, n_\op)\c(\bX, \op,\bot,\ot, \top)\rarr(\bY,\op,\bot,\ot,\top)\]
are bilax duoidal functors and therefore preserve medial bimonoids and medial bimonoid morphisms by Proposition \ref{prop:duoidal_functor_preserve}. \\

Thus, given a bicommutative medial bimonoid $\langle A, \Delta_{A}, e_{A}, \nabla_{A}, u_{A}\rangle$ in \bX, the object $F_\ot(A)$ equipped with the four maps
\begin{align*}
&\Delta_{F_\ot(A)} = F_\ot(A) \xrightarrow{F_\ot(\Delta_{A})} F_\ot(A\ot A) \xrightarrow{{m_\ot}^{-1}_{A,A}} F_\ot(A)\ot F_\ot(A) \\
&e_{F_\ot(A)} =F_\ot(A) \xrightarrow{F_\ot(e_{A})} F_\ot(\top) \xrightarrow{m_{\top}^{-1}} \top \\
&\nabla_{F_\ot(A)} = F_\ot(A)\op F_\ot(A) \xrightarrow{{m_\op}_{A,A}} F_\ot(A\op A) \xrightarrow{F_\ot(\nabla_{A})} F_\ot(A) \\
&u_{F_\ot(A)} = \bot \xrightarrow{m_{\bot}} F_\ot(\bot) \xrightarrow{F_\ot(u_{A})} F_\ot(A)
\end{align*}
and the object $F_\op(A)$ equipped with the four maps
\begin{align*}
&\Delta_{F_\op(A)} = F_\op(A) \xrightarrow{F_\op(\Delta_{A})} F_\op(A\ot A)\xrightarrow{{n_\ot}_{A,A}} F_\op(A)\ot F_\op(A) \\
& e_{F_\op(A)} = F_\op(A) \xrightarrow{F_\op(e_{A})} F_\op(\top) \xrightarrow{n_{\top}} \top\\
&\nabla_{F_\op(A)} = F_\op(A) \op F_\op(A) \xrightarrow{{n_\op}^{-1}_{A,A}} F_\op(A\op A) \xrightarrow{F_\op(\nabla_{A})} F_\op(A) \\
&u_{F_\op(A)} = \bot \xrightarrow{n^{-1}_{\bot}} F_\op(\bot) \xrightarrow{F_\op(u_{A})} F_\op(A)
\end{align*}
are bicommutative medial bimonoid in \bY. Moreover, given a medial bimonoid morphism \[ f\c \langle A, \Delta_A, e_A, \nabla_A, u_A\rangle \rarr \langle B, \Delta_B, e_B, \nabla_B, u_B\rangle\] in \bX, then \[ F_\ot(f)\c\langle F_\ot(A), \Delta_{F_\ot(A)}, e_{F_\ot(A)}, \nabla_{F_\ot(A)}, u_{F_\ot(A)}\rangle\rarr \langle F_\ot(B), \Delta_{F_\ot(B)}, e_{F_\ot(B)}, \nabla_{F_\ot(B)}, u_{F_\ot(B)}\rangle, \] \[ F_\op(f)\c \langle F_\op(A), \Delta_{F_\op(A)}, e_{F_\op(A)}, \nabla_{F_\op(A)}, u_{F_\op(A)}\rangle \rarr \langle F_\op(B), \Delta_{F_\op(B)}, e_{F_\op(B)}, \nabla_{F_\op(B)}, u_{F_\op(B)}\rangle\] are medial bimonoid morphisms in \bY. \\

As such, let $\B[F]_\ot\c\B[\bX] \rarr \B[\bY]$ denote the functor mapping bicommutative medial bimonoid on $A$ to the bicommutative medial bimonoid on $F_\ot(A)$ and mapping medial bimonoid morphism $f$ to $F_\ot(f)$. Similarly, let $\B[F]_\op\c\B[\bX] \rarr \B[\bY]$ denote the functor mapping bicommutative medial bimonoid on $A$ to the bicommutative medial bimonoid on $F_\op(A)$ and mapping medial bimonoid morphism $f$ to $F_\op(f)$.\\

Further, $\B[F]_\ot$ is a strong symmetric monoidal functor $(\B[\bX], \ot, \langle\top\rangle)\rarr (\B[\bY], \ot, \langle\top\rangle)$ when equipped with 
\begin{itemize}
    \item morphism $m_{\langle \top\rangle}\c \langle \top \rangle \rarr  \B[F]_\ot(\langle \top \rangle)$, defined to be $m_\top\c \top \rarr F_\ot(\top)$, and 
    \item natural transformation 
    \[{m_\ot}_{\langle A\rangle, \langle B\rangle}\c \B[F]_\ot(\langle A\rangle) \ot \B[F]_\ot(\langle B\rangle) \rarr \B[F]_\ot(\langle A\rangle \ot \langle B\rangle)\]
    defined to be ${m_\ot}_{A,B}\c F_\ot(A)\ot F_\ot(B) \rarr F_\ot(A\ot B)$
\end{itemize}
as $(F_\ot, m_\top, m_\ot)\c (\bX, \ot, \top)\rarr (\bY, \ot, \top)$ is a strong symmetric monoidal functor by definition of strong symmetric medial linear functor, and its structure morphisms are bimonoid morphisms as follows. \\

$m_\top\c \top\rarr F_\ot(\top)$ is a medial bimonoid morphism:
\begin{equation*}
\xymatrixrowsep{2.75pc}\xymatrixcolsep{1.75pc}\xymatrix@L=0.5pc{
\top\ar[rr]^-{m_\top}\ar[dd]_-{{u_\ot}_{\top}} \ar@{}[rd]|{(\nat)} & & F_\ot(\top)\ar[d]^-{F_\ot({u_\ot}_{\top})}\ar[dl]_-{{u^R_\ot}_{F_\ot(\top)}} & \top\ar[r]^-{1_\top}\ar[dd]_-{m_\top} & \top\\
& F_\ot(\top)\ot \top \ar[rd]^-{1_{F_\ot(\top)}\ot m_\top} \ar@{}[r]|{\eqref{cc:mon_functor_unit}} &  F_\ot(\top\ot \top)\ar[d]^-{{m_\ot^{-1}}_{\top,\top}} \\
\top\ot \top\ar[rr]_-{m_{\top}\ot m_{\top}}\ar[ru]_-{m_\top \ot 1_{F_\ot(\top)}} & & F_\ot(\top)\ot F_\ot(\top) & F_\ot(\top)\ar[ruu]_-{m_\top^{-1}}
}
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\xymatrixrowsep{1.75pc}\xymatrixcolsep{1.75pc}\xymatrix@L=0.5pc{
\top\op\top\ar[dd]_-{\nabla_\top}\ar[r]^-{m_\top\op m_\top} \ar@{}[rdd]|{\eqref{cc:F_ot_bilax} } & F_\ot(\top)\op F_\ot(\top)\ar[d]^-{{m_\op}_{\top,\top}} & \bot\ar[r]^-{m}\ar[d]_-{m_\bot} & \top\ar[dd]^-{m_\top}\\
& F_\ot(\top\op\top)\ar[d]^-{F_\ot(\nabla_\top)} & F_\ot(\bot)\ar[rd]_-{F_\ot(m)} \ar@{}[r]|{\qquad\eqref{cc:F_ot_bilax}} & \\
\top\ar[r]_-{m_\top} &F_\ot(\top) & & F_\ot(\top)
}
\end{equation*}
and ${m_\ot}_{A,B}\c F_\ot(A)\ot F_\ot(B) \rarr F_\ot(A\ot B)$ is a medial bimonoid morphism:
\begin{equation*}
\xymatrixrowsep{2.75pc}\xymatrixcolsep{1.75pc}\xymatrix@L=0.5pc{
F_\ot(A) \ot F_\ot(B)\ar[r]^-{{m_\ot}_{A,B}}\ar[d]_-{F_\ot(\Delta_A)\ot F_\ot(\Delta_B)}\ar@{}[rd]|{(\nat)} & F_\ot(A\ot B)\ar[d]^-{F_\ot(\Delta_A\ot \Delta_B)} \\
F_\ot(A\ot A) \ot F_\ot(B\ot B)\ar[r]^-{{m_\ot}_{A\ot A, B\ot B}}\ar[d]_-{{m_\ot}^{-1}_{A,A}\ot{m_\ot}^{-1}_{B,B}}\ar@{}[rd]|{\eqref{cc:strong_symmetric_monoidal_flip}} & F_\ot((A\ot A)\ot (B\ot B))\ar[d]^-{F_\ot(\tau^\ot_{A,A,B,B})} \\
(F_\ot(A)\ot F_\ot(A)) \ot (F_\ot(B)\ot F_\ot(B))\ar[d]_-{\tau^\ot_{F_\ot(A), F_\ot(A), F_\op(B), F_\op(B)}} & F_\ot((A\ot B)\ot (A\ot B))\ar[d]^-{{m_\ot}^{-1}_{A\ot B, A\ot B}} \\
(F_\ot(A)\ot F_\ot(B)) \ot (F_\ot(A)\ot F_\ot(B))\ar[r]_-{{m_\ot}_{A,B}\ot{m_\ot}_{A,B}} & F_\ot(A\ot B)\ot F_\ot(A\ot B) 
}
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\xymatrixrowsep{2.75pc}\xymatrixcolsep{1.75pc}\xymatrix@L=0.5pc{
F_\ot(A) \ot F_\ot (B)\ar[r]^-{F_\ot(e_A)\ot F_\ot(e_B)}\ar[dd]_-{{m_\ot}_{A,B}}\ar@{}[rdd]|{(\nat)} & F_\ot(\top)\ot F_\ot(\top)\ar[r]^-{m_\top^{-1}\ot m_\top^{-1}} \ar[dd]_-{{m_\ot}_{\top,\top}}\ar[rd]^-{1_{F_\ot(\top)}\ot m_\top^{-1}} & \top\ot \top\ar[r]^-{{u_\ot}_\top^{-1}} & \top\ar@{}[dl]|{(\nat)}\\
& \ar@{}[r]|{\eqref{cc:mon_functor_unit}}& F_\ot(\top)\ot\top\ar[d]_-{{u^R_\ot}^{-1}_{F_\ot(\top)}}\\
F_\ot(A\ot B)\ar[r]_-{F_\ot(A\ot B)}& F_\ot(\top\ot\top)\ar[r]_-{F_\ot({u_\ot}_\top^{-1})} & F_\ot(\top)\ar@/_0.5pc/[ruu]_-{m_\top^{-1}}\\  
}
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\xymatrixrowsep{2.75pc}\xymatrixcolsep{1.75pc}\xymatrix@L=0.5pc{
(F_\ot(A)\ot F_\ot(B))\op (F_\ot(A) \ot F_\ot(B))\ar[r]^-{{m_\ot}_{A,B}\op {m_\ot}_{A,B}}\ar[d]_-{\mu_{F_\ot(A), F_\ot(B), F_\ot(A), F_\ot(B)}}\ar@{}[rd]|{\eqref{cc:F_ot_bilax}} & F_\ot(A\ot B)\op F_\op(A\ot B)\ar[d]^-{{m_\op}_{A\ot B, A\ot B}} \\
(F_\ot(A) \op F_\ot(A))\ot (F_\ot(B)\op F_\ot(B))\ar[d]_-{{m_\op}_{A,A} \ot {m_\op}_{B,B}} & F_\ot((A\ot B)\op (A\ot B))\ar[d]^-{F_\ot(\mu_{A,B,A,B})}  \\
F_\ot(A\op A) \ot F_\ot(B\op B)\ar[d]_-{F_\ot(\nabla_A)\ot F_\ot(\nabla_B)}\ar[r]_-{{m_\ot}_{A\op A,B\op B}} & F_\ot((A\op A)\ot (B\op B))\ar[d]^-{F_\ot(\nabla_A\ot \nabla_B)} \\
F_\ot(A) \ot F_\ot(B)\ar[r]_-{{m_\ot}_{A,B}}& F_\ot(A\ot B)\ar@{}[lu]|{(\nat)}
}
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\xymatrixrowsep{2.75pc}\xymatrixcolsep{2.75pc}\xymatrix@L=0.5pc{
\bot\ar[r]^-{\Delta_\bot}\ar[rd]_-{m_\bot} & \bot\ot\bot\ar[r]^-{m_\bot\ot m_\bot}\ar@{}[rd]|{\eqref{cc:F_ot_bilax}} & F_\ot(\bot)\ot F_\ot(\bot)\ar[r]^-{F_\ot(u_A)\ot F_\ot(u_B)}\ar[d]^-{{m_\ot}_{\bot,\bot}} \ar@{}[rd]|{(\nat)}& F_\ot(A)\ot F_\ot(B)\ar[d]^-{{m_\ot}_{A,B}}\\
%
& F_\ot(\bot)\ar[r]_-{F_\ot(\Delta_\bot)} & F_\ot(\bot\ot\bot)\ar[r]_-{F_\ot(u_A\ot u_B)} & F_\ot(A\ot B)
}
\end{equation*}

We show similarly that $\B[F]_\op$ is a strong symmetric comonoidal functor $(\B[\bX], \op, \langle\bot\rangle)\rarr (\B[\bY], \op, \langle\bot\rangle)$ when equipped with 
\begin{itemize}
    \item morphism $n_{\langle \bot\rangle}\c \B[F]_\op(\langle \bot \rangle)\rangle \langle \bot \rangle$, defined to be $m_\bot\c F_\op(\bot)\rarr \bot$, and 
    \item natural transformation 
    \[{n_\op}_{\langle A\rangle, \langle B\rangle}\c \B[F]_\op(\langle A\rangle \op \langle B\rangle)\rarr \B[F]_\op(\langle A\rangle) \op \B[F]_\op(\langle B\rangle)\,,\]
    defined to be ${n_\op}_{A,B}\c F_\op(A\op B)\rarr F_\op(A)\op F_\op(B)$. 
\end{itemize}

Then, $\B[F] = (\B[F]_\ot, \B[F]_\op)\colon\B[\bX]\rarr\B[\bY]$ is a strong symmetric linear functor, with linear strengths:
\begin{align*}
&{v_\ot^R}_{\langle A\rangle ,\langle B\rangle } \c \B[F]_\ot(\langle A\rangle \op \langle B\rangle )\rarr \B[F]_\op(\langle A\rangle )\op \B[F]_\ot(\langle B\rangle ) \\
&{v_\op^R}_{\langle A\rangle ,\langle B\rangle } \c \B[F]_\ot(\langle A\rangle )\ot \B[F]_\op(\langle B\rangle )\rarr \B[F]_\op(\langle A\rangle \ot \langle B\rangle )
\end{align*}
defined to be ${\nu^R_\ot}_{A,B}\c F_\ot(A\op B)\rarr F_\op(A)\op F_\ot(B)$ and ${\nu^R_\op}_{A,B}\c F_\ot(A)\ot F_\op(B)\rarr F_\op(A\ot B)$ respectively in \bY, as \\

${\nu^R_\ot}_{A,B}$ is a medial bimonoid morphism by the following commuting diagrams:
\begin{equation*}
\xymatrixrowsep{2.75pc}\xymatrixcolsep{1.75pc}\xymatrix@L=0.5pc{
F_\ot(A\op B)\ar[r]^-{{\nu^R_\ot}_{A,B}}\ar[d]_-{F_\ot(\Delta_A\op \Delta_B)}\ar@{}[rd]|{(\nat)} & F_\op(A)\op F_\ot(B)\ar[d]^-{F_\op(\Delta_A)\op F_\ot(\Delta_B)} \\
F_\ot((A\ot A)\op(B\ot B))\ar[r]^-{{\nu^R_\ot}_{A\ot A, B\ot B}}\ar[d]_-{F_\ot(\mu_{A,A,B,B})}\ar@{}[rd]|{\eqref{cc:linear_strength_medial}} & F_\op(A\ot A)\op F_\ot(B\ot B)\ar[d]^-{{n_\ot}_{A,A} \op {m_\op^{-1}}_{B,B}} \\
F_\ot((A\op B)\ot(A\op B))\ar[d]_-{{m_\ot^{-1}}_{A\op B, A\op B}} & (F_\op(A)\ot F_\ot(A))\op (F_\ot(B) \ot F_\op(B))\ar[d]^-{\mu_{F_\op(A), F_\op(A), F_\ot(B), F_\op(B)}} \\
F_\ot(A\op B)\ot F_\ot(A\op B)\ar[r]_-{{\nu^R_\ot}_{A,B}\ot{\nu^R_\ot}_{A,B}} & (F_\op(A)\ot F_\ot(B))\ot (F_\op(A)\op F_\ot(B))
}
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\xymatrixrowsep{3.75pc}\xymatrixcolsep{1.75pc}\xymatrix@L=0.5pc{
F_\ot(A\op B)\ar[r]^-{F_\ot(e_A\op e_B)}\ar[d]_-{{\nu^R_\ot}_{A,B}}\ar@{}[rd]|{(\nat)} & F_\ot(\top\op\top)\ar[r]^-{F_\ot(\nabla_\top)}\ar[d]_-{{\nu^R_\ot}_{\top,\top}}\ar@{}[rd]|{\eqref{cc:linear_strength_nabla_delta}}& F_\ot(\top)\ar[r]^-{m_\top^{-1}} & \top\\
F_\op(A)\op F_\ot(B)\ar[r]_-{F_\op(e_A)\op F_\ot(e_B)} & F_\op(\top)\op F_\ot(\top)\ar[r]_-{n_\top \op m_\top^{-1}} & \top\op\top\ar[ru]_-{\nabla_\top}\\ 
}
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\xymatrixrowsep{2.75pc}\xymatrixcolsep{1.75pc}\xymatrix@L=0.5pc{
F_\ot(A\op B) \op F_\ot(A\op B)\ar[r]^-{{\nu^R_\ot}_{A,B} \op {\nu^R_\ot}_{A,B}}\ar[d]_-{{m_\op}_{A\op B, A\op B}}\ar@{}[rd]|{\eqref{cc:linear_strength_m_op_n_ot_canonicalflip}} & (F_\op(A)\op F_\ot(B))\op (F_\op(A)\op F_\ot(B))\ar[d]^-{\tau^\op_{F_\op(A), F_\ot(B), F_\op(A), F_\ot(B)}}\\
F_\ot((A\op B)\op (A\op B))\ar[d]_-{F_\ot(\tau^\op_{A,B,A,B})} & (F_\op (A)\op F_\ot(A)) \op (F_\ot(B)\op F_\ot(B))\ar[d]^-{{n_\op^{-1}}_{A,A}\op {m_\op}_{B,B}}\\
F_\ot((A\op B)\op (A\op B)) \ar[r]^-{{\nu^R_\ot}_{A\op A, B\op B}}\ar[d]_-{\nabla_A\op\nabla_B} \ar@{}[rd]|{(\nat)}& F_\op (A\op A) \op F_\ot (B\op B)\ar[d]^-{F_\op(\nabla_A)\op F_\ot(\nabla_B)} \\
F_\ot(A\op B)\ar[r]_-{{\nu^R_\ot}_{A,B}} & F_\op(A) \op F_\ot(B)
}
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\xymatrixrowsep{2.75pc}\xymatrixcolsep{1.75pc}\xymatrix@L=0.5pc{
\bot\ar[r]^-{m_\bot}\ar@/_1.5pc/[rdd]_-{{u_\op^{-1}}_\bot} & F_\ot(\bot)\ar[r]^-{F_\ot({u_\op^{-1}}_\bot)}\ar[d]_-{{u_\op^L}^{-1}_{F_\ot(\bot)}} \ar@{}[rd]|{\eqref{cc:linear_strength_unit}}& F_\ot(\bot\op\bot)\ar[r]^-{F_\ot(u_A\op u_B)}\ar[dd]^-{{\nu^R_\ot}_{\bot,\bot}} \ar@{}[rdd]|{(\nat)}& F_\ot(A\op B)\ar[dd]^-{{\nu^R_\ot}_{A,B}}\\
& \bot\op F_\ot(\bot)\ar[dr]^-{n_\bot^{-1}\op 1_{F_\ot(\bot)}}\ar@{}[d]|{(\nat)} &\\
& \bot\op\bot\ar[r]_-{n_\bot^{-1}\op m_\bot} & F_\op(\bot)\op F_\ot(\bot)\ar[r]_-{F_\op(u_A)\op F_\ot(u_B)} & F_\op(A)\op F_\op( B) \\
}
\end{equation*}
and ${v^R_\op}_{A,B}$ is a medial bimonoid morphism similarly.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:medial_linear_trans_is_cartesian_linear_trans}
Given a strong medial linear transformations $\alpha=(\alpha_\ot, \alpha_\op)\c F\Rarr G$ between strong symmetric medial linear functors, then it canonically extends to a linear transformation $\B[\alpha] = (\B[\alpha]_\ot, \B[\alpha]_\op)\c \B[F]\Rarr\B[G]$ between strong symmetric linear functors. 
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Consider a strong medial linear transformations $\alpha=(\alpha_\ot, \alpha_\op)\c F\Rarr G$ between strong symmetric medial linear functors. Define $\B[\alpha] = (\B[\alpha]_\ot, \B[\alpha]_\op)\c \B[F]\Rarr \B[G]$, where 
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\B[\alpha]_\ot\c \B[F]_\ot \rarr \B[G]_\ot$ has component morphisms ${\alpha_\ot}_A\c F_\ot(A)\rarr G_\ot(A)$ in \bY, 
    \item $\B[\alpha]_\op\c \B[G]_\op \rarr \B[F]_\op$ has component morphisms ${\alpha_\op}_A\c G_\op(A)\rarr F_\op(A)$ in \bY.
\end{itemize}

This is well-defined as ${\alpha_\ot}_A\c F_\ot(A)\rarr G_\ot(A)$ is a medial bimonoid morphism since $\alpha_\ot\c (F_\ot, m_\top^F, m_\ot^F)\Rarr (G_\ot, m^G_\top, m^G_\ot)$ is a monoidal transformation and therefore is a $\ot$-monoid morphism and $\alpha_\ot\c (F_\ot, m_\bot^F, m_\op^F)\Rarr (G_\ot, m^G_\bot, m^G_\op)$ is a monoidal transformation and therefore is a $\op$-monoidal morphism. \\

Similarly, ${\alpha_\op}_A\c G_\op(A)\rarr F_\op(A)$ is a medial bimonoid morphism. 
\end{proof}

With the previous lemmas, we can state that the medial bimonoid constructions is a 2-functor between the 2-category of symmetric medial LDCs and the 2-category of cartesian LDCs as desired.

\begin{proposition}
$\B[-]\c\SMLDC\rarr\CLDC$ is a 2-functor.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
   By Theorem \ref{thm:BX_CLDC}, Lemma \ref{lem:medial_linear_functor_cartesian_linear_functor} and Lemma \ref{lem:medial_linear_trans_is_cartesian_linear_trans}, $\B[-]$ determines well-defined maps from the 0-cells, 1-cells and 2-cells respectively of \SMLDC to \CLDC. The proof that this is 2-functorial follows immediately as composition in the 2-categories are defined similarly, the composite of two medial bimonoid morphisms is a bimonoid morphism, and 1-cells and 2-cells in \SMLDC preserve medial bimonoid morphisms. 
\end{proof}

It is fairly immediate that given a strong symmetric Frobenius medial linear functor $F = (F_\ot, F_\op)\c \bX\rarr\bY$, the induced cartesian linear functor $\B[F]\c \B[\bX]\rarr \B[\bY]$ is itself Frobenius as $F_\ot(A)= F_\op(A)$ and the medial bimonoid structure maps of $F_\ot(A)$ are equal to the ones of $F_\op(A)$ in the Frobenius contexts. Therefore:
\begin{corollary}
$\B[-]\c\SMLDC\rarr\CLDC$ restricts to a 2-functor $\B[-]\c\mathbf{SFMLDC}\rarr\mathbf{FCLDC}$.  
\end{corollary}

We have finally arrived at the main result of our paper which motivated all the work:

\begin{theorem}[The Linearly Distributive Fox Theorem]
$\B[-]$ is the right adjoint to the inclusion 2-functor, $\inc \vdash \B[-]\colon\CLDC\rarr\SMLDC$.  
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
To prove this 2-adjunction, we will provide the unit and counit 2-transformations. \\

Firstly, let us consider the unit $\boldsymbol{\eta}\c 1_{\CLDC}\Rarr\inc;\B[-]$. Let $(\bX, \times, \bone, +, \bzero)$ be a cartesian LDC. Recall that every object $A\in \bX$ has a canonical bicommutative medial bimonoid structure 
\[ \langle A, \langle 1_A, 1_A\rangle\c A\rarr A\times A, t_A\c A\rarr \bone, [1_A, 1_A]\c A+A\rarr A, b_A\c \bzero\rarr A\rangle \]
and every arrow $f\c A\rarr B \in \bX$ is a medial bimonoid morphism with respect to these canonical medial bimonoid structures, as discussed in Proposition \ref{prop:canonical_medial_bimonoids_in_CLDC}.  Then, let $\eta_{\bbX}\c\bX\rarr\B[\bX]$ be the functor that sends the objects in \bX\ to their canonical medial bimonoid structures and that is identity on the arrows in \bX. \\

$\eta_{\bbX}$ is further a strong symmetric monoidal functor between $(\bX,\times, \bone)\rarr (\B[\bX], \times, \langle \bone \rangle)$ when equipped with 
\begin{itemize}
    \item morphism $m_\bone\c \langle \bone\rangle \rarr \eta_X(\bone)$ to be $1_\bone\c \bone\rarr \bone$
    \item natural transformation with component morphisms ${m_\times}_{A, B}\c \eta_X(A)\times \eta_X(B) \rarr \eta_X(A\times B)$ given by $1_{A\times B}\c A\times B \rarr A\times B$ 
\end{itemize}
as the canonical medial bimonoid structure on $\bone$ described above is the same as as the medial bimonoid structure on $\bone$ when \bX\ is viewed as medial LDC:
    \[\langle \bone, {u_\times}_\bone, 1_\bone, \nabla_\bone, m\rangle = \langle \bone, \langle 1_\bone, 1_\bone\rangle, t_{\bone}, [1_\bone, 1_\bone], b_{\bone}\rangle\]
and the canonical medial bimonoid structure on $A\times B$ is the same as the medial bimonoid structure of the tensor product $\times$ in $\B[\bX]$ between the canonical structures on $A$ and $B$, i.e.
\begin{align*}
\langle A\times B, & ([1_A, 1_A]\times [1_B, 1_B]); \tau^\times_{A,A,B,B}, (t_A\times t_B); {u_\times}_\bone^{-1}, \\&\mu_{A,B,A,B}; ([1_A, 1_A]\times [1_B, 1_B]), \Delta_\bot; (b_A\times b_B)\rangle \\ \\\
&= \langle A\times B, \langle 1_{A\times B}, 1_{A\times B}\rangle, t_{A\times B}, [1_{A\times B}, 1_{A\times B}], b_{A\times B}\rangle
\end{align*}

Similarly, $\eta_X$ is a strong symmetric comonoidal functor between $(\bX,+, \bzero)\rarr (\B[\bX], +, \langle \bzero, \rangle)$ when equipped with
\begin{itemize}
    \item morphism $n_\bzero\c \eta_X(\bzero)\rarr \langle \bzero\rangle$ to be $1_\bzero\c \bzero\rarr \bzero$
    \item natural transformation with component morphisms ${n_+}_{A, B}\c \eta_X(A+ B)\rarr \eta_X(A)+\eta_X(B)$ given by $1_{A+ B}\c A+B\rarr A+B$ 
\end{itemize}

Then, $\boldsymbol{\eta}_{\bbX} = (\eta_{\bbX}, \eta_{\bbX})\c \bX\rarr \B[\bX]$ is a cartesian linear functor, when further equipped with identity linear strengths. \\

Let $\boldsymbol{\eta}\c 1_{\CLDC}\Rarr\inc;\B[-]$ be defined to be the family of maps $\boldsymbol{\eta}_{\bbX} = (\eta_{\bbX}, \eta_{\bbX})\c \bX\rarr \B[\bX]$. This is a 2-transformation because
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\boldsymbol{\eta}$ satisfies 1-cell naturality:\\
    Let \bX\ and \bX' be cartesian LDCS, and $F = (F_\times, F_+)\c \bX\rarr\bX'$ be a cartesian linear functor between them.  1-cell naturality in this case means 
    \[ \eta_{\bbX}; \B[\inc(F)]_\times = F_\times;\eta_{\bbX} \qquad \eta_{\bbX}; \B[\inc(F)]_+ = F_+; \eta_{\bbX}\]
    Now, these functors are defined as follows
    \begin{align*}
    \eta_{\bbX}; &\B[\inc(F)]_\times\c \\
    & A\mapsto \langle F_\times(A), F_\times(\langle 1_A, 1_A\rangle); {m_\times^{-1}}_{A,A}, F_\times(t_A); {m_\bone^{-1}}, {p_+}_{A,A}; F_\times([1_A, 1_A]), {p_\bzero}; F_\times(b_A)\rangle \\
    & f \mapsto F_\times(f)\\ 
    \\
    F_\times; &\eta_{\bbX}\c\\
    &A\mapsto \langle F_\times(A), \langle 1_{F_\times(A)}, 1_{F_\times(A)}\rangle, t_{F_\times(A)}, [1_{F_\times(A)}, 1_{F_\times(A)}], b_{F_\times(A)}\rangle \\
    & f \mapsto F_\times(f)
    \end{align*}
    which are equal as the medial bimonoid structures on $F_\times(A)$ is unique since \bX' is a cartesian LDC. One shows similarly that the other equality holds as both sides map to the unique medial bimonoid structure on $F_+(A)$.

    \item $\boldsymbol{\eta}$ satisfies 2-cell naturality:
    Let \bX\ and \bX' be cartesian LDCS, $F = (F_\times, F_+), G=(G_\times, G_+)\c \bX\rarr\bX'$ be a cartesian linear functor between them, and $\alpha = (\alpha_\times, \alpha_+)\c F\Rarr G$ be a linear transformation between them. 2-cell naturality in this context means 
    \[ 1_{\eta_{\bbX}}; \B[\inc(\alpha)]_\times = \alpha_\times; 1_{\eta_{\bbX}} \qquad 1_{\eta_{\bbX}}; \B[\inc(\alpha)]_+ = \alpha_+; 1_{\eta_{\bbX}}\]

    Now, these natural transformations as component morphisms for each object $A\in\bX$ the medial bimonoid morphisms given by 
    \[ (1_{\eta_{\bbX}}; \B[\inc(\alpha)]_\times)_{A} = {\alpha_\times}_A = (\alpha_\times; 1_{\eta_{\bbX}})_A\]
    and therefore are equal. Similarly, for the second equality.
\end{itemize}

Secondly, let us consider the counit $\boldsymbol{\epsilon}\c \B[-];\inc\Rarr 1_{\SMLDC}$. Let $(\bX, \ot, \top, \op, \bot)$ be a symmetric medial LDC, then define $\epsilon_{\bbX}\c\B[\bX]\rarr\bX$ to be the forgetful functor 
\[ \langle A, \Delta_A, e_A, \nabla_A, s_A\rangle \mapsto A \qquad f\mapsto f\]

Now, $(\epsilon_{\bbX}, 1_\top, 1_{-\ot-})\c (\B[\bX], \ot, \langle \top\rangle)\rarr (\bX, \ot, \top)$ is a strong symmetric monoidal functor when equipped with identity structure maps, and similarly \\
$(\epsilon_{\bbX}, 1_\bot, 1_{-\op-})\c (\B[\bX], \op, \langle \bot\rangle)\rarr (\bX, \op, \bot)$ is a strong symmetric comonoid functor. Further, $(\epsilon_{\bbX}, 1_\bot, 1_{-\op-}, 1_\top, 1_{-\ot-})\c (\B[\bX], \op, \langle \bot\rangle, \ot, \langle \top \rangle)\rarr (\bX, \op, \bot, \ot, \top)$ is a bilax duoidal functor.\\

Therefore, $\boldsymbol{\epsilon}_{\bbX} =(\epsilon_{\bbX}, \epsilon_{\bbX})\c \B[\bX]\rarr \bX$ is a strong symmetric medial linear functor when equipped with identity linear strengths. \\

Let $\boldsymbol{\epsilon}\c \B[-];\inc\Rarr 1_{\SMLDC}$ be defined to be the family of maps $\boldsymbol{\epsilon}_{\bbX} =(\epsilon_{\bbX}, \epsilon_{\bbX})\c \B[\bX]\rarr \bX$. It satisfies 1-cell and 2-cell naturality fairly trivially and therefore it is a 2-natural transformation.\\ 

To prove the desired 2-adjunction, it remains solely to show the triangle inequalities hold.
\[ \inc(\boldsymbol{\eta}); \boldsymbol{\epsilon}_{\inc} = 1_{\inc} \qquad \boldsymbol{\eta}_{\B[-]}; \B[\boldsymbol{\epsilon}] = 1_{\B[-]}\]

Consider a cartesian LDC \bX, then \[ (\inc(\boldsymbol{\eta_{\bbX}}); \boldsymbol{\epsilon}_{\inc(\bbX)})_\times = (1_{\inc(\bbX)})_\times \qquad (\inc(\boldsymbol{\eta_{\bbX}}); \boldsymbol{\epsilon}_{\inc(\bbX)})_+ = (1_{\inc(\bbX)})_+\] as the left-hand side functors  behave as identities:
\begin{align*}
(\inc(\boldsymbol{\eta_{\bbX}}); \boldsymbol{\epsilon}_{\inc(\bbX)})_\times = (\inc(\boldsymbol{\eta_{\bbX}}); \boldsymbol{\epsilon}_{\inc(\bbX)})_+ \c\qquad &A\mapsto \langle A, \langle 1_A, 1_A\rangle, t_A, [1_A, 1_A], b_A\rangle \mapsto A\\
&f\mapsto f\mapsto f
\end{align*}

Consider a symmetric medial LDC \bX, then 
\[ (\boldsymbol{\eta}_{\B[\bbX]}; \B[\boldsymbol{\epsilon}])_\ot = (1_{\B[\bbX]})_\ot \qquad (\boldsymbol{\eta}_{\B[\bbX]}; \B[\boldsymbol{\epsilon}])_\op = (1_{\B[\bbX]})_\op\] as the left-hand side functors behave as identities:
\begin{align*}
(\boldsymbol{\eta}_{\B[\bbX]}; \B[\boldsymbol{\epsilon}])_\ot=& (\boldsymbol{\eta}_{\B[\bbX]}; \B[\boldsymbol{\epsilon}])_\op \c\\
& \langle A, \Delta_A, e_A, \nabla_A, u_A\rangle \\
&\mapsto\,\langle \langle A\rangle, \langle 1_{\langle A\rangle}, 1_{\langle A\rangle} \rangle, t_{\langle A\rangle}, [1_{\langle A\rangle}, 1_{\langle A\rangle}], b_{\langle A\rangle}\rangle \\
&\mapsto\,  \langle \epsilon_{\bbX}(\langle A\rangle), \epsilon_{\bbX}(\langle 1_{\langle A\rangle}, 1_{\langle A\rangle} \rangle); 1_{A\ot A}, \epsilon_{\bbX}(t_{\langle A\rangle}); 1_\top, 1_{A\op A};\epsilon_{\bbX}([1_{\langle A\rangle}, 1_{\langle A\rangle}]), 1_\bot; \epsilon_{\bbX}(b_{\langle A\rangle}) \rangle \\
&= \langle A, \Delta_A, e_A, \nabla_A, u_A\rangle
\end{align*}
since, by Theorem \ref{thm:BX_CLDC}, the unique maps induced by the universal properties of products and coproducts in $\B[\bX]$ are given by the structure maps of the medial bimonoids themselves, i.e.
\[ \langle 1_{\langle A\rangle}, 1_{\langle A\rangle} \rangle, = \Delta_A \qquad  t_{\langle A\rangle} = e_A \qquad  [1_{\langle A\rangle}, 1_{\langle A\rangle}] = \nabla_A \qquad  b_{\langle A\rangle} = u_A\]

Therefore, $\boldsymbol{\eta}$ and $\boldsymbol{\epsilon}$ are the unit and counit of the 2-adjunction $\inc \vdash \B[-]\colon\CLDC\rarr\SMLDC$.
\end{proof}

Perhaps the most commonly used result of the traditional of the Fox theorem is that a symmetric monoidal category is cartesian if and only if it is isomorphic to its category of comonoids, as stated in Corollary \ref{cor:SMC_cartesian_iff}. We can of course state an analogous Corollary to our 2-adjunction for symmetric medial LDCs:

\begin{corollary}
A symmetric medial linearly distributive category is cartesian if and only if it is isomorphic to its category of bicommutative medial bimonoids and medial bimonoid morphisms.
\end{corollary}

The above corollary can be unwrapped to give an explicit description in terms of medial bimonoid maps, as in the case of traditional cartesian categories.

\begin{corollary}
A symmetric medial linearly distributive category is cartesian if and only if there are natural transformations 
\[ \Delta_{A}\c A\rarr A\ot A \quad\quad e_{A}\c A\rarr \top \quad\quad \nabla_{A}\c A\op A\rarr A \quad\quad u_{A}:\bot\rarr A \]
such that, $\forall A, B\in\bX, \langle A, \Delta_{A}, e_{A}, \nabla_{A}, u_{A}\rangle$ determines a bicommutative medial bimonoid, and 
\[\Delta_{A\ot B} = (\Delta_{A}\ot\Delta_{B}); \tau^\ot_{A, A, B, B} \qquad e_{A\ot B} = (e_{A}\ot e_{B}); {u^{R}_{\ot}}_{\top}^{-1} \]
\[ \nabla_{A\ot B} = \mu_{A,B,A,B}; (\nabla_A \ot \nabla_B) \qquad u_{A\ot B} = \nabla_\top; (u_A\ot u_B) \]

\[\nabla_{A\op B} = \tau^\op_{A, B, A, B};(\nabla_{A}\op\nabla_{B}) \qquad u_{A\op B} = {u^R_\op}^{-1}_{\bot};(u_{A}\op u_{B}) \]
\[ \Delta_{A\op B} = (\Delta_A \op\Delta_B) ; \mu_{A,A,B,B} \qquad e_{A\op B} = (e_A\op e_B);\Delta_\bot \]

\[\Delta_{\top} = {u^{R}_{\ot}}_{\top} \qquad e_{\top} = 1_{\top} \qquad  \nabla_{\bot} = {u^{R}_{\op}}_{\bot} \qquad u_{\bot} = 1_{\bot} \]
\[ e_\bot = u_\top = m \]
\end{corollary}
This is in essence an amalgamations of Proposition \ref{prop:char_CLDC} and Corollary \ref{cor:char_CLDC_naive}, developed at the start to get a better understanding of the underlying structure of CLDCs. 

