	\section{Related Works}

	\subsection{LLM-as-a-Judge}
	\label{rel:judge}
	We begin with \cite{zheng2023judging}, which uses the generative capabilities of LLMs to act as evaluators. These can be grouped into two types: prompt-based and fine-tuned evaluators. For the former, \cite{li2025dna} adopts a "decompose and aggregate" strategy, identifying weighted evaluation aspects and combining their scores to assess candidate responses. For the latter, which has recently gained momentum, \cite{kim2024prometheus} is a representative work that trains evaluators using large-scale synthetic data in both pointwise and pairwise setups, incorporating a weight-merging technique. In multilingual settings, consistency remains limited, as noted by \cite{fu2025reliable}. Among the few multilingual methods, \cite{doddapaneni2024cross} translates between languages to anchor outputs in English for consistent scoring, while \cite{pombal2025m} follows \cite{kim2024prometheus} to generate multilingual evaluation data and fine-tune models accordingly.

	\subsection{Checklist-based Evaluators}
	Several works have explored checklist-based evaluation. RocketEval \citep{weirocketeval} generates binary checklist items, then optimizes or reweights them to produce final scores. TICK \citep{cook2024ticking} uses instructions to generate checklists, which LLMs use for self-improvement. CheckEval \citep{lee2024checkeval} defines high-level criteria, then decomposes, diversifies, and filters them to form evaluation checklists. FBI \citep{doddapaneni2024finding} employs checklists for meta-evaluation to assess evaluator LLMs. Unlike these works, our framework introduces a novel architecture and, notably: (1) extends to multilingual settings; (2) supports pairwise evaluation beyond pointwise framing; and (3) uniquely incorporates broadness, descriptiveness, dynamism, and answer-mentioning in a unified manner.

