	To evaluate our CE-Judge framework in pointwise mode, we used accuracy—defined as the number of correct predictions over the total number of samples. For the pairwise setting, we measured performance using Kendall’s Tau correlation coefficient \citep{kendall1938new}, which assesses the agreement between our model’s rankings and human judgments. 

	\subsection{Baselines}
	We compare our framework with three types of models. The first includes proprietary models like \texttt{GPT-4o}. The second is \texttt{Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct}, a strong multilingual open-source LLM that is instruction-tuned from a pretrained model without further fine-tuning. The third category consists of models explicitly trained as evaluators, such as \texttt{Prometheus 2} \citep{kim2024prometheus}, \texttt{Hercule} \citep{doddapaneni2024cross}, and \texttt{M-Prometheus} \citep{pombal2025m}, as discussed in Subsection~\ref{rel:judge}.

	\section{Results}

	We evaluate CE-Judge on multilingual reasoning, chat, and literary translation tasks against proprietary and open-source baselines, including the fine-tuned M-Prometheus.

	\begin{itemize}
		\item In the reasoning task (Table~\ref{tab:res}), CE-Judge achieves the highest average (0.77) among open-source models, outperforming several large fine-tuned evaluators like M-Prometheus 14B (0.70). Despite using only 7B parameters and no training, it performs close to GPT-4o (0.79), excelling in both high- and low-resource languages.

		\item In chat evaluation (Table~\ref{tab:chat}), CE-Judge leads again with 0.75 average accuracy, surpassing GPT-4o (0.73) and significantly outperforming M-Prometheus 7B (0.62).

		\item For literary translation (Table~\ref{tab:liteval}), which requires fine-grained linguistic and stylistic insight, CE-Judge attains an average Kendall correlation (0.38) comparable to GPT-4o and much better than its backbone model (0.17). It slightly trails M-Prometheus 7B (0.43), which benefited from fine-tuning on large supervised multilingual data.
	\end{itemize}


	\section{Conclusion}
	In this work, we introduce CE-Judge, a novel and straightforward checklist-based framework for multilingual LLM-as-a-Judge that is training-free and built on an open-source model. By leveraging dynamic, broad, and flexible checklist items, CE-Judge supports both pointwise and pairwise evaluations across diverse languages. Experiments on multiple multilingual benchmarks show that CE-Judge not only generally outperforms open-source baselines but also performs comparably to GPT-4o. These results highlight the promise of structured, dynamic evaluation techniques for improving the reliability and interpretability of LLM judgment, particularly in multilingual contexts, for more consistent performance.

	\section{Future directions}
	Despite its strong results and training-free design, our framework has several limitations to address in future work. First, crafting effective prompts for each of the three steps per task can be time-consuming, so developing an automatic, adaptable prompt generation module would be beneficial. Second, our method relies solely on LLM generation, which may suffer from misalignment between training objectives and robust text generation. Incorporating internal LLM representations, as shown by \citet{sheng2024repeval}, could capture more accurate implicit knowledge. Finally, our framework’s flexibility suggests potential extensions as a plug-and-play method or adaptations to other evaluation strategies, such as interview-based evaluation \citep{kim2024llm}.


