The Hamiltonian in Eq.~(\ref{eqn:eqn1}) effectively describes an SSH-like, semi-infinite chain in a synthetic Fock-state lattice when either \( v_r = 0 \) or \( v_{cr} = 0 \) (Fig. \ref{fig:figure1}). 
The Fock states \( |n\rangle \) serve as lattice sites, with the hopping amplitude between neighboring sites scaling as \( \sqrt{n} \), lacking the discrete translational symmetry. 
Since \( w \) remains constant across all lattice sites, the increasing hopping amplitude induces a domain wall at the site \( |n\rangle \) where the relative strength between \( w \) and \( |v| \sqrt{n} \) is reversed, where \( v \) denotes the non-zero hopping parameter.
In this semi-infinite SSH configuration with broken translational symmetry, the domain wall results in a defect at \( |n\rangle \), as illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:figure1}. 

