To unveil the dynamics in this synthetic Fock-state lattice, we derive the analytical solutions of its states and spectrum for the entire regime between two extremes illustrated in Fig. \ref{fig:figure1}.
For a given \(w\), the dynamics over the lattice, including the emergence of defect states, is governed by the one with larger amplitude between \( v_r \) and \( v_{cr} \), while the smaller one acts as a long-range hopping correction to the system \cite{SSH_long_range_hopping}.
By diagonalizing the Hamiltonian in Eq.~(\ref{eqn:eqn1}), we obtain the analytical solution for the complete set of energy eigenstates and eigenvalues for the general case \( |v_r| \neq |v_{cr}| \)~\cite{supplement}:
\begin{align}
|\psi_{n+1}^{\pm}\rangle &= \hat{S}(\xi') \hat{D}(\alpha) \hat{R}(\theta) \left( \frac{|n{+}1\rangle |A\rangle \pm e^{i\phi_n} |n\rangle |B\rangle}{\sqrt{2}} \right), \nonumber \\
|\psi_0\rangle &= \hat{S}(\xi) \hat{D}(\alpha) \hat{R}(\theta) |0\rangle |A\rangle, \nonumber \\
E_n^{\pm} &= \pm \sqrt{ \left| \left( \left| v_r \right| - \left|v_{cr} \right| \right) n \right| },
\label{eqn:eqn2}
\end{align}
for \( n \in \{0, 1, 2, \dots\} \). Here, \(|A\rangle = |g\rangle\), \(|B\rangle = |e\rangle\) for \(|v_r| > |v_{cr}|\), and \(|A\rangle = |e\rangle\), \(|B\rangle = |g\rangle\) for \(|v_r| < |v_{cr}|\).
The operators \(\hat{D}(\alpha)\), \( \hat{S}(\xi) \), and \( \hat{R}(\theta) \) are the displacement, squeezing, and phase-shifting operators, respectively~\cite{quantum_optics, phase_shift_operator}. 
The phase factors $\phi_n$ indicate arbitrary relative phases between the two sublattices.
The relevant parameters are defined as:
\begin{align}
&\alpha = \frac{w (|v_{cr}|e^{-i\phi} - |v_r|e^{i\phi})}{ |v_r|^2 - |v_{cr}|^2 }, \quad \tanh(2\xi') = \frac{2 |v_r v_{cr}|}{ |v_r|^2 + |v_{cr}|^2 }, \nonumber \\
&\tanh \xi =  \frac{\min{\{ |v_{r}|, |v_{cr}| \}}}{\max{\{ |v_{r}|, |v_{cr}| \}}}, \ \ \theta =\frac{\phi_{cr}-\phi_r}{2}, \ \ \phi=\frac{\phi_r + \phi_{cr}}{2}
\label{eqn:eqn3}
\end{align}
where \(\xi, \xi'\in\mathbb{R}\). Figure~\ref{fig:figure2} shows the energy spectra obtained via numerical diagonalization of the Hamiltonian for \( \phi = 0 \) and \( \phi = \pi/2 \). 
The analytical expression in Eq.~(\ref{eqn:eqn2}), indicated by cross markers, shows excellent agreement with the numerical results. 
Notably, the spectra exhibits qualitatively different behaviors near the critical point \( |v_r| = |v_{cr}| \), depending on the values of \( \phi \), which will be discussed later.

As shown in Eq.~(\ref{eqn:eqn2}), the weaker of the two coupling (\(v_r\), \(v_{cr}\)) modifies the phase-space structure of the eigenstates by introducing additional displacement, rotation, and squeezing. Moreover, the zero-energy defect state \( |\psi_0\rangle \) maintains a fixed spin polarization, \( \langle \psi_0 | \hat{\sigma}_z | \psi_0 \rangle \).

When \( |v_r| = |v_{cr}| = |v| \), the energy spectrum is:
\begin{align}
E^{\pm}(q, \phi) = \pm \sqrt{ \frac{w^2}{2} \left( 1 - \cos{2\phi} \right) + 2 |v|^2 q^2 },
\label{eqn:eqn4}
\end{align}
where \( q \) is the position eigenvalue in the transformed phase space defined by \( \hat{q} = \hat{R}(-\theta) \hat{D}(-\beta) \hat{x} \hat{D}(\beta) \hat{R}(\theta) \), with \( \beta = -\frac{we^{i\phi_r}}{\sqrt{2}|v|} \) and \( \hat{x}= \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\left(\hat{a}+\hat{a}^{\dagger}\right) \)~\cite{supplement}. The energy gap closes at \( \phi = 2\pi m \) for \( m \in \mathbb{Z} \), forming a periodic Dirac-cone structure in a \((1\!+\!1)\)-dimensional space spanned by the phase-space coordinate \( q \) and the parameter \( \phi \).

\section{Phase-space topology}
To investigate topological features of our infinite and aperiodic system, which are indicated by the topologically protected spin polarization of the defect state around a periodic Dirac cone, we develop the theoretical framework of phase-space topology.

The spin polarization of the zero-energy defect state is topologically protected and robust against noise in the hopping parameters \(w\), \(v_r\), and \(v_{cr}\). It depends solely on the relative magnitude between \(|v_r|\) and \(|v_{cr}|\): 
\begin{align}
\left\langle \psi_0 \left| \hat{\sigma}_z \right| \psi_0 \right\rangle = \text{sgn}(|v_{cr}| - |v_r|),
\label{eqn:eqn5}
\end{align}
as derived from Eq. (\ref{eqn:eqn2}). Thus, the two regimes \(|v_r| > |v_{cr}|\) and \(|v_r| < |v_{cr}|\) correspond to distinct topological phases, characterized by opposite spin polarizations of the defect state. Since the defect is localized at the interface between two distinct domains (see Fig. \ref{fig:figure1}), it can be interpreted as a boundary property of the lattice.

As Eq.~(\ref{eqn:eqn2}) shows, the energy gap vanishes in the limit \( n \to \infty \), defining the bulk regime of the lattice. 
However, the non-uniform hopping amplitudes prevent a conventional momentum-space analysis of the system. 
Furthermore, the semi-infinite structure of the Fock-state lattice hinders the application of real-space topological invariants developed for systems lacking translational symmetry~\cite{realspace_winding, topological_marker, supplement}.

To resolve the difficulty in directly applying band topology to Fock-state lattices that lack translational symmetry, we focus on the phase parameter \( \phi \), which provides alternative periodicity over the phase space.
We note that the eigenstates are centered along a circular trajectory given by \( \alpha(\phi) = C + R e^{i\phi} \).
As \( \phi \) varies from 0 to \( 2\pi \), \( \alpha(\phi) \) traces a circular loop in Fig.~\ref{fig:figure3}, which corresponds to a helical trajectory of the eigenstates along the \(\phi\)-axis.
Accordingly, the regimes \( |v_r| > |v_{cr}| \) and \( |v_r| < |v_{cr}| \) correspond to the geometric conditions \( R > |C| \) and \( R < |C| \), respectively.
In analogy with the winding number in the SSH model~\cite{ssh_lecture}, we define a phase-space winding number for the SSH-like chain in the Fock-state lattice as
\begin{align}
W = \frac{1}{2\pi i} \int_{0}^{2\pi} d\phi \, \frac{d}{d\phi} \ln \alpha(\phi) = 
\begin{cases}
1 & |v_{r}| > |v_{cr}|, \\
0 & |v_{r}| < |v_{cr}|.
\end{cases}
\label{eqn:eqn6}
\end{align}
This quantity indicates whether the trajectory \(\alpha(\phi)\) encloses the origin in phase space, where \( \phi \) substitutes the role of the Brillouin zone in defining the Zak phase in 1D periodic systems. 
The resulting winding number exhibits a discrete jump at the critical point \( |v_r| = |v_{cr}| \), as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:figure3}.

