\end{figure}

The proposed topological invariant provides an insight into the topological properties of SSH-like lattices and their defect states described in Fig.~\ref{fig:figure1}.
We note that the winding number \( W \) is defined through the geometric properties of the bosonic mode coupled to the spin state, such that fluctuations in the Hamiltonian parameters appear as deformations in the phase-space geometry.

We compute the phase-space winding number in Eq.~(\ref{eqn:eqn6}) for various values of \( w \) in Fig. \ref{fig:figure4}, under noise sampled uniformly from \([0, \delta v]\) for \( |v_r| \) and from \( [-\pi, \pi] \) for \( \phi_r \).  
Using the parameters \( |v_r| = 0.5 \), \( |v_{cr}| = 1 \), and \( \phi_r = \phi_{cr} = 0 \), we observe that both the spin polarization and the phase-space winding number \( W \) remain robust against amplitude and phase noise across different values of \( w \), as long as the condition \( |v_r| < |v_{cr}| \) is preserved, which corresponds to continuous deformation to quantum state geometry.
However, as \( \delta v \) goes beyond the threshold \( |v_{cr}| - |v_r| \), the robustness of both quantities breaks down due to the loss of topological protection.  
Since the noise acts uniformly across all lattice sites, exceeding this threshold results in a statistical mixture of distinct topological phases, which hinders the topological nature of the system.
Therefore, the proposed winding number characterizes the quantum-unique topological feature of our systems---while bosonic properties, such as the expectation values of \(n\) or the phase space distribution, vary under continuous parameter deformation, the proposed invariant illustrates the topological protection of fermionic states, as shown in Fig. \ref{fig:figure4}.

The physical meaning of the phase-space winding number can be understood by analyzing the geometric phase accumulated along the phase parameter \(\phi\).
As shown in Eqs.~(\ref{eqn:eqn2}) and (\ref{eqn:eqn6}), the parameter \( \phi \) characterizes the periodic structure of the eigenstates \( |\psi^{\pm}_n\rangle \) in phase space, and can be interpreted as a quasi-momentum of the SSH-like chain defined on the Fock-state lattice.  
Accordingly, the accumulated phase difference between the two sublattices over \( \phi \in [0, 2\pi] \) corresponds to the Zak phase~\cite{Zak_phase} of the SSH-like chain.  
As \( \phi \) varies from 0 to \( 2\pi \), each Fock state \( |n\rangle \) gains a phase of \( n\pi/2 \) due to the phase-shifting operator \( \hat{R}(\theta) \), forming a helical structure along the \( \phi \)-axis.  
The dressed eigenstates \( |\psi_n^{\pm}\rangle \) in Eq.~(\ref{eqn:eqn2}) thus imply that the \( |A\rangle \) sublattice exhibits a relative phase of \( \pi/2 \) compared to \( |B\rangle \) for all \( n \).  
As a result, a normalized bulk state \( |\psi\rangle \) acquires a quantized geometric phase in the \( |A\rangle \) sublattice as \( \phi \) varies from 0 to \( 2\pi \).

As described in Eq. (\ref{eqn:eqn6}), the fermionic spin state \( |A\rangle \), which is entangled with the bosonic state \( |n+1\rangle \), is determined by the relative strength between \( |v_r| \) and \( |v_{cr}| \).  
As a result, the sign of the phase difference between the sublattices \( |e\rangle \) and \( |g\rangle \) exhibits a discrete jump at the transition point \( |v_r| = |v_{cr}| \).  
To analyze this behavior, we fix \( \phi_{cr} \) and examine the accumulated geometric phase difference between the two sublattices for a normalized bulk state \( |\psi\rangle \) as \( \phi \) varies over the interval \( [0, 2\pi] \)~\cite{berry_phase_coherent}.
\[
\Delta \gamma = i\int_{0}^{2\pi} d\phi\, \langle \psi | \hat{\sigma}_z | \partial_{\phi} \psi \rangle =
\begin{cases}
-\pi/2 & |v_r| > |v_{cr}| \\
\pi/2 & |v_r| < |v_{cr}|,
\end{cases}
\]
which denotes the difference between phase-space Zak phase that illustrates the same quantum state geometry with the winding number in Eq. (\ref{eqn:eqn6}).
The discrete change in \( \Delta \gamma \) reflects the spin polarization of the SSH-like chain, illustrating the bulk--boundary correspondence in the Fock-state lattice.  
The winding number, defined along a single loop in \( \phi \)-space, captures the difference in the helical structure between \( |n\rangle \) and \( |n+1\rangle \), with the sign information encoded in the geometric parameters \( R \) and \( C \).

\section{Quantum phase-space analysis}
To interpret the observed topological properties in terms of quantum states, we introduce the quantum phase-space illustration of defect states.
The \(\phi\)-dependent energy spectrum, as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:figure2} and Eq.~(\ref{eqn:eqn4}), reflects the critical behavior of defect states emerging in phase space.
Figure~\ref{fig:figure5} illustrates the phase-space trajectories for \( |v_r| - |v_{cr}| = \pm 0.75\), along with the Wigner functions of the zero-energy defect states at \( \phi = \pi/4 \). 
The squeezing axes are perpendicular to the black dotted line connecting the centers of the two defect states in different regions. 
As the difference \( |v_{r}| - |v_{cr}| \to 0 \), the defect states become infinitely squeezed along the squeezing axis, and both the center \( C \) and the radius \( R \) of the trajectory diverge. 
At \( \phi = 0 \), the centers of the eigenstates in different phases coincide, as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:figure5}, and the energy gap in Eq.~(\ref{eqn:eqn4}) closes at the critical point \( \Delta |v| = 0 \). 
In contrast, for \( \phi \ne 0 \), the squeezing axes remain parallel between the two regimes, but a spatial separation between the phase-space centers emerges and reaches a maximum at \( \phi = \pi/2 \). 
This separation diverges as \( \Delta |v| \to 0 \), resulting in a finite energy gap at the critical point. 
This critical behavior underlies the Dirac-cone structure shown in Eq.~(\ref{eqn:eqn4}).

In contrast to classical systems, where topological phenomena emerge from the band structure, the topological features in our system are rooted in its entanglement structure and phase-space characteristics.
The defect states possess topologically protected spin polarization, and the spin--boson entanglement in Eq.~(\ref{eqn:eqn2}) induces a Zak phase in the single-atom quantum Rabi system.
The phase-space geometry defines the corresponding topological invariant, the phase-space winding number, and the interplay between displacement and squeezing of the eigenstates leads to a conical energy spectrum.
Our results highlight phase-space geometry as a powerful framework for realizing and probing topological phenomena in hybrid quantum spin--oscillator systems~\cite{hybrid_qc_device, hybrid_qc_device2}, where topology arises from intrinsic quantum properties.

