\section{Discussion}
In this work, we have shown that a single-atom system described by the quantum Rabi model exhibits topological features characterized by its phase-space geometry.
To overcome the absence of translational symmetry and the unconventional band structure of the Fock-state lattice, we introduced a phase-space winding number as a topological invariant, reflecting the quantized geometric phase.
We showed that the proposed invariant describes the topologically protected fermionic spin state in the defect states of the Rabi SSH-like chain and links directly to the Zak phase of the Fock-state lattice.
Furthermore, we analyzed the $\phi$-dependent dynamics in phase space, which leads to a conical energy spectrum at the critical point.
Our results demonstrate topological features emerge from the intrinsic quantum nature of a single-atom synthetic lattice, establishing a compact and controllable framework for exploring topological physics.
We demonstrate that topological phases, typically characterized by spatial symmetries in real-space lattices, can instead be identified through geometric features in the phase space of a synthetic Fock-state lattice.

The Hamiltonian in Eq.~(\ref{eqn:eqn1}) can be implemented using trapped ions or superconducting circuits, where the coupling strengths $|v_{r}|$, $|v_{cr}|$, and $w$ correspond to sideband and carrier Rabi frequencies~\cite{dsc_ion, ion_sdf, sideband_superconducting, sideband_superconducting2, sideband_superconducting3, sideband_superconducting4}.
In trapped-ion systems, typical red- and blue-sideband Rabi frequencies are on the order of tens of kHz, and \(v_r\), \(v_{cr}\), and $w$ can be independently tuned via radio-frequency (RF) amplitude modulation.
We note, however, that the accessible parameter regime may be constrained by the Lamb–Dicke condition~\cite{ion_sdf}, which limits both the maximum achievable sideband coupling strength and the Fock-state population.
In superconducting circuits, sideband Rabi frequencies can reach tens of MHz~\cite{sideband_superconducting4}, allowing for faster dynamics.
However, short coherence times~\cite{sideband_superconducting} may restrict the evolution time, and the effective parameter regimes remain similar, as the topological features of the system are determined by relative coupling strengths rather than absolute values.
In both platforms, the phase factors \( \phi_r \) and \( \phi_{cr} \) are tunable by controlling the relative phases of RF electronics used for sideband generation, enabling access to the desired phase-space geometry.
These systems offer precise control over the drive parameters, making them suitable for the experimental demonstration of single-atom topological features via adiabatic preparation and manipulation of defect states.

An important implication of our results is the separation between the fermionic and bosonic nature of the defect state. 
While the spin polarization of the defect is topologically protected and robust against parameter noise, the bosonic properties, such as the occupation number \( \langle n \rangle \) and the phase-space distribution, remain tunable via system parameters.
This separation enables the realization of tunable, topologically protected quantum states, where the protected spin degree of freedom acts as a stable quantum memory and the bosonic mode can be exploited for control or interfacing.
Such controllable yet protected quantum states may be useful in hybrid quantum computing architectures that require coherence in one degree of freedom and tunability in another~\cite{hybrid_qc_device, hybrid_qc_device2}.
Furthermore, by utilizing multiple bosonic modes in hybrid quantum devices~\cite{hybrid_ion, hybrid_ion2, hybrid_superconducting}, our system can be extended to higher-dimensional synthetic lattices. 
These extended systems enable the exploration of novel topological phenomena arising from interactions between spin-protected defect modes. 
Such developments open up new possibilities for engineering topological matter, where topology originates from phase-space characteristics.

