\section{Sample selection}\label{sec2}

\subsection{Keck Merger Sample}\label{sec2_1}
We have developed a pre-selection method for detecting candidate dual AGNs from Galaxy Zoo \citep{lintott+2008} and CatWISE2020 \citep{marocco+2021} detections. We selected all matches within a 1.5$''$ radius between the full Galaxy Zoo DR1 table and the CatWISE2020 catalog, where Galaxy Zoo positions are derived from SDSS DR7. This resulted in 554,214 matches corresponding to 549,279 Galaxy Zoo targets. We then selected galaxies that have a high likelihood of being strongly disturbed mergers based on the Galaxy Zoo's weighted merger-vote fraction parameter, $f_{m}$, where $f_{m}>0.4$ indicates a system is a strong merger candidate \citep{darg+2010}. We also applied a MIR color cut of $W_1 - W_2 > 0.5$ which has been shown to be an extremely reliable selection criterion to  identify AGN activity in mergers \citep{blecha+2018}. To ensure robust detections in CatWISE2020, we apply a magnitude cut of $W_1 < 15$ where the reliability of a CatWISE2020 detection is above $\sim98\%$ \citep{marocco+2021}.
\begin{figure}[!t]
\centering{\bf{\normalsize{Color-Color Diagram of Keck Merger Sample}}}
\begin{flushleft}
    \includegraphics[width = \linewidth]{color_color_kecks_fluxratio.pdf}
\end{flushleft}
\caption{A WISE color-color plot of the 48 target Keck merger sample and the parent sample of mergers in Galaxy Zoo. Crimson squares correspond to systems in our sample with substructure (see Section~\ref{sec4_1}) and blue points correspond to all other galaxies in our sample. The shaded contours correspond to all merger systems ($f_{m}>0.4$) in Galaxy Zoo. The red dotted horizontal line illustrates the $W_1 - W_2 > 0.5$ color cut applied to the sample \citep{blecha+2018}. The dashed grey box corresponds to the stringent AGN wedge defined in \citealp{jarrett+2011}. Note that one target (J152659) from the merger sample is not included in this plot since it does not have an available $W_3$ magnitude.}
\label{colorcolormergers}
\end{figure}
\par
This selection criteria resulted in 415 matches across 413 highly disturbed merger systems with red WISE colors, ensuring that we are selecting from the most likely targets to harbor at least one AGN \citep[e.g.][]{blecha+2018}. We then leverage the high astrometric precision of CatWISE2020 and SDSS, with astrometric accuracies of 50~mas and 150~mas respectively, to select sources with significant positional offsets between CatWISE2020 and SDSS ($0.15'' < $ offset  $< 1.5''$). We define the lower and upper limits of the acceptable range of offsets as the astrometric precision of SDSS, 150 mas, and the approximate spatial resolution limit of SDSS. Since both SDSS and CatWISE2020 can measure the position of a galaxy's nucleus with great precision, a discrepancy between these measurements may be indicative of unresolved nuclear substructure such as bright star clusters, multiple nuclei or AGN. In Galaxy Zoo, for targets with more than one matching CatWISE2020 coordinate, we expect to find more than one unresolved source of emission traced by each CatWISE2020 coordinate. Since the target selection in our methodology begins with mergers classified as AGNs based on their WISE MIR colors, we optimize the chance of revealing at least one or more AGN with higher resolution follow-up observations. 

