 the Keck and HST images we find that the central near-infrared nucleus is likely spatially coincident with the large dust cloud in the Western region of the optical nuclear emission and not the bright optical emission we see. This suggests that the SDSS/WISE offset we detect is caused by the central nucleus or AGN being obscured by dust and thus biasing the SDSS coordinate away from the true location of the AGN. The host galaxy of this object is optically classified as a $K_{03}$ type AGN in the MPA-JHU catalog. Additionally, it lies within the composite classification of our BPT diagram and outside of the Jarrett AGN region of our WISE color-color diagram.

