\section{Extension to Multi-Receiver Case}
\label{sec:multiple}

The goal of this section is to extend Algorithm \ref{alg:OP} to multiple actuators. Following the industrial communication protocol called the Occupy CoW \cite{swamy2017real}, we assume that the controller broadcasts a common message to $U\ge2$ actuators. Denote by $h_{0,u}$ the direct channel from the controller to the $u$th actuator, and $h_{m,\ell,u}$ the reflected channel induced by MTS $m$ for the $u$th actuator when the MTS is placed at position $\ell$ in its cell, where $m\in\mathcal{M}$, $\ell\in\mathcal{L}_m$, and $u=1,\ldots,U$. The resulting SNR of actuator $u$ is then given by
\begin{equation}
    \mathrm{SNR}_u = \frac{P}{\sigma^2}\left|h_{0,u}+\sum_{m\in\mathcal{M}}\sum_{\ell\in\mathcal{L}_m} x_{m,\ell}h_{m,\ell, u}\right|^2.
\end{equation}
Thus, the placement of each MTS $m$, $\bx_m$, now impacts the performance of all the $U$ actuators.

We seek the optimal $\bX$ to maximize the worst SNR among the actuators:
\begin{subequations}
\label{MU_problem}
\begin{align}
    \underset{\bX}{\text{maximize}} \quad &\min_u \{\mathrm{SNR}_u\}
    \\
    \text {subject to} \quad &x_{m,\ell}\in \{0,1\},\,\forall m,\forall\ell, \\
    &\sum_{\ell\in\mathcal{L}_m} x_{m,\ell} = 1,\,\forall m.
\end{align}
\end{subequations}

% \begin{figure}[t]
