In this work, we derive scaling laws for the quantum information lifetime in dynamical settings with and without bath monitoring enabled by mid-circuit measurements. When the system interacts with a periodically monitored bath and the measurement trajectory is recorded, the information lifetime can scale exponentially with the system size (in the context of NISQRC, this corresponds to the size of the memory subsystem). While without the monitoring -- when the individual measurement trajectory is not recorded, the lifetime scales linearly or is a constant in system size.
%More precisely, when $N_A$ data qubits interact with an arbitrary number of periodically monitored bath qubits via Haar random unitaries, the quantum information lifetime can be exponential $\sim {\rm exp}(N_A)$, regardless of the bath system size $N_B$. While when the bath is not monitored---the measurement trajectory is not recorded---the lifetime of quantum information $\sim 1/N_B$ inversely decays with the bath size, while either linear or independent in system size.
We establish our results analytically for typical unitaries using quantum mutual information between the system and a maximally entangled reference. These findings are supported by numerical simulations under both Haar-random and chaotic Hamiltonian dynamics across various initial states. For chaotic Hamiltonians, we identify atypical regimes where residual correlations persist for times that scale exponentially with system size.  Our analysis highlights the importance of training in QuDDPM for achieving efficient generative learning. \QZ{In the context of monitored quantum circuits studied for entanglement phase transitions, our results demonstrate an exponentially long quantum information lifetime in the weak measurement limit.} We also interpret these results in the context of quantum reservoir computing, suggesting strategies for the design of the memory time in general dynamical systems. \QZ{In the quantum communication setting, our results have implications for entanglement-assisted protocols, both with and without environment assistance.} Finally, we propose a \BZ{hardware-friendly} protocol for verifying mutual information dynamics through noisy simulations of the IBM Quantum hardware. 
