\section{Exponential QMI lifetime in monitored dynamics}
\label{sec:exp}


With the framework for information propagation in place, we now examine the dynamics of quantum information under mid-circuit measurements and resets, focusing on the measurement-conditioned QMI in Eq.~\eqref{QMI_cond} proposed for monitored dynamics.
To begin with, we consider the maximally entangled state, $\ket{\Phi}_{RA_0} \propto \sum_{j} \ket{j}_R \ket{j}_{A_0}$, to obtain analytical results; Then, we numerically extend the analytical results with less-entangled states, perturbed Haar random states and classical-quantum (CQ) hybrid states.
We define the perturbed Haar random states as $\ket{\phi^\delta}_{RA_0} \propto \ket{\phi_{\rm Haar}}_{RA_0} + \delta\ket{\bm 0}_{RA_0}$. Similarly, we define the family of CQ states to be  $(\rho^\delta)_{RA_0} \propto \sum \ketbra{j}{j}_R \otimes \ketbra{u_j^\delta}{u_j^\delta}_{A_0}$ with perturbed basis \HETACC{$\ket{u_j^\delta}_{A_0} \propto \ket{u_j}_{A_0} + \delta\ket{\bm 0}_{A_0}$, where $\ket{u_j}$ forms an orthonormal basis.} 
%\HET{Do you mean $\sum \ketbra{j}{j}_R \otimes \ketbra{u^\delta_j}{u^\delta_j}_{A_0}$?} 
In both cases, $\delta$ interpolates highly correlated ($\delta=0$) and uncorrelated states ($\delta\gg1$).
Throughout the paper, we take the number of reference qubits equaling $N_A$ for simplicity.

%When $\delta = 0$, the QMI of initial Haar random states is near maximal $2N_A$ up to a small correction from Page~\cite{page1993average}, and the initial QMI $I(R:A_0)$ decays when the initial state approaches product state with larger $\delta$. When $\delta = 0$, the initial CQ state owns the maximal classical mutual information of $I(R:A_0) = N_A$, and increasing $\delta$ leads to a monotonically vanishing of mutual information. 


% While we have started with the Bell state for analytical evaluation, we expect the overall linear decay to be generic. here we explore the QMI dynamics of other family of less entangled initial pure states, perturbed Haar random states, which is defined as $\ket{\phi_\delta}_{RA_0} \propto \ket{\phi_{\rm Haar}}_{RA_0} + \delta\ket{\bm 0}_{RA_0}$. When $\delta = 0$, the QMI of Haar random states $\ket{\phi_\delta}_{RA_0} = \ket{\phi_{\rm Haar}}_{RA_0}$ is close to $2N_A$ up to a small correction from Page~\cite{page1993average}, and the initial QMI decays when perturbation becomes stronger. In Fig.~\ref{fig:mi_traceout}c, the QMI dynamics of such perturbed Haar random states (greens) remains linear decay in early time as long as the perturbation is relatively weak. We further notice that the linear decay rate also decreases with stronger perturbation (dark to light). In Fig.~\ref{fig:mi_traceout}d, we generalize the QMI dynamics to CQ states, which is highly related to applications of QRC. $\rho_{RA_0} \propto \sum_{k} \ketbra{k}{k}_R \otimes \ketbra{u_\delta}{u_\delta}_{A_0}$ with perturbed orthonormal basis $\ket{u_\delta} \propto \ket{u} + \delta \ket{\bm 0}$ 











Using Haar random unitaries to model the chaotic evolution, we have the following theorem, where $d_A=2^{N_A}, d_B=2^{N_B}$ are dimension of data and bath Hilbert spaces (see Appendix~\ref{app:theorem1} for proof).
\begin{theorem}
\label{theorem_avgMI}
    The expected measurement-conditioned mutual information of an initial Bell state in quantum dynamics with mid-circuit measurements and reset at time $t$ is asymptotically ($d_A\gg 1$) lower bounded by 
    \begin{align}
        &\E_{\rm Haar}\overline{I(R:A_t|\bfz)} \nonumber\\
        &\gtrsim 2(2t+1)N_A + 2 t N_B - 2\log_2\left[(d_A d_B + 1)^t (d_A + 1)^{t+1} \right] \nonumber\\
        &\quad - 2\log_2\left[\left(\frac{d_A d_B - 1}{d_A d_B + 1}\right)^t - \left(\frac{d_A - 1}{d_A + 1}\right)^{t+1}\right] + 2
        \label{eq:avgMI_lb}\\
        &\simeq 2N_A - 2\log_2\left[(1-1/d_B)t + 1\right]. \label{eq:avgMI_lb_simplify}
    \end{align}
\end{theorem}
In the large bath limit of $d_B \gg 1$, the dynamics of QMI converges to a universal form $\E_{\rm Haar} \overline{I(R:A_t|\bfz)} \gtrsim 2N_A - 2\log_2\left(t + 1\right)$.
We can estimate the $\epsilon$-lifetime for measurement-conditioned QMI as
\be
    \tau_{\rm cond} \gtrsim \frac{d_B}{d_B - 1}\left(2^{(1-\epsilon) N_A} - 1\right) \sim \Omega(\exp(N_A)), \label{eq:avgMI_time}
\ee
which grows exponentially with the data system size $N_A$ and independent of bath system size once $d_B \gg 1$. 


