\vspace{10px}
\begin{abstract}
    \noindent Sampling from a dynamic discrete distribution involves sampling from a dynamic set of weighted elements, where elements can be added or removed at any stage of the sampling process. Although efficient for static sets, the Alias method \cite{walker1977} becomes impractical in dynamic settings due to the need to reconstruct the sampler after each update, which incurs a computational cost proportional to the size of the distribution, making it unsuitable for applications requiring frequent insertions, deletions, or weight adjustments. To address this limitation, different approaches have been studied, such as the Forest of Trees method \cite{matias2008} and the BUcket Sampling (BUS) method \cite{zhang2023}. However, all previous methods suffered from numerical issues which can bias the sampling process. In this paper, we describe EBUS (Exact BUcket Sampling), the first exact algorithm with $O(1)$ sampling and update cost. The sampler can be updated by base-$b$ numbers with bounded precision and exponent, and sample the distribution of its elements exactly and efficiently. We provide also a state of the art implementation\footnote{the method's implementation can be found at \url{https://github.com/LilithHafner/WeightVectors.jl}.} of the method using IEEE 64-bit floating point numbers which we empirically show to be more efficient than several implementations of previous inexact methods.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Sampling over a discrete distribution is a well-studied problem because of its many applications and can be formulated straightforwardly: Given a set of indices $a_1, a_2, \dots, a_N$ with weights $w_1, w_2, \dots, w_N$ the task is to sample an index $a_i$ with probability $w_i/\sum_{i=1}^{N}w_i$. Importantly, the set of indices and their corresponding weights can be fixed throughout the sampling or can change over time. 

In this work, we provide a novel sampling methodology for sampling exactly and efficiently from a set of weights which can change dynamically over time. To the best of the authors knowledge, this is the first algorithm which is both fully correct when working with finite precision weights as well as requiring only $O(1)$ instructions for all the supported operations (\textsf{sample}, \textsf{add} \& \textsf{remove}).

Generating random variates efficiently from dynamic discrete distributions has been widely recognized as a useful tool in several domains and applications such as, for example, database systems \cite{xie2021}, network analysis \cite{slepoy2008}, bioinformatics \cite{colvin2010} or complex systems science \cite{glielmo2025}.

In this work, we will show a new algorithm having the following key properties in the word RAM model:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \emph{Exactness}: the probability of selecting each element matches its weight exactly.
  \item \(O(1)\) \emph{worst-case expected} time per sample (with probabilistic termination).
  \item \(O(1)\) \emph{amortized} time to update any single weight
  \item \(O(n)\) \emph{memory} to store the weigths.
  \item Low constant factors in practice: tens of cycles in typical cases, and at most hundreds in the worst case.
\end{itemize}

These features make it competitive with the most efficient previously known algorithms for dynamic sampling described in \cite{zhang2023, allendorf2024, matias2008}, with the added advantage that it does not suffer from any numerical error.

\section{Related Work}

When the set of indices and their weights is known and fixed for all the sampling process, the classical solution provided by the Alias Method \cite{walker1977} can be employed to solve the problem efficiently, with $O(1)$ time for random draw. The method falls short when the weights have to be updated frequently, since the sampler needs to be re-initialized after each update, incurring an $O(n)$ operation for each of them. Another classical solution often employed is to use a Binary Search Tree to record the sum of the weight in the constituent sub-trees \cite{wong1980}, but this still requires $O(\log(n))$ sampling and update time. For this reason, previous works looked for efficient methodologies which could deal with this limitation. In \cite{matias2008}, the author developed a data structure to deal with the case where the number of elements of the distribution is fixed but the weights could vary over time achieving expected constant time both in sampling and update operations, and they noted that a slight modification of their approach could be used to deal also with the case where also the number of elements in the sampler varies over time. The basic technique at the core of their method is exponential bucketing, namely, to group elements in levels such that level $i$ contains all elements with weights in the range $[2^i, 2^{i+1}]$ so that rejection sampling in a level can be done in amortized constant time. In \cite{zhang2023}, the same approach in taken, but differently from \cite{matias2008}, the authors used linear search for finding the level from which sampling an index and, by tracking the total weight of all the elements in the sampler, they were able to search into an interval of length of max $\log_2(n)$ size. This was done in the general setting where elements can have weights belonging to $\mathbb{R}$. While that method can be considered $O(1)$ in sampling and update if restricted to the finite precision setting, it still suffers strong numerical issues since it works on the floating point representation both for inter-level and intra-level sampling.

\section{Our Exact Algorithm}

As in \cite{zhang2023}, weights are divided into levels according to their exponents, and we first sample a level through linear search and then sample an element within that level through rejection sampling. Differently from that paper, we assume to work in finite-precision arithmetic, and throughout the text, we will further assume to work with non-negative 64-bit floating point for simplicity but the results are easily extended to numbers with different finite precision and basis. To sample exactly, both phases cannot operate on the weights with the standard floating-point operations, but, instead, need to work with their more low-level bit representation, extracting mantissas and exponents.

The method performs a weighted random selection where each group of numbers is assigned a weight based on the precise bit-level representation of their floating‑point values. It first computes a high‑precision weight by extracting and summing the transformed significands, then scales these sums according to the numbers' exponents and a global shift to obtain an approximated weight. The selection process involves drawing a random integer within the total of these weights and then determining the corresponding group by a linear scan of the level weights, employing rejection sampling in specific conditions to refine the process in order to ensure that the probability of selecting any group remains exactly proportional to its true weight, eliminating bias from rounding errors. After having sampled a level exactly, we need to be able to sample exactly within that level. This can be done by using the same intra-level rejection technique described in \cite{dambrosio2022, slepoy2008, zhang2023}, but, to do it exactly, instead of using the finite precision representation of the weights, we use their significands. We now go into the details of each phase.

\subsection{Inter-Level Sampling}

Each level \(L_i\) contains weights $\{\,w_i = m_i \times 2^{e}\mid e = \operatorname{exponent}(w_i)\,\}$ all sharing the same exponent \(e\) so that we can store the exact sum of these weights by accumulating their 52-bit significands into a 128-bit integer. If we denote the significand sum of level $L_i$ by $SS_{L_i}$ we then have that $ SS_{L_i} = \sum_{w_i\in {L_i}} m_i$.

To avoid the use of expensive arbitrary precision arithmetic during sampling, we also maintain a 64-bit integer approximation of the total weight of each level \(\widetilde{SS}_{L_i}\), defined by
\[
\widetilde{SS}_{L_i} =
\begin{cases}
SS_{L_i}  \ll  (G + {L_i}) + 1 & SS_{L_i} > 0\\
0 & SS_{L_i} = 0
\end{cases}
\]

where the global shift $G$ is adjusted during updates so that accumulation of the approximate weights during the linear search phase doesn't overflow i.e. $\sum_{i} \widetilde{SS}_{L_i} < 2^{64}$. However, we need to refine the process in level sampling to make sure to keep the correct probabilities for each level since each $\widetilde{SS}_{L_i}$ is only an approximation of the true weight, to do so we proceed as follows:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Draw a uniform 64-bit integer in \(u\in[1,\sum_{i} \widetilde{SS}_{L_i}]\) \cite{lemire2019}.
  \item Scan significands sums $\widetilde{SS}_{L_i}$ in descending order of $i$, accumulating until the running sum becomes $\ge u$.  Let the selected level be \(L_*\).
  \item Since \(\widetilde{SS}_{L_*}\) only approximates \(SS_{L_*}\), if \(u\) falls exactly on the boundary between levels \noindent{\footnote{ an event of probability at most \(2098/\sum_{i} \widetilde{SS}_{L_i}\) in our case since 2046 is the number of possible levels for normal 64-bit floating point numbers and we normalized subnormals in other 52 levels.}}, perform a rejection test using the exact \(SS_{L_*}\) to correct the bias.
\end{enumerate}

More precisely, the sampler uses the following algorithm:

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\small
\caption{Exact Sampling of Levels Grouped By Exponent}\label{alg:cap}
\vspace{5px}
\vspace{5px}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Procedure{Exact-Inter-Level-Sampling}{$ws$, $G$} \\
    \hspace*{\algorithmicindent} \textbf{Inputs:} Array of doubles $ws$ containing the weights for each level, a global shift $G$ used to construct approximate weights\\
    \hspace*{\algorithmicindent} \textbf{Output:} \text{An index $L_i$ of the array }  
    \State $N = \text{length of }ws$
    \State $SS = $ exact significands sums of levels (stored as 128-bit ints)
    \State $ES = \text{exponents of levels}$
    \State $\widetilde{SS} = SS_{L_i}  \ll  (G+ES_{L_i})   + 1\text{ for all } i \text{ in } 1...N$
    \State $x = \text{random integer in } [1,  \sum_{i}\widetilde{SS}_{L_i})]$
    \For{$i \text{ in } 1...N$}

    \If{$x <\widetilde{SS}_{L_i}$}
        \State {$\textbf{return } i$}
    \ElsIf {$x = \widetilde{SS}_{L_i}$}
        \State $s = G + ES_{L_i}$
        \While{true}{
            \State $s = s + 64$
            \State $x = \text{random integer in } [0, 2^{64})$ 
            \State $SS_{L_i,\:frac} = (SS_{L_i}  \ll  s) \mod{2^{64}}$ 
            \If{$x > SS_{L_i, \: frac} $}
                \State {$\text{repeat the sampling starting at line 8}$} 
            \ElsIf {$x < SS_{L_i, \:frac} \text{ or } s \ge 0$}
                \State {$\textbf{return } i$}
            \EndIf
            }
        \EndWhile
    \EndIf
    \State $x = x - \widetilde{SS}_{L_i}$
    \EndFor
\EndProcedure
\vspace{3px}
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

Consider, though, that, for performance reasons, all $SS_{L_i}$, $\widetilde{SS}_{L_i}$ and their sum can be tracked during the updating phase. Notice also that, while the algorithm could work in principle with different global shift values, some values will cause an amount of rejections greater than others. We will analyze how to set it in a satisfying way in section 3.3.

The key result which proves that algorithm 1 is an exact linear search algorithm for sampling, can be expressed by considering that at each repetition of the sampling procedure we have that there is a probability of $\frac{SS_{L_i}}{\sum_{i}\widetilde{SS}_{L_i}}$ to be selected for each level $L_i$ thanks to the refinement process, so the missing ingredient is to clarify that after considering all possible repetitions of the procedure the sampling probability will be instead the correct value $\frac{SS_{L_i}}{\sum_{i}SS_{L_i}}$. This is true because the event for the acceptance of one of the indices follows a geometric distribution with probability $P_{L_i} = \frac{\sum_{i}SS_{L_i}}{\sum_{i}\widetilde{SS}_{L_i}}$, and an index $i$ has probability to be selected at a certain round equal to $p_{L_i} = \frac{SS_{L_i}}{\sum_{i}\widetilde{SS}_{L_i}}$, therefore the probability to select $i$ at the acceptance round is $\frac{p_{L_i}}{P_{L_i}} = \frac{SS_{L_i}}{\sum_{i}SS_{L_i}}$, which means that the probability is the correct one. Furthermore, since the acceptance follows a geometric distribution we have that the expected number of repetitions before termination is equal to $\frac{1}{P_{L_i}}$.

Notice that for performance reasons, algorithm 1 also makes use of the equality $\frac{\widetilde{SS}_{L_i}-1}{\sum_{i}\widetilde{SS}_{L_i}} + \frac{1-(\widetilde{SS}_{L_i}-SS_{L_i})}{\sum_{i}\widetilde{SS}_{L_i}} = \frac{SS_{L_i}}{\sum_{i}\widetilde{SS}_{L_i}}$ since $i$ is accepted immediately when the edge of the approximate significands sum is not hit, and the further refinements happen only in this case. With the nested while rejection loop, we make sure to take into consideration the least significant bits which were neglected by approximating the exact significands sums with the 64-bit approximations. The refinement process works by progressively incorporating additional bits of precision that were not included in the initial 64‑bit approximation of the weights. When the random sample exactly matches the boundary value determined by the approximate weight, the algorithm recognizes that the decision is ambiguous because the fractional part of the weight was lost during the initial conversion. It then "zooms in" on this fractional part by drawing a new random 64‑bit value and increasing the bit shift.

\subsection{Exact Intra-Level Sampling}

Once a level is chosen, we can sample an element \(w_j\) from \(L_*\) by rejection sampling with the following algorithm:

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\small
\caption{Exact Intra-Sampling of Level}\label{alg:cap}
\vspace{5px}
\vspace{5px}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Procedure{Exact-Intra-Level-Sampling}{$ws$} \\
    \hspace*{\algorithmicindent} \textbf{Inputs:} Array of doubles $ws$ containing weights sharing the same exponent\\
    \hspace*{\algorithmicindent} \textbf{Output:} \text{An index $j$ of the array }
    \State $n = \text{length of }ws$
    \State $S = [(2^{63} + \text{significand}(w_j) \ll 11) \text{ for } w_j \text{ in } ws]$
    \While{true}{
        \State $j = \text{random integer in } 1...n$
        \State $x = \text{random integer in } [0, 2^{64})$
        \If{$x < S_j $}
            \State {$\textbf{return } j$}
        \EndIf
        }
    \EndWhile
\EndProcedure
\vspace{3px}
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

This is correct because the relative probability between the indices is preserved by the significands since the weights have the same exponent, and, additionally, the rejection strategy employed is efficient because $\min({S}) \geq \frac{1}{2} \max(S)$ which means that the average number of rejections for one draw in the worst case, where $S_j =\min({S}) = 2^{63}$ for all $S_j \in S$, is $2$. As before, in algorithm 2, each of the element in $S$ can be computed in the update phase. 

\subsection{Global Shift Adjustments}

The true weight of a level $L_i$ is the sum of the weights of its elements, which could be represented accurately as $SS_{L_i}\ll L_i$ using arbitrary precision arithmetic. However, as already discussed, for fast level sampling the algorithm can use an approximate weight for each level that fits into a 64-bit integer. To do so, we use a global shift $G$ to scale the true level weights down into a 64-bit range, so that we then use $\widetilde{SS}_{L_i}$ for most of the sampling. There are two main situation in which we need to adjust the approximate level weights:

\paragraph{Out-of-Range Approximate Levels Sum} To not overflow a 64-bit integer to store the levels approximate sum, we need to evaluate at each update if the sum is going to overflow a 64-bit integer with the current global shift $G$ and reduce it if this is going to happen.

\paragraph{Single Approximate Level Overflow} To adjust the sum when it overflows, we need to be able to add the new weight to the appropriate approximate level weight, however, this can in itself cause the overflow of the level weight, so a new smaller target global shift needs to be computed also in this case.

\paragraph{Bounding Rejection Probability} To keep the level sampling rejection probability $P_r$ low, we need to keep the sum of the approximate level weights minimum at a sufficiently high value, since, for a total number of levels equal to $N$, we have in the worst case that $P_r = \frac{N}{\sum_{i} \widetilde{SS}_{L_i}-N}$. We can deal with this by only increasing $G$ when the algorithm hits the "zooming in" phase during sampling and the approximate sum has fallen below the chosen threshold, which we set in the implementation at $2^{32}$.

Crucially, in all three cases we need to recalculate all existing approximate level weights using the new global shift and update the total approximate sum. Note though that this operation is infrequent in non-pathological cases and still $O(1)$ for finite precision arithmetic since the exponent range is bounded, e.g. in the case of 64-bit floating point number, there are at most 2098 levels.

\section{\hbox{Miscellaneous Optimizations}}

To improve the constant factors of our method, we applied several key optimizations to its implementation. These changes aim to make the algorithm more efficient in practice, even if the asymptotic complexity remains unchanged. 

First, instead of searching the level with the maximum exponent as in \cite{zhang2023}, we track it after each addition or deletion so that we can start the sampling from that level without incurring any searching cost. We use a bit-set data structure for tracking non-empty levels so that if the current maximum level becomes empty, finding the new one is efficient. Another important optimization concerns the global shift adjustment. When the global shift changes, rather than recomputing the entire set of approximate level weights, we limit the update to a smaller range. This is possible because many level weights are already 0 or 1 and will remain unchanged after the shift.

Memory-wise, we use a single vector to store all the different weight significands used in Algorithm 2 for sampling, this requires a delicate manual memory management implementation, but has the advantage that we can re-compact the levels which become empty due to deletion of elements to save memory in respect of using a vector for each level, which, in practice, would have required in the worst case $2098 \cdot 2n$ integers for $n$ stored weights and indices. This could be the case for instance if the $n$ indices had all weights to be stored in the first level, then due to updates, all the weights need to be moved to the second level, then to third, and so on. Many memory allocators do not reclaim memory automatically after removals, and, even if that is the case, allocating and deallocating frequently is costly. Our allocator implements a custom compaction mechanism to deal with this so that we don't need to allocate/deallocate when weights are updated. With our approach, we require $7n$ integers to store indices and weights not to worsen our amortized $O(1)$ time per update. A single 64-bit integer for each index is used to store its level location together with the corresponding exponent to save space. Thus, the memory consumption of our sampler grows as $O(8n)$. 

Finally, when sampling many indices at once we implemented a specialized method which constructs an exact multinomial distribution for level sampling based on a simplified version of the algorithm described in \cite{farach2015}, here we use arbitrary arithmetic, but the size of the maximum integer is still bounded by $(2098+128+1)$ bits, since the maximum sum of levels for double-precision numbers when they are represented in arbitrary arithmetic is bounded by $2^{2098+128+1}$.

\section{Experiments}

To strengthen our understanding of the numerical and performance characteristics of the EBUS sampler, we will perform both numerical and performance benchmarks between EBUS and previous methods\footnote{All the benchmarks were executed on a x86\_64 Linux OS with a AMD Ryzen 5-5600H CPU and 16 GB of RAM available.}. To find candidates to compare EBUS with, we looked for implementations provided by previous studies, trying to represent all of the dynamic algorithms which could be competitive. Accordingly, we selected three representative methods. We will then compare:

\begin{itemize}

    \item the Julia EBUS implementation we designed;

    \item the C++ BUS implementation of \cite{zhang2023}, which could be considered the inexact version of our method;

    \item the Rust implementation of \cite{matias2008} used in \cite{allendorf2023} in a non-linear preferiantial attachment algorithm. We will call it FT, standing for "Forest of Trees" since in \cite{matias2008} the authors referred to it with this name;

    \item the Dynamic Proposal Array* (DPP*) implementation in C++ of \cite{allendorf2024}. An algorithm which was shown to be the most performant when compared to other methods by its author.

\end{itemize}

\subsection{Numerical Comparison}

To compare the numerical efficacy of our sampler with respect to other methods, we designed a simple experiment: we take a fixed set of $n$ items and allow the initial weights to decay gradually over $t$ iterations. At each decay step we draw a large number of samples to see how closely the empirical frequencies match the exact distribution.

Concretely, for each index $i = 1,\dots,100$, define the initial weights $w_i^{(0)} \;=\;\Bigl(2 + \tfrac{1}{10000}\,i\Bigr)^{1000}$. This gives us a distribution of weights which are large enough to admit a large number of decay steps. For each $t = 1,2,\dots,100$, we simulate a “decaying” effect by dividing each weight by the same base factor once per iteration.  In closed form, one obtains $w_i^{(t)} \;=\; w_i^{(t-1)} \,\Big/\Bigl(2 + \tfrac{1}{10000}\,i\Bigr)$ so that $ w_i^{(t)} = \Bigl(2 + \tfrac{1}{10000}\,i\Bigr)^{1000 - t}$. As $t$ increases, the distribution gradually flattens, moving from being highly skewed at $t = 0$ toward a more uniform‐like shape by the time $t \approx 1000$.

In practice, we only take $t$ up to 100 (since the inexact sampler stopped working already at $t \approx 50$) so the distribution remains somewhat non‐uniform but still less peaked than the initial one. At each $t\in\{1,\dots,100\}$, we perform $10^6$ independent draws from the dynamic discrete distribution $\{\,w_i^{(t)}\}_{i=1}^{100}$ to construct the frequencies of the empirical distribution. 

To quantify how well the sampler frequencies approximate the theoretical distribution, we measured the Jensen–Shannon divergence \cite{lin2002} between the frequency vector and the true probability vector at each iteration. This measure of divergence symmetrizes and smooths the Kullback–Leibler divergence \cite{kullback1951} by comparing each distribution to their average. The figure below shows that over time, the divergence value tends to increase for the inexact methods, while it is near 0 throughout the sampling for EBUS:

\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{numerical.pdf}

Moreover, all inexact samplers failed to proceed beyond approximately 50 iterations, entering a state that effectively resembled an infinite loop: even after a substantial amount of time, the samplers were unable to return the empirical frequency distribution for the next time step.

As an example, if we take the 50th decay step, we can see that the probability distributions for the inexact methods greatly differ from the true one, which is instead perfectly represented, apart from random sampling noise, by EBUS, as can be seen by the overlap of the theoretical distribution, depicted in the figure by the red line, and the empirical histogram:

\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{numerical50.pdf}

\subsection{Performance Comparison}

We will evaluate the sampler performance across three scenarios\footnote{The code to reproduce these comparisons is available at \url{https://github.com/Tortar/DynamicDiscreteSamplersComparisons}.}, each representing a progressively higher level of dynamicity:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textsl{Static Sampling}: elements are only added at initialization time, and then the distribution is fixed throughout the sampling process. In this case, as a metric we use the average sampling time for a single draw;
    \item \textsl{Dynamic Sampling with Fixed Range}: a weight of a random index is updated after each random draw from the distribution. The metric is the average time of sampling and updating the distribution at each extraction;
    \item \textsl{Dynamic Sampling with Variable Range}: new weights are added until a certain maximum capacity throughout the sampling, in the experiment shown below, we choose to resize the sampler to 10 times its initial capacity. The metric is the same as the previous case.
\end{itemize}

In all experiments, we will use random numbers extracted from a standard gaussian distribution in absolute value as weights.

The following figures show the results of our experiments:

\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{static.pdf}

\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{dynamic_fixed.pdf}

\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{dynamic_variable.pdf}

It can be observed that our implementation of EBUS in Julia is much more performant than the original BUS implementation in C++ and that it outperforms or is competitive with the best inexact implementation of the methods we benchmarked it with in all three scenarios. The FT method did not support the dynamic sampling with variable range setting and so it is not shown in the last figure. It is particularly noteworthy that EBUS can match DPA* since this data structure was devised in \cite{allendorf2024} to specifically improve the performance of previous methods and in the paper in which it was presented it was claimed that it could beat those by half an order of magnitude.

\section{Conclusions}

In this paper, we presented a new algorithm, which we called EBUS (Exact BUcket Sampling), which is the first algorithm capable of maintaining exactness and performance in sampling from a weighted dynamic distribution in any finite precision setting. This required carefully designed rejection sampling procedures which we believe could be also adapted to other algorithms in the sampling realm. The numerical experiments revealed that inexact samplers can suffer from big numerical errors, which could completely bias the sampling process, or even halt it because the propagation of those errors can break some internal state of those samplers. Furthermore, we have shown how our state of the art implementation can compete with the most efficient inexact implementations of previous methods we could find. This novel method could be useful in cases where biasing the sampling could give spurious results, and in cases where sampling efficiency is important as in \cite{allendorf2023}. Finally, this could be used as a benchmark for exact methods for specific distribution subclasses given its generality.

