\section{Conclusion and discussion}
\label{section:discussion-and-conclusion}
This paper describes a generic generation-to-memory transition from generative diffusion to associative memory as noise levels diminish. In particular, universal approximation properties, robustness or stability to perturbations of parameters or inputs, and learning dynamics of associative memory are generically described at a global level using the geometric approach to dynamical systems theory. In parallel, these properties are applied to diffusions in the zero-noise limit by leveraging probabilistic approaches to dynamical systems.

Reliable and stable associative memory satisfies the conditions for gradient fields to be Morse-Smale. Conversely, current models can be universally approximated by this class of systems. Memory retrieval is therefore generically downhill, and associated to each "memory landscape" is an invariant DAG defining connections between rest points (\colorAutoref{fig:figure-generic-properties}). The Morse lemma implies that these models generically satisfy the asymptotic stability property for reliable memory storage. In addition, they are structurally stable -- their fixed-point and orbit structure is unchanged in a neighborhood of a model in use. This takes the local form of stability global, and it qualitatively describes the robustness of these systems to model parameter perturbations. Pragmatically, Morse-Smale gradient models of associative memory can be written as an inverse Riemannian metric times the gradient of a potential, which broadens their applicability to more geometrically structured domains. 

Generative diffusion appears as small random perturbations of associative memory, defined by stochastic differential equations with gradient drift (\colorAutoref{fig:figure-intuition-example-zero-noise}). Their invariant measures are Boltzmann-Gibbs distributions that generically correspond to the stable manifolds of the associative memory model defined by the drift field. The Morse condition implies that these invariant measures are stable to stochastic and deterministic perturbations on stable manifolds. The Morse-Smale assumptions enable a global extension of these results; though, the image of a Boltzmann-Gibbs distribution under a homeomorphism between topologically equivalent systems need not preserve its absolute continuity.

Memory formation is characterized by breakdowns of the Morse-Smale assumptions. The learning processes of associative memory appear as one-parameter families of gradients that trace an arc in the space of gradient fields, and memory formation is generically described by the sequential occurrence of only two bifurcations (\colorAutoref{fig:figure-intuition-saddle-node-bistable} and \colorAutoref{fig:figure-intuition-heteroclinic-flip-bifurcation}). The learning processes of time-dependent associative memory appear as two-parameter families that are characterized by eleven possible bifurcations (\cref{subsubsection:two-parameter-families-gradients}). These results also apply directly to diffusion models using probability flow ODEs for sample generation, viewed through this deterministic lens, while large deviation type results from classic FW theory imply that stochastic flows of generative diffusion converge in probability to the flows of their gradient drift fields at vanishing noise levels. We introduced a definition for stochastic flows in the zero-noise limit analogous to the topological equivalence of flows (\cref{subsection:paths-large-deviations}). Data generation for models with time-varying drift can be understood through this construction by the bifurcations for one-parameter families. Their learning dynamics can be viewed through the bifurcations for two-parameter families.

Our results apply to classic models, including the Hopfield network and Boltzmann machine, along with energy-based models and modern diffusion models (\cref{section:applications-examples}). In particular, we gave explicit computations that determine when classic and modern Hopfield networks are not structurally stable (\cref{subsection:applications-hopfield-boltzmann-models} and \cref{subsection:applications-modern-hopfield-networks}). Even some simple scenarios violate the Morse-Smale conditions for these models, and the results of the modern Hopfield network are directly relevant to the attention mechanism. Although associative memory is generically structurally stable, it is not clear if the Morse-Smale constraints are computationally tractable to satisfy, in general. It is also not clear if the connections made between diffusion models and associative memory models can be used to enhance their performance. 


