\section{Introduction}
\IEEEPARstart{S}{ynthetic} Aperture Radar (SAR), as an active microwave imaging system, has emerged as a pivotal technological tool in Earth remote sensing due to its all-weather and day-night imaging capabilities, demonstrating unique application value in military reconnaissance and environmental monitoring\cite{APreliminaryStudyonSARAdvancedInformationRetrievalandSceneReconstruction,1}. Unlike optical camera imaging principles,  the active sensing mechanism of SAR acquires target information through electromagnetic wave scattering interactions with terrain features \cite{1717717,4909462}. This results in pronounced geometric sensitivity in SAR imagery, where identical targets exhibit substantial appearance variations under different viewing angles, frequency bands, and polarization configurations \cite{2014Microwave}. While this distinctive characteristic provides unique advantages in multidimensional information acquisition, it poses formidable challenges for SAR image interpretation and three-dimensional (3D) geometric reconstruction tasks. Traditional 3D reconstruction techniques critically depend on profound understanding of complex scattering mechanisms and imaging geometry.
% In this context, differentiable scene modeling approaches offer promising solutions to decode intricate scattering information embedded in SAR imagery, enabling accurate recovery of both geometric structures and scattering properties of observed scenes.

