\section{Exploring the Capability of LLMs in Aiding Kernel Fuzzing: a Case Study}
\label{sec:solution}

% High-quality seeds can help fuzzers reduce warm-up time and increase the likelihood of discovering vulnerabilities. 
%To address the critical issue of fuzzing efficiency, 
In this section, we conduct a case study on validating potential solutions that are critical to kernel fuzzing. We aim to explore the feasibility of LLMs in aiding kernel fuzzing and choose seed generation as the starting point as highlighted in the implication \yellowcircle{6}.
We propose \sysname, the first LLM-powered seed generation framework for kernel fuzzing. Our framework is publicly released to facilitate further research.

\subsection{Design and Implementation}
% There are numerous valuable syscall sequences from the real world available in various forms \cite{}.
% However, utilizing them is challenging because the de facto kernel fuzzer, Syzkaller, uses a Domain-Specific Language (DSL) to describe its test cases. 
The de facto kernel fuzzer, Syzkaller, uses a Domain-Specific Language \textit{syzlang} to describe its test cases, making it challenging to utilize traces from the real world.
We aim to automate the integration of these traces with fuzzing using LLMs. We decompose the process into the following tasks: (1) retrieve the relevant syscalls and their arguments, (2) convert them into valid syz programs using in-context learning, and (3) enhance them via automatic repair.

% \subsubsection{Essence Retrieval}
% LLM has rich knowledge about kernels and programming, which is the basis of the task. 
\noindent \textbf{Essence retrieval.} Corpora found in the wild are not designed for fuzzing and often contain irrelevant information, such as wrapper functions. Therefore, the first step in \sysname's interaction with LLMs is to filter out the crucial components: syscalls and relevant variables. Rather than copying the contents of multiple files, we use an on-demand prompt policy that provides additional details only when necessary, i.e., LLMs can not recognize functions defined in other files. This approach helps reduce costs and improve efficiency.

% \subsubsection{Program Generation}
\noindent \textbf{Program generation.} In this task, our goal is to generate valid programs conformed to \textit{syzlang} based on the retrieved elements. To achieve this, we propose an iterative and in-context learning prompting method. Specifically, we first task the LLMs with producing syz programs using initial instructions and then teach them the syntax of \textit{syzlang}. We also provide concrete examples, 
% that demonstrate how to map raw input to valid programs, 
aiming to enhance the LLMs' understanding of the task and familiarize them with the expected output format. After constructing the above prompts, the syscall sequences and arguments in raw forms are then fed into LLMs which output a series of desired programs.
Given that LLMs are prone to hallucinations, factual information, such as kernel macros, is not translated but faithfully retained. 
% Similarly, we use the \textit{\&AUTO} field to handle undecided parameter cases.

% \subsubsection{Program Repair}
\noindent \textbf{Program repair.} The final step 
% of our framework 
is to validate the programs generated by LLMs and enhance them through automatic repair. It is well-known that LLMs can sometimes exhibit unpredictable 
% or inconsistent 
behaviors, particularly in complex scenarios involving nested structures. Hence, we implement a self-validation strategy using a set of rules expressed as if statements. For example, "\textit{You should check \{rules\} and \{act\} if violated}". While this strategy does not guarantee soundness, it assists LLMs in reviewing their output, leading to improved results.

\noindent \textbf{Implementation.} We use OpenAI's LLM GPT-4 with version \texttt{gpt-4-turbo} and access it through HTTP requests using Python. For seed generation, we choose strace \cite{strace} as the dataset. Regarding kernel fuzzing, we integrate \sysname\ with Syzkaller (commit \texttt{373b66c}). 

\subsection{Evaluation}
\noindent \textbf{Evaluation setup.} Currenly, we focus on the Linux kernel and evaluate Syzkaller and \sysname\ on the latest version 6.11-rc4. Both fuzzer share the same environment and resources. It is worth noting that the support for additional kernels can be conveniently extended. 

\noindent \textbf{Seed generation.} 
% As \sysname\  aims to provide high-quality programs for kernel fuzzing, 
We first study the effectiveness of our designs for seed generation. We conducte a comparative analysis using a direct prompt with \textit{syzlang} syntax. \sysname\ generates 551 syz programs, with 298 of these being successfully recognized by Syzkaller. This results in a 54\% validity rate for \sysname, surpassing the 18\% validity rate achieved by the direct prompt method, thereby highlighting the superior performance of our designs in satisfying \textbf{F2.2}. 
We also find that the average cost of generating a single seed is \$0.15. The price is acceptable and may be further reduced with the development of LLMs.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.7\linewidth]{figs/coverage.pdf}
